      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A MAN never realises how much personal pros perty he owns until he moves. "My boy," said a musician to his son, "don't be too sharp nor yet too flat. Just be natural." PETERS; I had a close shave this morning." Soskins : "Where ?" Peters "At the barber's shop." A QUESTION IN PHYSICS.—" When does a man weigh the heaviest?" "When he treads on you corns." SHE: "Am I the first girl you ever proposed to, darling?" He (sincerely) : "No; but you are the only girl who ever accepted me." Miss ANN Tim:lE : "I get a photograph taken of myself every year." Miss Caustioue "What a big collection you must have, dear ! A YOUTH begged a West-end belle to give him something he could wear next his heart. She sent him a red flannel chest protector. AN Irish counsel was asked by the judge "for whom he was concerned ? " He answered, "I am I concerned, my lord, for the plaintiff but am em- ployed by the defendant." "Ou, Henry!" exclaimed Mrs. Younghusband, breathlessly, " I have something terrible to tell you. The baby has swallowed your shaving braili." "Oh, that's all right," responded the brute:" it was worn out, and I Was going to get a new one anyhow." Wour..i) im-Puncuaszn "How much for this picture?' Artist: The price is £1,000." " Why, man alive I you expect to be paid for your work as if you had been dead four or five hundred years !" Mit. Spiker (in search of a boarding-house): "There is no limit to the diet, I presume, madam?" Boarding-house-keeper (proudly): "No limit, sir. During last year five of my boarders died from over-eating." THERE is a great difference between military engagements and love engagements. In one there is a great deal of falling-in, and in the other there is a good deal of falling-out. " SUMCRIBERS coning in at the rate of 20 a day," wrote the editor of a country weekly and the , rival journal explained that they were coming in to order :heir paper to he discontinued. Boox AGENT: "You had better buy a copy. It contains valuable information, sir, and will be sure to pay for itself in a short time." Merchant: " Will it? Then I'll take a copy. I was afraid I'd have to pay for it." AT THE ELECTRICAL EXAM.—Examiner: "What is the best Laulator we know of?" Candidate: "Poverty." Docrou (who had ordered his patient to take , some sleeping powders): " Well, do you sleep any better now?' Patient: "Good heavens! Doctor, .1 can sleep well enough, but just as I am about to close my eyes the nurse wakes me up and gives me a sleeping powder.' A SENTIMENTAL novelist once wrote: "Edwin then kissed Angelina under the silent stars." The compositor set it up thus: "Edwin then kicked Angelina under the cellar stairs." You may scramble up mountains all over the • world, Of the biggest and highest description ; But you'll find the most difficult sort of ascent When you try to get up a subscription. THE ELOPEMENT. —Bride: "Here's a telegram from papa." Bridegroom (eagerly) : "What does he say ?' Bride (reading): "'Do not come back, and all will be forgiven.'" No REFORM LIKELY Now.—Little Ethel: "Mrs. Nexdoor told mamma that she ought to put her jellies and preserves in a dark closet." Little Johnny: "That's all right. There's only one dark closet in this house, and that's the one mamma locks me in when I'm naughty." EVENLY MATCHED.—Magistrate (to witness): "And where were you when this assault occurred?" Witness: "Just across toe street, your honour." Magistrate: "Then why did you not go to the plaintiff's assistance when you saw him attacked?" Witness: "Faith, I wasn't sure then he wouldn't be the defendant, your honour." JOHNNY had been carefully brought up; any- body could see that. One (lay he sat upon his father's knee in a crowded tram ar. A lady entered. "Madam," he said, as he rose to his feet, "take my seat !" AN AUTHOR S Erma CRY.—" Here I've been hard at work sixteen hours out of the twenty-four during the last fortnight on my new treatise, in which I am trying to demonstrate the necessity of an eight hours labour day. FOR seven years a nervous old gentleman at 'Woolwich made a circuit of half a mile twice per day rat'ner than pass a powder magazine. The other day he learned that it had been empty for more than eight years ! TWELVE persons will go out yachting, and each one will afterwards tell you privately th-it "every- one was sick but me." We begin to think that yachting has a very demoralising effect. WestaN is composed of 234 hones, 169 muscles, and 303 pins Fearfully and wonderfully made, and to be handled with great care to avoid scratches. Miss Furzis : "I want to break my engagement with Mr. Spooney, but I don't know how to do it without driving the poor fellow to suicide." Little Brother: "Why don't you let him see you in curl- papers ? " A PATHETIC A PPEAL. —" Brethren," said a preacher when the cm/flee ion was being made, "perhaps one of you will be good enough to put a needle and some thread in the bag, so that 1 may be enabled to make some use of the buttons with which you are all so liberal." PRETTY GIRL: "Do you think it would be im- modest for a woman to propose during leap year!" Old Bachelor (fervently): "No, indeed: no, indeed. I think it would be eminently sensible." Pretty Girl : "That's just what I told Mrs. Sour- face, who admires you so ninch ; but she said you'd be shocked. I'll run and tell her." IN a family where there are two boys, one Df them brought home a worthless dog, to the disgust of the rest of the members. At, last the elder - sister offered him half a-crown if he would give the dog away. He assented and pocketed the coin. When night catne on, prompted by curiosity, she asked the little fellow to whom lie had given his dog, and was told with charming. naiveM, "Oh, I've given him to Jack,"—his brother. THE SINGLE MAN: "N, sir. You might hunt the wide world over, and you wouldn't find a more sensible, reasonable little girl than the one I am going to marry. The Married Man: "I guess you haven't known her very long, have you?" The Single Man: "Known her! Why, man, I have been with her constantly for three weeks! DOCTOR : "Yes; the symptoms are very seri)us. You must get to bed at once, and I will call and see you twice a day." Pitient : "In that case, it's lucky I joined the club last week." Doctor : " A club case! Good heaven,- 1 why didn't you say so Take a pennyworth of Epsom salts; you'll be all right to-morrow."</p>
        <p>-- -- , Cocoa is more than a mere stimulating and refreshing drink, it is also a nut, itionS food, and one of the most precious gifts of nature—sustaining and invigor,,ting the system probably more than any other beverage. The " Lancet" refers to Cadbury's Cocoa as f. the standard of highest purity at present attainable in regard to cocoa."— .Idrt.</p>
      </div>
