      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>" CA.eITAL"</head>
        <p>" CA.eITAL"</p>
        <p>The real name of Rustem Pasha was Rial Atitoine Chinelli de Marini. He was an Italian, and never a Mussulman. The Roman Catholics, therefore, claimed him on Monday, and most splendidly did they conduct the service for their dead. The -body lay OR a catafalque with hundreds of wreaths around and thousands of candles, while the 'crim- son tolds of the Turkish flag could be seen under the floral tributes. The musical portion of the ser- vice was beautifully rendered, but the great feature waS the array of uniforms of the different Ambas- sadors and Ministers to the Court of St. James's. These were resplendent with gold and silver lace and orders of all countries. The American Ambas- sador was, as usual, in plain evening dress, looking like a rook among peacocks. The funeral was in every way a most brilliant affair. At the last moment it was arranged that there should be a guard of honour at the chapel, and the unfortunate soldiers thus engaged suffered most, for the cold was bitingly tierce, and not a man of them hid an overcoat. While the service was going on the soldiers were allowed to pile their arms and walk about, but it was a long and weary hour for them. Much comment was made on the absence of Lord Salisbury, for, whatever our relations with Turkey, Rustern Pasha was one of Lord Salisbury's greatest friends. It appears that his lordship could not get up to town in time, and Mr. George Curzon did duty for him. Lord Gecrrge amilton represeeted;tibe Cabinet. He arrived a-quarter of an hour late,' Si- -thottgb he lives quite close to the chapel. - - ;</p>
        <p>The Government are not disposed to let tbe secret of their policy break out, as they are not preparing their leading' Bills for next session ht the offices at Whitehall. There is tile greatest anxiety among. the ..Church and Education parties to find out their prcposals as soon as possible, hut these are far from being yet finally settled. The school question. involves many complex considera- tions, which only the permanent officials can work onhin tbf first instance, and it will be many weeks before the complete provisions of any Bill can come before the Cabinet. The various Bills will not, in fact, be officially considered till after the Christmas holidays.</p>
        <p>The Tory clubs are very much disconcerted at the idea of Sir John Pewier and Sir Donald Currie being raised to the peerage. Not that they grudge them the honour from a personal point of vie* of their business deserts. But they are Unionists, and their seats are not too safe, and Lord Salisbury will need to balance their elevation by a liberal selec- tinn of Tory aspirants to a similar honour. It may be that the constituencies which the two UnioniAts represent may not repent of their adhesion to the Unionist cause ; but if they do,and return Liberals, the jealous Tories fear thatit will be regarded as an opportunity for the Liberals to raise the cry that the Ministerial majority has already begun to moulder away.</p>
        <p>The "Engineer" announces the terms of its Thousand Guinea llcad Carriage Competition, designed to further the adoption and development of the automatic.,vehicle. The limit of weight has been fixed at Witt tOUP, in accordance with Mr. Shaw Letevre's Bill in the last Parliament, and the speed at ten miles an hour; while in the case of oil being used as the motive power, the flAshing point is fixed at 73deg. Fehr., which is the AMP as thailaid down in the Act for the domestic lamp. Ihe competition, it is thought, cannot take place before October 1896, when the various vehicles—two cluses of two tops each, and two of one ton each—will be required to run 200 miles, end Sir F. Bramwell, Mr. J. A. F. Aspinall, of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway, and Dr. John Hopkinson will act as judges.</p>
        <p>The Becbuana chiefs /Sit Waterloo on Saturday forenoon to embark on the Arundel Castle for South Africa. They Were seen sway by a number of the Mends they have made ft exe,and: theieivas cordial round of hand-shaking. , Jihama, Sebele, and Bathoen are bad sailorsoindjudging by the weather, the first pare of their voyage has probably proved somewhat trying to them. They intend to spend Christmas together at Bathoen's home, which is reached first from Cape Town.</p>
        <p>The issue of the new volunteer weapon has at last been begun in London and the home district. and during the next four months about 40,000 will be in the hands of the London and some provincial corps. It will be remembered that Sir 11. Camp- bell-Bannerman stated in the house that he was unable to arm the volunteers with the new rifle, in conFequence of the impossibility of finding suitable rifle ranges adapted to its great carrying powers. Since he made that statement ranges have been ob- tained in the neighb4urhood of the metropolis for nearly a third of the corps, and it is believed that during the volunteer year which has now begun the whole metropolitan force will have secured ranges and have received their weapons. As some of the corps may not have been provided by the time the next year's Bisley meeting comes round, and as many of them, even if they have, will hove had very little time to practise with it, the Rifle Association have wisely resolved that fer another year the prizes in the national contest will be com- peted for with the old Martini-Henry, and not , with the new Martini-Enfield.</p>
        <p>A correspondent gives the following story of the late Peter Mackenzie :—Perhaps the most charac- teristic anecdote about him was that which des- cribed his behaviour in Madame Tussaud's, where ho sat down on Voltaire's chair and, having ad- dressed the bystanders on the errors of that author, sang the hymn beginning, 'Jesus shall reign where'er the sun.'"</p>
        <p>A new form of competition threatens the .op- pressed British agriculturist. A sale of Anglo- Australian horses will be held in London early in December. These horses, which have been sent from New South Wales, include Clydesdales for draught purposes, and ladies' hacks and cobs for riding or driving. If the animals fetch profitable prices, no doubt we shall have further importa- tions. For a long time it has been a reproach to the agricultural interests of this country that we had to get many of our army remounts from abroad, and now horses are coming to this market all the way from Australia, while the English far- mer idly bemoans his ill fortune. The Australian horses have been bred mostly from sires imported from England and Scotland.</p>
        <p>In Mr. Holyoake's book on p nblic speaking he gives the following as phrases used at one time by the London daily papers when alluding to each other :—The "Times" calls its neighb our, the "Morning Chronicle," "That squirt of filthy water," and the "Chronicle "calls the " Post," " that slop- pail of corruption." The " Standard" describes the "Globe" as "our blubber-headed contemporary," the" Morning Post " assails the" Courier" as "that spavined old hack," whilst the " Morning Adver- tiser " hurls its wrath against the "Times "as "The bully of Berkshire, and braggadocio of Printing House-square." The "Times," not to he outdone, commenced one of its leaders on June 13, 1835, with "The Liberal Liars."</p>
        <p>It is understood that one of Mr. John Morley's chief reasons for desiring to remain out of Parlia- ment was his intention to devote more time to literary pursuits. Mr. Morley is not a man of means, and his pen is his bread-winner. He has a steady source of income as reader tor the publish- ing house of Macmillan's. This post is worth £l,OOO a year to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>" TIIE WORLD'S GREAT MATRIMONIAL AGENCY."</head>
        <p>" TIIE WORLD'S GREAT MATRIMONIAL AGENCY."</p>
        <p>Further evidence was taken at the Bow-street Police Court on Monday in the case in which the conductors of " The World's Great Matr;monial Agency, Limited," are charned with conspiring to detraud persons who had en- listed their services in their search for wives One wit- ness stated that after much negotiation a " very special offer " was made to him—that for the lump sum of £lO be would be made a" free associate," with "a practic- ally unlimited choice of ladies." There was an amusing cross-examination of the witness, whose description of himself during the negotiation as "twenty last June, looks older, tall, dark, slim, military appearance," seemed to he enj)yed in court. The case was again ad- journed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</head>
        <p>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</p>
        <p>I will send a valuable. instructive, and highly Interesting book which every woman would be glad to have Contains important and confidential advice and tells both married and single how to keep healthy and avoid the anxieties so distressing to the sex. Sent free, securely sealed, for one stamp to cover postage. ADDRESS: Professor B. LaSars.s, Carb rton House Carburton Street, London, W.</p>
