      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1895. • LADIES' LETTER. THE HIDDEN HAW., OR, CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. BY MRS. E. D. E. N. BOUTHWORTIY</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1895. • LADIES' LETTER. THE HIDDEN HAW., OR, CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. BY MRS. E. D. E. N. BOUTHWORTIY</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST Nar.ran, The following hints are, perhaps, a little super- fluous, but if they are I know you will forgive me for stating them. It is remarkable how few Women, comparatively, know how to dress, so here goes for a few ideas for them. If you are not among the number, so much the better for you; you can read and pads on. A woman, then, must - learn how to dress—toning down here, making up there, as need be, in order to obtain as symmetrical a whole as possible. For the small woman much may be done to apparently increase her height. Broad boot heels, as high as can be worn with comfort, will make a little difference. Striped materials, in preference to plaid or plain for dresses, are another help. The princess cut of gown increases • the apparent height Much more than the round body or basque, and the dress collar ought to be worst rather high. The hat or ' bonnet should be as small and high as can be be- comingly worn. 14trge hats should,.by all means, be avoided. A large woman never looks well in a dress with a huge pattern upon it: she should rather "chyme „narrow stripes or plain . materials. 1 Any material which shines, such as black satin,' makes the figure look , larger. Her close-fitting! dresses or jackets should be without trimmings' and frills, erosswise4eimmings, or any *mazy styles.l A womanwhese body is tali in 'privation to her( legs should wear short, smart jackets, and shel shoi,ild .avoid shortening ber skirt length:" by deep, frills and other lines of trimming; she ought also to arrange her .bodices 'and sleeves so that they detract from the length of the body. Many of the new-mcsiee of-trimming are a sort of piling of Pelion'on Ossa. Each would be good in itself; brit each must be enhanced by another. A fawn velvet cloak is an example of this, for the</p>
        <p>graduated trimmings that go downwards from the neck are of string-coloured silk cord, overlaid with cut jet beads. The fur trimmings are of natural racoon, and the whole cloak is lined with a-shot- blue and apricot brocade. As regards evening attire, which you will want more and more as the winter advances I can tell you 41 a very jolly little evening dress. It is a remarkably simple dress and made 'of prim- rose colour silk spot er4pon, with a narrow pearl trimming. The stylish six-gored skirt- is lined throughout with linenette, and the lower edge is interlined six inches up with hair cloth; it fits smoothly round the figure in front, having one dart in each side gore, and, the three gores are gathered. Both front seams are decorated at the waist with ,a pointed tab of double material, edged with two rows of pearl trimming. The tight-fitting bodice is cut with round neck at the back and V-shape in' front, the basque being slightly pointed in the centre of front and back where the bodice fastens with a silk lace. The neck is outlined with seven prettily shaped tabs of doubled material, edged with the narrow trim. fling, then secured to the edge of neck, which, when neatened, is finished with a row of the trimming to correspond with the edge of basque. The fall puff sleeves are lined with muslhi and gathered at the lower edge, then arranged over a shaped lining, and the upper edge gathered over the perforations to form four tiny puffs, then eenred with the foundation into the armhole, the .ower edge of each being trimmed with four tabs to correspond with the neck. Here is a neat little coat for a quite tiny child. It reaches to the knees, to which gaiters should reach, in the fashionable style of the fin-de-siecle juvenile lord (or lady) of creation. But to return nos m0.,x43 (and this - very appropriate</p>
        <p>phrase, as you will see in a moment), the coat is made of white peau de mouton, with the yoke and square collar and full sleeves trimmed with a fine silk passementcrie. The bonnet which completes this has a full silk crown decked with bows and small 1., bite ostrich feathers. Skirts are being more and more trimmed. Not only do we see embroideries, inscrustations, lace, and fur upon them, but we also see wide flounces on them. One wide flounce, reaching to mid-skirt, is most in favour. It is headed with a ruching. It is becoming to tall figures; as also are the small flowers and ruches, which are sometimes seen round the hips. But imagine a stout person thus arrayed Plain skirts with trimmings round the bottom suit most figures, and are still worn. Hats are as lovely, for the most part, as ever, and I notice that wings are becoming more and more fashionable. A quite up-to-date chapeau has</p>
        <p>black wings on either side, and a large bunch of gardenias and foliage adorn the back. This is an idealised version of the toque, which should not fail to prove becoming to the most fashiouable coiffure.</p>
        <p>t fail</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>JEN NIE.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. W. Alcock, secretary of the Surrey Club, re- ceived by the Australian mail arriving on Monday, in • structions to make the programme for the Australian cricket team that is to visit England next summer.</p>
        <p>....- .-....-_-._.</p>
        <p>To MnTyr-Eng.—Afro. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildrvn while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasantto taste ; it produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Of al hemists, Is. 1 d. per bottle.—didet.</p>
        <p>Feathers, as an article of dress, were at first worn only by men in their helmets. It is said that the Prince of Wales receives two hundred begging letters, on an average, every day. Sweden has a deaf and dumb corps of the Salvation Army. Four meetings are held weekly. Someone has said that a diet of oatmeal and brown bread tends to promote the growth of hair. However this may be, the diet is a good one for many more as- sured reasons.</p>
        <p>The disagreeable smell produced by cooking cabbage may be got rid of if a piece of breadcrumb. tied in a fine white Tag, is placed in the boiler with the Witter. At the end of a quarter of an hour it should be taken out and thrown away. . .</p>
        <p>In passing fro rn darkness into light the eye is pained because the pupil is widely expanded and so much light enters as to.cause.pain to the optic nerve. .</p>
        <p>The ears have become immovable on account of the extreme mobility of the neck muscles. For the same reason the eyes of the owl are fixed in his head._</p>
        <p>Fully twenty thousand of the populaticn of India are annually killed by snake-bites. With a view to reducing the mortality the Government offer a reward fer snakes' heads,which,instead of diminishing the number of these reptiles, tends to increase it,as the natives breed snakes I solely to secure the reward.</p>
        <p>Sunshine and Microbes. Sunshine is the very best cure in the world for those microbeS that scientists have frightened most people so bay about. For, according to some experiments made by two distinguished Germans not long since, microbes multiply in the darkness at such a rate that it seems miraculous. In a drop of water the baccilli,which num- bered Bor 10 in the early evening, had increased to some 30 in a drop before morning. These German seientists spent an entire night by the Iser, dipping out water at intervals to make these observations. Every hour they dipped out fresh water. In the morning the bacilli began to disappear again.</p>
        <p>Are Potatoes Indigestible It is said by some medical scientists that seven-tenths of the djopepsia among people would disappear within a few years if the potato were eliminated from the diet of the human family. About three-fourths of the potato in weight is water, the other fourth is made up of five parts Of starch and one part of gluten. So the potato furnishes us a diet of starch water, which is, to a great extent, the cause of dyspepsia. Persons with weak 'digestion who have tried the experiment of going with- out potatoes entirely are quite unanimous in the opinion that improvement began almost immediately alter quit- ting this article of food.</p>
        <p>. ." .•</p>
        <p>CTIAPTER 3C.</p>
        <p>THE ROOM OF THE TRAP DOOR. " If you have hitherto coacesled this sight,</p>
        <p>Lei it be tenable, in your silence still; And whatsoever else doth hap to-night, " 'dive it an understanding, but no tongue."</p>
        <p>-SIiAIErCSPEAEL</p>
        <p>Capitola meanwhile, in the care of the major, arrived at Hurricane Hall, much to the discomfi- ture of good Mrs. Condiment, who was quite unpre- pared to expect the new inmate; and when Major Warfield said: .</p>
        <p>• "Mrs. Condiment, this is your young lady, take her up to the best bedroom, where she can take off her bonnet and shawl," the worthy dame, thinking secretly: "The old fool has gone and married a young wife, sure euough ; a mere cbit of a child" —made a very deep curtsey, and a very queer cough, and said: •</p>
        <p>"I'm mortified, Madam, at the fire notbeing made in the best bedroom; but then. I was not: warned of your coming, Madam !" _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Madam! Is the old woman crazed? This child Is no rhadam 1' She is Miss 'Black, m Vard, the daughter of a deceased friend 1" sharply exclaimed Old HurriCane. "Excuse me, Miss, I did not know; I was un- prepared to. receive a young lady.- Shall I attend you, Miss .Black ?" said the old lady in a mollified tone. "If you please," said Capitola, and arose to follow her:</p>
        <p>"Not expecting you, Miss, I have no proper room preptired--rnost of them are not.furnished; and in Borne the chimneys are foul ; indeed, the only tolerable room ,I can put you in is the room with the trap-door--if you would not object to it ?" said Mr x; Condiment, as with a candle in her hand, she preceded Capitola along the gloomy hall, and then opened a door that led into a narrow passage.</p>
        <p>"A room with a trap-door ?—that's a curious thing; but why should I object to it! I don't at all. I think I should rather like it," said Capitola. "I will show it to you and tell you about it, and then if you like it, well and good! If not, I shall have to put you in a room that leaks, and has swallows' nests in the chimney," answered Mrs. Condiment, as she led the way along the narrow passages, and up and down dark, black stairs, and through bare and deserted rooms, and along other passages until she reached w remote chamber, opened the door, and invited her guest.to enter. ' It was a large shadowy room, through which the single candle shed such a faint, uncertain light, that at first Capitola could tee nothing but black masses looming up through the darkness.</p>
        <p>• But When Mrs. Condiment advanced and sat the candle upon the chimney-piece, and Capitola's sight accommodated itself to the scene, she saw that upon the right of the chimney-piece stood a tall tester 'bedstead, curtained with very dark crimson serge; on the left hand, .thick curtains of the same colour draped ,the windows. Between; these- windows, directly opposite the bed, stood al dark mahogany :dressing -bureau, with a large! looking-glass.; a washstand in the Ifft-hitytecorner of the cliitithey-piece; and a rocking-chap, d two plain chairs completed the furniture of this room, that r particular in describing,. as upon the simple accident of its arrangement', depended, upon two occasions, the life and liefiour of its occupant. There was no carpet °lithe floor, with the excep- ti6nOf a large old Turkey rug which was laid before the fire-place. " Here, , my dear,Alfis room is perfectly: dry and comfortable, and we always keep kindling; built' up in the fireplace ready to light in case a guest, should ,come,' said Mrs. Condiment, applying a match to the wastepaper under the pineknots and logs that filled the chimney. Soon there arose a! cheerful' blaze that lighted up all the room glowing on the crimson sergetied-eurtains and window-curi tam, and flashing upon the large looking-glass between them, " Thefe,- my dear ;I down, and mike yourself comfortable," said Mrs. Condiment, drawing up the roCking-chair. • Capitols threw herself into it, and looked around and around the zoom, and then into the face of the old lady, saying "But what about the trap-door I—l see no trap- door !"</p>
        <p>" Ah, yes—look !" said Mrs. Condiment, lifting up the rug and revealing a large drop some four feet square, that was kept up in its place by a short iron bolt. "Now, my uear, take care of yourself, for this bolt slides very easily, and if, while you happened to be walking across this place, you were to push the bolt back, the trap-door would, -drop and you fall down—heaven knows where !" "Is there a cellar under there ?" inquired Capi- tola, gazing with interest upon thy door. "Lord knows, 'child, I don't'! ,' I did once make one of the nigger men let it down so I could look in' it ; but, Lord, child, I saw nothing but a great,. black, deep vacuity, without bottom or sides! It put such a horror over me, that I never looked • down there'since, and never want to, I'm sure." " Ugh ! for goodness sake what was the horrid thing made for 1" ejaculated Capitola, gazing as if fascinated by the trap. , "The Lord only knows, my dear; for it was made long 'before ever the house came into the majo:'s family. But they do say—" whispered ;Mrs. Condiment, mysteriously. • . " Ah! what do they say?" asked Capitola eagerly throwing off her bennet and shim!, and settling ,herself.to hear some thrilling explanation. 'Mrs. Condiment slowly replaced the rug, drew another chair to the side of the young girl, and said:</p>
        <p>"They do say it was—a trap Or Indiana." "A trap for Indians ?" "Yes, my dear. You must know that this room belongs to the oldest part of the house. It was all built as far back as the old French and Indian war; but this room belonged to the part that dates back to the first settlement of the country." "Then I shall like it better than any room in the house, for I dote on old places with stories to them. Go on, please." " Yes, my dear." 'Well, first of all, this place was a part of the grant of land given to the Le Noire, And the first owner, old Henri Le Noir, was said to be one of the grandest villains that ever was heard of. Well, you see, he lived out here in his hunting-lodge, which is this part of 'the house."</p>
        <p>"Oh, my! then this very room was a part of the old pioneer hunter's lodge ?" " Yes, my dear, and they do say that he had this place made as a trap for the Indians. You see, they say he was on terms of friendship with the Succapoos, a little tribe of Indians that was nearly wasted away, though among the few that was left there were several braves! Well, he wanted to buy a certain large tract of land from this tribe, and they were all willing to sell it, except these half a dozen warriors, who wanted it for camping- ground. So what does this awful villain do, but lay a snare for them. He makes a: great feast in his lodge and invites his red brothers to come to it; and they come. Then he proposes that they stand upon his blanket and all swear eternal brotherhood, which he made the poor souls believe was the right way to do it. Then when they all six stood close together as they could stand, with hands held up touching above their heads, all of a sudden the black villain sprung the bolt, the trap fell, and tho six men went down—down, the Lord knows where."</p>
        <p>"Oh, that is horrible! horrible cried Capi- tols' "but where do you think they fell to 1" "I tell you the Lord only knows. They say that it is a bottomless abyss, with no outlet but one crooked one miles long that reaches to the Demon's Punch Bowl. But if there it, a bottom to that abyss, that bottom is strewn with human bones." _</p>
        <p>"Oh, horrible! most horrible!" exclaimed Capt. tola.</p>
        <p>"Perhaps you are afraid to sleep here by your- self? if so, there's the damp room—" "Oh, no! oh, no! lam not afraid. I have been in too much deadly peril from the Using ever to fear the dead. No, I like the room, with its strange legend; but tell me did that human devil escape without 'punishment me, the tribe of the murdered victims?" • _ .</p>
        <p>"Lord, child, how were they to know of what was done? There wasn't a man left to tell the tale. Besides, the tribe was now brought down to a tew old men, women, and children. So, when he showed a bill of sale for the land he wanted, signed by the six braves---' their marks ' in six blood-red arrows, there was none to contradict him." "How was his villainy found out ?" "Well, it was said he married, had a family, and prospered for a long while; but that the poor Succapoos always suspected him, and bore a long grudge, and that when the sons of the murdered warriors grew up to be powerful braves, one night they set upon the house and massacred the whole fa.mily_eacept the eldest son, a lad of ten, who</p>
        <p>escap:ed and ran away and gave the alarm to the 'blochouse, where there were soldiers stationed. , It is said that after killing and scalping father, mother, and children, the savages threw the dead bodies down that trap-door. And they had just set fire to the house, and were dancing their wild dance around it, when the soldiers arrived and dis- persed the party, and put out the fire." -. "Oh, whet bloody, bloody days I" "Yes, my dear, and as I told you before if that horrible pit has any bottom, that bottom is strewn with human skeletons!" -</p>
        <p>"It is an awful thought--" "As, I said, my dear, if you feel at all afraid you can have another room." "Afraid—what of? 'Those skeletons, supposing them to be there, cannot hurt me. lam not afraid of the dead—l only dread the living y and not them much either," said. Capitols. . .</p>
        <p>"Well, my dear, you will want a waiting-woman, anyhow, and I think I will send Pitapat to wait on you; she can deli) on a pallet in your room, and be some company." "And who is Pitapat, Mrs. Condiment f' "Pitapat? 'Lord, child, she is the youngest of the housemaids. I've called her Pitapat ever since she was a little one beginning to walk, when she used to steal away from her mother, Dorcas, the cook, and I would hear her little feet coming pit-a. pat, pit-a-pat up the dark stairs up to my room. As it was often the only Bound to be'heard in the still house, I grew to call my little visitor Pitapat." "Then let me have Pitapat by all means. I like company, especially company that I can send away when I choose. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Very well, my dear, and now I think you'd better' smooth your hair and come down with me to tea, for,it is full time, and the major, as you may know, is not the most patient of men." Capitola took a brush from her travelling-bag, hastily arranged her black,ringlets, and announced herself ready. . . They left the room, and traversed the same laby- rinth of passages, stairs, empty Dooms and halls, back to the dining-room, where a comfortable ffre burned and a substantial supper was spread. , Old Hurricane took Capitola's hand with a hearty grasp, and placed her in a chair at the side, and then tor,k his own seat at the foot of the table.</p>
        <p>Xrs. Condiment sat at the head and poured out thc lea.</p>
        <p>"Uncle," said Capitola, suddenly, "what is under the trap-door in my room ?" " What have they put you in that room ?" exclaimed the old man, hastily looking up. -• " There 'was no other one preparedreir,'! said the housekeeper. "Besides, I like it very well, uncle," said Capitola. Iftitnph humph! humph !" grunted the old man, only half satisfied. "But, uncle, what about the—" Here Mrs Condiment stretched out her feet, and troll upon the toes of Capitola so sharply as to make her stop short, while she` deXterottsly changed the conversation by, asking the major .if he would not send Wool ,to Tip-Top in the morning; for another bag of coffee. Soon after 'supper was over,- Capitola, saying that she was tired, bade her uncle good-night, and, attended lay her little black maid Pitapat, whom Mrs. Condiment hatl..called up for the purpose, retired to her distant chamber. There were already collected her trunks, which the liberality of her uncle had. filled. _ _</p>
        <p>_ As soon as she .had got in and looked the door, she detached- one of the strongest straps_ from hex largest trunk, and tllen turned up the rug and: secured the end of the strap to the ring in the trap- door. Then she withdrew the bolt, and holding on to one end of the strap, gently lowered the trap, and kneeling, gazed down into an awful black void —without boundaries' without ' sight, without sounds, except a deep, faint, subterranean roaring as of water.</p>
        <p>"Bring the light, Pitapat, and hold it over this place, and take care you don't fall in," said. Capitola. "Come,' as I've got a ' pit 'in my name and you've got a 'st' in yours we'll see if we two can't make something of this third 'pit' I" "'Deed, I'se 'fraid, Miss," said the poor llttlà darkey. ' '"Afraid! what of !" 9 "Glioses." . "Nonsense, I'll agree to lay every ghost you see!"</p>
        <p>The little maid approached, •candle in hand, but In such a gingerly sort of way, that 'Capitol seized the light from her hand, and stooping, held it down as far as she could reach, and gazed once more into the abyss. But this only made tho horrible darkness ` visible " ; no object caught ot reflected a single ray of light all was black, hollow, void, and silent, except the faint, deep, distant roaring as Of subterraneous water 1 'Capitol&amp; 'pushed the as far' down as she could poosibly reach, and then yielding to a strange fascination, dropt it into the abyss! It went down down, down into the darkness, until far, below it , glimmered out of sight.! Then .with .szt_awful shudder Ca,pitola pulled up and fastened the trap• door, laid down the rug and said her prayers,_ .sind went to bed by the fire-light,—with little Pitapat sleeping on a pallet. The last thought °I., Cap, before falling to sleep was : . "It is awful to go to bed over such a horrible r mystery.; but I will ben hero 1" (To be continued.) ' • . ENdLISII dxar,é'ire DANGER.—A serious danger to which girls and young men are subjected, for want of proper precaution, is set forth in an interesting letter dated from Ida Villa, Wycombe 'Marsh, Bucks, on the 12th of November. It runs as follows :—lt is not be- cause lam anxious to have my name published that I write to you, but because having received benefit I should like others to benefit also. About fourteen months ago I received a severe shock to my nerves. From that time I begin to "run down" in health ; even after a rest at the seaside I continued to grow worse and worse, and became too ill to travel. My health was so bad that I could not retain my food, and I was often too weak to speak and was prostrated by violent attacks of neuralgia. A friend acquainted with the remarkable cures of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills persuaded me to try them. I followed the directions very minutely antishortly began to feel very much better. I was able to walk a considerable distance,though formerly even a . short exertion utterly tired me out. After taking the third box I was as well as ever, and able to resume my employment. All my friends were verymuch surprised at my recovery,as they thought I had gone home to die. I have witnesses ta prove that this is my true experi- ence.—Sarah L. Abbott. To people of ordinary health Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are the best pos- sible tonic,and they cure paralysis, 10,:onaotor ataxy, in- fluenza, rheumatim, sciatica, neuralgia, nervous head- ache, scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and sallow corn- pexion. Sold by chemists ; also by Dr. Williams' Medi- cine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box ; six boxes 13s. 9d., post free. A specific for the female sex, and in men they cure all cases from worry, I overwork, or excesses.</p>
        <p>PRESIDENT CLEVELAND IN THE STORM. How much exposure can a person endure and not taxa. cold ? It depends on circumstances. No, it dosen't either ; it depends on the person. Here is an illustration that will make you open your eyes and put on your thinking cap. On Saturday, March 4th, 1893, Hon. Grover Cleve- land was inaugurated President of the United States of liinerica,,at the City of Washington. The weather was as 'Atm bad could be; Snow, sleet, rain, with a cold bltlngwind,wereeomeof its elements. The streets of the.city were deep with. mud and slush. No More abominable or beastly weather , can be i =glued. Yet Mr. Cleveland exposed himself to it bareheaded for fee hours ; half an hour in delivering his inaugural address, and the remainder of. the time standing without head covering of any kind, on an open platform reviewing an immense procession. People watched him in wonder and, amazement. "He will catch his death." will need the doctor to-night," they said. They savY his face and his bald crown turn blue in the wintry 91116* Rheumatism, pneumonia, and gout seemed to flap ti3eir black wings over him, like death angels. • Yet he braved it out, hat in hand, went to a ball tee' -night, arose next morning fresh and chipper as a 0101- boy and never suffered at all—not even from a commou touch of the "snuffles." What on earth protecteeim? Good luck ? Special providence ? Not a bit. Still be was protected ; as everybody else might be, but usually isn't. We all remember the epidemic of influenza in the autumn and winter of 1891-92. How it did mow PecTle down, like a scythe swishing through the tall grael Yet it prostrated, or killed, only those who were its natural victims. Take a case. In January, 18920 Miss Mary Jones, of Towers Buildings, Llandrinio, near Oswestry, had an attack of influenza. After this she never got up her strength. She remained languid and feeble. P alpitation of the heart, pain in the chest' sour risings in the throat, dry and discoloured skim lc'ee of appetite, and distress after eating, were among the symptoms of her complaint. She grew weaker arid weaker until she could walk only with great eff°l.,,ti Medical treatment brought no relief. Aftkrmorith apparently hopeless illness, Miss Jones was at _Al' entirely cured by a medicine recommended by in Manchester. She states these facts in a letter dal' January 26th, 1893. • , "For over ten years," says Mr. Thomas Alford, Of Bedwardine Cottages, Quest Hill, Malvern Link, "1 Wafj 'ill. I always felt tired and weary, and had no life or energy. My mouth tasted foul, and I constantly sPa; up a thick phlegm. After eating I bad intense pain and oppression in the chest and sides, and a gnaWing sentw • tion in the stomach. Nothing that was done relieved me, until I used a medicine of which I heard by' aCei- dent. Having taken this for a few weeks all Pain aw'l, , discomfOrt left me,and I was like a man newly created. Mr. Alford's letter is dated January 13th, 1893. 4 Now, one moment, please,while we quote the !clue - a leading English physician. He says " untos Per; son(alriady has the poison of disease in him, damp exposure no no more lead to illness than do the stars ire heaven." • "The English people," says an American author, "e 3,11 do anything when once they, set their jaw and trY• Quite so ; and they Can understand this illuminating truth from one of their medical lights., • We see the point, don't ' we? We see what protected President Cleveland from harm during his 11C hours' ex- posure on that dreadful day. It w_as pure blood. 5.1 e bodily organs were sound and healthy. There were him no seeds of disease for the cold to develop into Woes! alai death. He was a live man all the way through,and could defy the snow, sleet, and wind: What would yell give for that sort of insurance on your own72A</p>
        <p>million—if you bad it ? Yes. Well, it won't cost you so much. The medicine that cared the writers of the letters above named will do thet same for anybody—Mother Seigel's Curative SyruP. I expels the poisons formed by indigestion and dysPePst and lifts between you and danger the shield of pure blo' enabling you to stand unscathed when others are falling like brown leaves in ttui blast. The introduction of machinery oU• St. Petersburg his led to serious rioting. About ath sand of the rioters are said to have been arrested. into a cigar factory in</p>
