      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Artifically-Scented Flowers.</head>
        <p>Artifically-Scented Flowers.</p>
        <p>Flowers that have los-t sopiething of their perfume are now scented artificially, by Wateringthem with an aloe- holic solution of essence, using 9,* tittle glyelerine to fix the odour. Thus, for violetsytheliquid is cothialsod of one hundred grammes of glycerine and 10 gram Mes Of essence of vinlet. In many places, while scented violets are comparatively rare, the unscented kinds glow wild in great profusion. These are now bought up in large quantities, scented artificially, and put into the market in advantageous competition with the perfumed violets grown by the horticulturists. Cut flowers which have wilted from time; or transport are revived by being plunged into a weak solution of Sal ammoniaC. Flowers which have little Or no scent are also perfumed for sale by being put into a box with ice, and then satu- rated with a current of carbonic aid charged with perfintue.</p>
        <p>k. Who Invent the Toys. , "Plenty of information is published regarding Edium and the other great inventors," observed the manager Of a large 'West-end fancy-goods emporium to a writer hi Cassell's Saturday Journal." the other day," but very few people outside the trade know anything at all abottt the inyedtors of toys, which annually' areuse perhaps millions of,young people. The, men who engage them- selves in this class of work are ,qUite soecialias in their line. That is to say, they invent toys for allying, and do nothing else. But as a rule, they are not very afflu- ent,for the work is poorly paid. You see the invenMr Is, as i rule, in such low water, that he is often compelled to sell- for a pound or two an invention out of which a manufacturing capitalist may make several hundreds pf pounds. As an instance, I -know for a. factethitt Sule bf the most popular puzzles of recent years was invehted by a man now living in a garret in Bethnal Green, and was sold by him to a manufacturer for fifty shillings. The tatter put the puzzle on the market,and netted over £6OO profit. Again, I know of a man who turns out some ingenious puzee or mechanical toy nearly every week, and, although they are good marketable milde , yet he is almost always hard up, Yes, most of these inventors are of the upper artisan class, but one of* cleverest of present-day toy inventors was formerly a clergyman, though drink has so reduced him that toe him you would hardly believe he was a man of educa- tion." '</p>
        <p>Fashion Fancies. Chiffon cf unusual shades of green, of brown, and pf purPlish read, is used for very elaborate blouses. lOis made up in accordion plaits,and is trimmed with points, tabs, or a collar of white and black lace, embroidered with jet. Skirts are at least not enlarging. Those of to-day are a shade narrower than yesterday's. The latest sleeve is moulded to the arm from the wrist to two inches above the elbow, and the puff at the top is full, short, and drooping. Leather for dress ,bodices and vests is something new in the winter fashions. It is tan in colour, thin enough to be pliable, and is decorated with applique lace as if it were satin or velvet.</p>
        <p>Fur trimmings fro-sa one to three inches wide are more in demand than ever, and they are in combination with lace, chiffon and dainty embreideries on the most dressy gowns. Sack Collars, revers, wide belts, and bretelles of fur are seen in the cloth gowns, and an entire bodice of fur is not unusual. 7 Another variety of the muchttrimmed seller band is gained by tying the bow in front instead of at the back. If ribbon is used, one yard and a half is the necessary quantity, This passes around the back through a button- hole made in the ribbon at the back, and ties in front. We inay take a useful hint from Gainsborough ; for, while he painted his beauties in gorgeous dress, he was careful to surround their.faces with subdued tints. No doubt 3.0 n think this idea Is Continually harped upon; but so many will kill whatever life may be in their faces with ungrateful colours that the reiteration seems riot uncalled for. Eyed the peacock has the testi; tb wear his gorgeous feathers behind him, Instead of on his head or neck.</p>
        <p>UL THOUSANDS THAN/0 All over the world testify to the peculiar curative powers of Hood's Sarsaparilla. Its remarkable sue. cess is one of the wonders of the day. Everywhere you go you will find someone who has be u benefited by this great medicine. It is the household word in many communities, and in hundreds of homes it is THE ONLY MEDICINE . . .</p>
        <p>and the only physician that is ever employed. Thousands ot men, women, and children are living to-day as illustrations of the health-giving powers of Hood's Sarsaparill.,,and in scores of cases they freely admit that they would not be alive tc-day but for Hood's Sarsaparilla. SLEEPLESS NIGHTS.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism and Pains in Head CaUSA the Trouble. It.lief and Cure Follow Taking hood's Sarsiparilla. " Gentlemen,—l suffered for three or four years witb rheumatism and pains in my head. I lost all appetite and could not sleep at night. I had been under the treatment of several doctors, but could not get any permanent relief. About six months ago I had my attention attracted by an ADVERTISEMENT OF HOOD'S</p>
        <p>Sarsaparilla. I tried so many different remedies that I bad got tired of them, but my husband in- duced me to try a bottle of Hood's Sarsaparilla. I was relieved a little after the first, but after taking the second and third bottles, I recovered my appe- tite, and could sleep splendidly all night, and the RHEUMATISM AND HEADACHE</p>
        <p>have entirely disappeared. I have continued to take Hood's Sarsaparilla and can say that I am now is the best of health, doing all my housework and thinking nothing of it, whereas formerly I had to employ a domestic. Myself and husband have such faith in Hood's Sarsaparilla that we do not intend to be without a bottle in the house, and shall recom- mend it to all our friends." Cwt.&amp; ADAMSON, 17, Southgates, Leicester. ITOOD'S SARSAPARILLA,</p>
        <p>The One True Blood Purifier. Sold by chemists • or sent post paid, 23. 9d. or 4s. 6d. by C. I. flood' and Co., 34, Snow Hill, London, HOOD'S PILLS Cure Constipation and Liver Ills. is. lid. (1)</p>
