      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BICYCLE FACE.</head>
        <p>THE BICYCLE FACE.</p>
        <p>We have heard much of late aonut the effects of occu- pation on physiognomy. A enrrespoudent now asks if we have observed the bicycle face. We certainly have noticed the earnest and grave expression usually car- ried by the professional cyclist, but it differs in no es- sential from that of any other sort of rider. For cen- turies it has been known to close observers that all men and women who ride horses, camels, or elephants have set faces. When Alexander commanded his orderlies to face Bucephalus towards the sun that he might not see his shadow, and mounted the fretful charger, does any- one believe that the king's features were relaxed and that the face of the mighty conqueror wore a grin ? A hundred times, no. The great general wore the so- called bicycle face. Did not Julius Cam say that the stern, warlike features of his cavalry were a host when kis troopers confronted the infantry of Pompey ? In short, nobody can ride any animal or any machine that requires the centralisation of thought without his fea- tures reflecting the concentration. Railroad engineers, sailors, jockeys, cavalrymen, and all other equestrians, have the bicycle face. The boys who ride or exercise thoroughbreds acquire the bicycle face in a few weeks, as their minds are on their safety, even if their fancy suns riet,and they imagine that the homes they ride are future Derby or St. Leger winners. They must be alert and quick to act in an emergency. Consequently all successful jockeys have the bicycle face. In the Russian Cossack the bicycle face is hereditary,as the race is prac- tically born in the saddle. Nobody ever saw a negro of South Africa with a bicycle face.</p>
