      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CALIFORNIA AND IMMIGRA TION.</head>
        <p>CALIFORNIA AND IMMIGRA TION.</p>
        <p>BY MR. H. WRITHAM.</p>
        <p>It seems like ancient history to turn back to the time when Mr. Gladstone advised the British far- niers to turn their attention to fruit growing. We can remember the flouts and gibes his advice was received with, and yet time has proved that the farmers were well advised. One has but to turn to the agricultural press to learn the fact that farm- ing is not a good paying business, and, strange though it may seem, we may also find in the same Column ways and means pointed out whereby the farmers might benefit themselves immensely. When We read of the thousands of pounds spent annually in the purchase of foreign produce, poultry, eggs. Cheese, butter and fruit, it does seem absurd, and takes one feel that the British farmer is most cer- tainly the most stupid lout the foreign press depict him. We have all heard, times without number, of the unwillingness of the British work- Man to change, or accept changes in the methods of doing work, or of production. It has been said that his conservatism was ruinous to himself, and to his employer, but what shall we say of the fer- nier, who by a persistence in old and antiquated Methods is ruining a whole country ? There are Signs that this apathy on the farmers' part is being Cleared away. Men, and women also, who have studied the question are coming forward, and by lectures, and through cheap literature, are pointing out the way it is done in other countries, notably Denmark, where good butter and good cheese can be profitably made and exported, the stupidity of John Bull making England a good market. I see from an " ad." in the Examiner, that Mr. J. B. Isaac affords an opportunity to the Warrington Patriot to help the Cheshire farmer. A Creamery has been established, pure butter is made, by help- ing such a movement the farmer can be helped in a surer and readier fashion than by whining at the doors of St. Stephen's. It seems a strange thing to me when I look over tity paper, to find that such distant countries as Australia and California should be competing for the English market. That beef, butter, and fruit Should be sent thousands of miles over land and sea to a country like England so well able to provide these commodities for herself. I am surprised to find it stated that plums are rotting on English trees,while Californian plums are being sold in Lon- lion at good prices. Reading between the lines, it Will be seen from the above, that there is one great and prominent idea in the minds of all who are engaged in the manipulation of capital, and the Conduct of business—that is the finding of a mar- ket. There are wheat growing districts in America that could easily supply the whole of the civilised World with bread. These districts having to corn- Pete with Egypt, Russia, and the Argentines will Certainly provide the English workman with cheap bread, but the English farmer finds his grief and riot his profit in the result. But why should America seek England as her special market? Because she must have a market for her surplus produce, and no other country at present offers the same profits. France with her system of small farming has a sur- plus for export,and no market for imports. America Must find a market or perish. A short time ago a letter from Mr. Winstanley Was printed in the Examiner. As lam writing this away from home, I cannot quote his exact Words, but the purport of it was that fruit was a drug in the district, and he would be glad to get bold of a market. Readers may imagine how much More is this the case in a State like California where fruit growing forms the shape of business. Sun- shine nine months out of twelve, and this mixed With no uncertainty. Astate of things here that seems somewhat mixed. The more people come here the more land is token, and of course the More fruit grown, and yet ten outside markets has to be found or made. To induce people to come, Clubs are formed, composed of land-owners, and real estate agents. Of course,these persons are pri- marily interested in selling land, and have hitherto Pursued an unprincipled course, not caring one rap as to whether the" ads." they placed in European Papers were true or false. I may here give a couple of samples of American Smartness, so that, would-be emigrants or specula- tors may make careful enquiries before deciding on 1M important a step as coming out to the States. A new district called "Lakeview" was advertised. The tax collector looked around for the place. Neither be nor the postal authorities could find it. "Lake- 'hew, however, does exist, and there is now on file in the Recorder's office at Hanford an elaborate lithographic plot of the imaginary town, which tepresents a railroad depot, churches, schoolhouses and other public buildings and is described as being on section 8, township 23, range 20. That portion of section 8 on which the town is represented to be, ie now and always has been under the waters of Tulare lake." The next sample does not come from California but ought to be widely kuown. The United States Commissioner at Kansas is now after a man named Atkinson who made it a business "to induce the sons of English professional men to come to the United States and work on Kansas farms, With the understanding that they will acquire a large estate in a few years. It is claimed that When Atkinson gets the Englishmen here he makes a contract with them by which they agree to work at sdols. a month for a year, and Atkinson then hires them out to Kansas farmers." I could tell also of other large and real towns, in which are railways, depots, hotels, churches, and streets full Of houses, with not a single inhabitant, but let us deal especially with California and its prospects. I am not going to belittle the State and home of ray adoption, but I wish to enjoin care upon all Who think ot coming here. I have received several letters of late asking advice on those points. I had rather give my answer through the "Examiner" than privately. The clubs I have referred to are known as half million, or million clubs. Their ob- ject is to raise the population of their districts to the number desired. This city has its million club. Why it should be anxious to raise the population of the city up to that figure I cannot say. All I know ' ie, that there is, the year round, a very large num- ber of men and women who cannot get work, and it is not by any means an unusual thing to read in the papers smsational accounts of suicides through inability to find work, and of wretched deaths through want and starvation. I know that rents are very high. I pay 259. a week for a house that In Warrington would not cost me more than 6s. or is. a week at most, and only in exceptional cases do he wages average Bs. a day. I think there is a Three million club" for the States,so let us go out of , the city into the country. A year ago I was working in the country at a place known as Fresno, and I remember as I got into the car to leave it that a gentleman sitting opposite me made the remark, " What a lovely country this is." " Yes," I said, "it is, a lovely miserable country." He seemed as- toniahed, and said he had been travelling that Country for ten years, and had never heard ;such a remark before He had no further use for me, and at once left his seat. I think I was deep in a fit of the" blues" est the time. Picking grapes by day and sleeping under the vines at night, for less than a dollar a day was not exhilarating. Packing raisins for ss. a day and paying a pound a week for board and lodging did'nt seem to my poor intellig- ence the best way to keep a wife and three chil- dren. But perhaps I was too much hipped alto- gether. Well how does the following cutting which I happen to have in my pocket book read : `' Fresno, August 19th.—The annual immigration of grape-pickers has suddenly assumed large propor- tions, and hundreds of white men from San Fran- cisco and Los Angelos are walking about the streets Of the city, with nothing to do and poor prospects of getting work. Table grapes will not be ripe for several days, and the picking of this class of grape does not usually last more than ten days or two Weeks. There is much discontent at the wages of- fered. idol. a ton, it being claimed that only an eXperienced picker working in the best vineyards can pick a ton a day. The average will probably be not more than 75 cents a day.' Not much hope in the country judging from the above,—that is—not for the common labour. Let ns look at the man who may have a little capital. have had, within the last few days, an offer made ,to Me of a small fruit farm. A good house, barn, norse, buggy, cow, two hundred chickens here,frnit trees all well laden with fruit, all for the small sum °f 12. a week. The drawback is that it is eighty miles from the city, and has no market, for the Want of which the fruit has consequently rotted on the trees. Not much of a prospect even for a small capitalist. But for an independent man who wishes to end his days in a district where the dust is, in summer, up to the hub of his buggy, and the sun is generally somewhere about 100," why the mere thought is sublime. Aa a matter of fact, the fruit growers of Cali- Mrnia must have a market or ruin stares them in the face. Prom an article in a paper before me I cut the following :—" As matters stand, the small Vineyarnist is growing poorer every day and is ut- terly incapable of paying interest on the mortage</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>on his vineyard. His creditor does not expropriate him simply because he dare not bring the property to sale. ',these are conditions which work out their own cure in time. There will be a day when th bank or capitalist who has leaned mlney on Oral ea land will have to sell it to save him•elf.lt will'com- mand some price and the sale will start the proceBB of liquidation. The small men will be wiped out and their lands will pass into stronger hands. Then but not till then, will the Fresno raisin-grower begin to get the legitimate rewards of his industry." Cold-blooded as this may seem, it is the truth. In another district of this Ftate, things seem to have reached a climax. Men out of work have not hesitated to u.,e incendiarism. At Woodland the packing-house and smokehouses, together with 5,000 boxes and trays and several cars, all belonging to C. A. Blake, an orchardist who lives near Winters, were destroyed by incendiary fire on Saturday night. A large number of white, men are out of employment, solely because Japanese are employed entirely by the orchardists all about there. Of late the orchardists have been receiving letters threatening them with all sorts of destruc- tion if they persisted in hiring Japanese help. Little or no attention has been paid to these com- munications, although some of the white labourers have been open in their threats. The fruit-growers at their wits end, send their fruit down to the city There is no market, and thousands of tons of fruit of all kinds have this year been thrown into the bags. What I have said applies all over the State. The Santa Clara Valley, the garden of California, is mortgaged for 12,000,000d015. and the entire fruit crop of that section is sold for hardly enough to pay the interest on the indebtedness and the taxes.</p>
        <p>Concluding this rather doleful letter, I ought to say that the clubs I have referred to are now being composed of reputable citizens, who claim that they will furnish all needful and truthful information to parties wishing to buy land and settle here. Such facts will, of course, consist of information, touch- ing railway convenience, irrigation, and general situation of lands applied for, and will, I think, be honestly given. In spite of all I have said above I think that men with small families of growing children will do well here. They must have a few hundred pounds, and must be workers. At present a deal of the land is in the hands of speculators. One man, an Englishman, owns 12,000,000 acres, an extent of land more than twice as large as York- shire, Northumberland, Durham, and Cumberland combined. Time will alter this, small farms will be the rule and then the under man will have a chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>2,409, Mission-street, San Francisco. AN EXTRAORDINARY BRIDGEBUILDING FEAT.</head>
        <p>2,409, Mission-street, San Francisco. AN EXTRAORDINARY BRIDGE- BUILDING FEAT.</p>
        <p>A work of considerable interest as illustrating what can now be done in the way of rapid bridge construction was carried out, says the "Times," on the Great Eastern Railway at Ely on Sunday. The Cutter bridge which passed over the river Ouse at Ely was an old timber and cast-iron girder structure, which was put up in 1845, but was not considered equal to the heavier rolling-stock of the present day. It had, therefore, been decided to substitute for it a bridge of cast-iron girders, and having, too, a single span across the river, on brick abutments, in place of the three spans and wooden piles of the old bridge. The problem that engineers and contractors set themselves to solve was how to put the one bridge in place of the other with the least possible interfer- ence with any of the ordinary trains, whether passen- ger or goods, the point in question being one of the busiest on the Company's system. The first part of the programme to be carried out consisted in the construction of the new bridge on temporary stag- ing on one side of the line, parallel with and clear of the existing bridge, so that it would be quite ready for placing in position as soon as the latter was taken away. This was done, and on Saturday the new bridge, com- plete in every respect, including the lines of the perma- nent way thereon, was to be seen standing alongside the main line in readiness for the work on Sunday. The ends of the main girders were supported on small " trolleys," and each of these worked on a rail placed on longitudinal timber on the top of the temporary staging, so as to allow of the new structure being eventually drawn into position. At ten minutes past one on Sunday morning operations were begun. In ten minutes the first rai4 on the up line had been removed, and in another ten minutes the first girder was being carried off by two locomotive steam cranes working on the down line. The scene was illuminated by power- ful Wells' lights, and girder after girder was carried off, steadily increasing the gap. The wooden piles which had supported the girders were sawn throuek above water-level, and the " crowns " carried off by the cranes, the lower portions being left to be " drawn " on a future occasion. At last the upline and the greater porticn of each of the particular piles on which it had rested had been cleared right away, leaving only the down line and its supports standing. Then came the most interesting feature of the morning's work—the " traversing " or drawing sideways of the new bridge, so that it would occupy (until the whole of the old bridge had been removed) the place where the up line had formerly stood. This traversing was done by means of two ropes worked by winches.and although the massive structure weighed, including rails and timber' something like 120 tons, it was drawn into its half-way position—a distance of 15ft. —within a minute or two over half an hour. By this time it was nearly six o'clock in the morning, and the actual time taken by the opera- tions referred to was only about three and a half hours. Work was resumed at noon, and the workers, about forty in number, proceeded to clear away the down line. The new bridge was then brought into position, for the remainder of the distance, in half an hour; the connec- tions between the rails and with the abutments were completed, and at eight o'clock in the evening the ex- press from Norwich to London passed safely over the new bridge. The substitution of the new bridge for the old one had taken about eleven hours' ectual working. and had involved scarcely four hours' interruption of traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PECULIAR "HERO."</head>
        <p>A PECULIAR "HERO."</p>
        <p>At the Central Criminal Court on Tuesday, Edward James Collis, 38. lately a constable in the employment of the London County Council, was indicted for unlawfully marrying Mary Goddard, his wife being then alive. The prisoner served formerly in the Royal Artillery in India, and for distinguished conduct at the Battle of Mai wand he received the Victoria Cross. The evidence showed that the prisoner married first on March 14, 1882, at the Mission Church, Girgann, Bombay, his wife being a widow named Sense. The alleged bigamous marriage , was solemnised on February 26, 1893, at St. Paul's Church, in Battersea. The prisoner was arrested at his house in the St. Dunstan's-road, East Dulwich, and, in reply to the charge, he said that he had not seen his lawful wife for many years. He added, " I thought you could always marry after seven years. I think I can get out of it. Is there anything else against me. Don't stir up dirty water." The second wife, a respectable young woman, said the prisoner pretended that he was a single man when he married her. He turned her and her child into the street, after treating her very cruelly.—The jury found the prisoner guilty.—Sergeant Smale said about eight years ago the prisoner came home from India, and , met a most respectable young girl, whose parents lived in the East-end. He ruined her under the promise of marriage, and her father turned her out of home owing to her condition. She was subsequently confined of a ' cripple. The prisoner deserted the girl, whose father ' had died since, as soon as he knew of her condition. He next treated a young girl named Mary Jones in a very ; cruel manner. He cut the girl all over the body,knocked her teeth out, and on one occasion threw her through al ' plate-glass window. After deserting Jones he met Mary t Goddard, whom he turned into the streets destitute with ; her child. After ruining another girl, whom he also • deserted as soon as be found that she was enceiente, he • went to live, as a single man, at a house in Bow-street. , When arrested he was living with a girl 20 years of age, to whom he had promised marriage in a fortnight. His , career for the last eight years had been one round of ' immorality. He was dismissed from a corps of Corn- ; missionaires, and from the service of the London County ' Council for misconduct. He had got these situations ' trading on the fact that he held the Victoria Cross. At • the police court the prisoner turned to the witness and 3 said, "I shall get out of this. I had ' kidded' the doe- tor at Holloway that I am insane, and he has placed me • in the infirmary and when I get to the Old Bailey I will kid 'the judge."—The Common Sergeant said the pri- soner had been guilty of a very cruel case of bigamy, . and had ruined a respectable young woman by fraud. Be seemed to have made a regular business of this sort Of thing. His conduct detracted much from the services 3 he had rendered his country, for which he received the r Victoria Cross. But for the holding of that mark of honour he would have sent the prisoner to penal 3 servitude. The prisoner was sentenced to 18 months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>Sir Ughtred Kay-Shuttleworth,'- M.P., addressed a meeting at Habergham on Tuesday night on the subject of the navy. Hesaid the country might be satisfied that the machinery of the Admiralty was being worked with an intelligent, active, patriotic determination to main- tain and increase in all respects the strength and readi- ness of the navy.</p>
        <p>BEAUTY AND PURITY go hand in hand. They are the foundation of health and happiness; health, because of pure blood ; happiness, because of clear skin. Thou- sands of useful lives have been embittered by distressing humours. CIITICURA remedies are the greatest of skin purifiers as well as blood purifiers. Because of their peculiar action on the pores they are successful in pre- venting and curing all forms of skin, scalp and blood humours, when the best physicians and all other methods fail. Safe, innocent, and effective, they especially ap- peal to mothers and children. Their use at all seasons insures a clear shin and pure blood, as well as sound bodily health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
