      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>14',XAMINER, SAT'URDAY, NOVEMBER 30 1895.</head>
        <p>THE 14',XAMINER, SAT'URDAY, NOVEMBER 30 1895.</p>
        <p>FOREIGN MISSIONARY MEETING AT CHRYSANTHEMUM SHOW IN WYCLIFFE CHURCH. THE PARR HALL. MISSIONARY WORK IN MADAGASCAR AND THE SOUTH SEA.O. On Thursday evening last week there was a missionary tea meet.rig in Wycliffe Hall,Warrington,to which about 130 persons sat down. After tea a public meeting was held in the church, and there was a fair attendance. Mr. E. J. Guest, 8.A., J.P., took the chair and was accom- panied on the platform by the Revs. John Yonge' W. J. Kirkland, W. E. Cousins, (Madagascar,) and W. N. Lawrence, (South Seas). After devotional excercises, the Nev. John Yonge said that in tho centenary report of the London missionary Society for the year ending 31st March, 1895 it was recorded that collections at Wycliff. a during the year for the society had amounted to £3O ss. sd. For the widows' fund £1 17s. 2d. had been sent ; week of special giving had realized £1 143. 7d. ; ladies working society £l2; girls' sewing class £1 103. : Y.P. 5.C.E.,£3 7s. ; Sunday school £l9 us. Id.•, New Year's offering, £7 73. sd. ; sums under 103., 9s. 6d.•' all these items made a total of £7B Bs. 2d. Cockhedge had contri- buted £3 63. 6d. and Mr. Armitage had given £25. He hoped that they would be able to send up from Wycliffe church one way or another to the centenary fund cer- tainly not less than £250, and he personally expected that they would exceed that sum. Mr. Armitage had promised £lOO and Mr. Mathi3son of Liverpool, £5O, while there were several promises of good amounts from other friends so that they could see their way to a minimum sum of £250 from Wycliffe Church. He was quite sure they could give all that if they did what God enabled them to do.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Floral and Horticultural Society has still plenty ot vigour, as evidenced by the size and excellence of the display of chrysanthemums at the show held under the auspices of the Society , in the new Parr Hall on Wednesday afternoon. Competition seemed to have been entercd into with considerable keenness. The various exhibits were arranged down the sides of the room, while in the centre of several minor groups arranged nearer the middle, stood a huge and brilliant cluster from the greenhouses of the Town Hall, which have been under the care and cultivation of Mr. Isaac Parker, head gardener. Special attention was drawn to a beau- tiful selection of orchids from the hothouses of Alderman Bolton, which were effectively laid out on the front part 0f the platform. Refreshments were supplied at a stall in charge ot Mrs. Mottram. The adjudicators /of prizes were Messrs. G. Tandy (Norton Priory) and Mr.J. Smith (Arley Hall), and they commenced the work of judging shortly after eleven o'clock in the morning. , The prizes were awarded as follows : OPEN DIVISION GENTLEMEN'S GARDENERS.—PIants in pots, group of chrysanthemums, arranged for effect in space not under 40 square feet and not over 60 square feet : 1, Mrs. Peter Rylands, (305.) ;2. Mr. R. A. Nay- lor (205.) Collection of 12 cut blooms, six Japanese and six incurved, distinct and one of each variety : I, Mrs. Long (103.) ; 2, Mr. J. K. Glazebrook (7s. 6d.) AMATEUR DIVISION.—PIants in pots : Group of chrysanthemums, arranged for effect in space not less than 20 square feet and not over 30 square feet : 1, Mr. John Randles (203.); 2,Mr. Joseph Grounds (125.6 d. 3, Mr. Joseph Heesors (55.). Collection of six cut blooms, various and distinct, one of each variety : 1, Mr. John Rigby (7s. 61.) ; 2, Mr. Joseph Heesom (55.) ; 3, Mr. Arthur Rowlinson (2s. 6d.) Specimen plant in blaam, any variety (Prizes given by Mr. George Starkey, seeds- man, Warrington) : 1, Mr. John Rigby (7s. 6d.) ; 2,Mr. Joseph Grounds (55.) Spray of blooms, any variety, (Prizes given by Messrs. Pierpoint and Co., seedsman, Warrinzton) : 1, Mr. John Handles (10s,) ; 2, Mr. John Rigby (7s. 6d.) At the commencement of the opening ceremony there were present Mr. John Crosfield. J.P., the Mayor (Councillor James Fairclough, J.P.), the Mayoress, Councillor Smethurst, Mr. R. G. Flenshall, Dr. White, J.P., Alderman Wright, J.P., Mr. R. Davies, the Rev. H. V. Pigot, Mr. J. C. Thompson, Mr. R. T. Fairclough, Mr. John Houghton, Mr. E. Richardson Mr. John Fairclough, J.P., Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., dr. T. May, Councillor Lee, Mr. W. Chantry, Mr. J. Seward, Mr. S. Wilson,Mr. T. Fletcher,Mr. R.I. Wynne Jones, the Revs. L. J. T. Darwell and W. FL Drummond, B.A. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. GUEST said he came there in obsedi- , ence to an invitation of their worthy pastor, and he was very glad to be there for twa reasons especi- ally. One was to show a brotherly feeling and sympathy for them though he belonged to another section of the Christian Church,and also because he took the deepest interest in the work of the foreiga missions. There bad been a poet deal said of late of reunion, and he wanted to know what it meant. Did it mean that we were to give up all the principles we had held dear from our childhood ? If reunion meant that whilst we retained our distinctive denominational features we held out the right hand of fellowship to each other then he thought the sooner we had reunion the better. There were some objections raised to missionary work,objectiona which to some extent were latent in the minds of many of us. One objection was that we had better set our own house in order first. There was no doubt that although Chris- tianity had existed in England for a long time,that we still had men in this country who were as deeply imbued with heathenism as those to whom we sent our mission- aries. Then it was said that we had not the means to , do all we wanted at home and send missionaries abroad; we could not spare some of our best men and the means to send them out. He did not think that many churches had suffered much by the work they had done for foreign missions. (Hear, hear.) We had sent out grand and noble men like Livingstone and Henry Martin and a host of others who had carried out their work at great self- sacrifice,and who when they returned home had great influence and perhops greater that they would have had had they not gone abroad. He was thinking that very day that the contributions to philanthropic and such like work did not amount to one-tenth of the money spent on the luxuries of life in this country. If they put together the Drink Bill and the bills for luxuries, etc., they would amount to something like £300.000,000, and if the Church could get bold of £30,000,000 how much could he done not only at home but abroad? He believed that this and similar enterprises were the best institutions in which to spend our money. (Hear, hear; and applause.) Many Englishmen to-day did not know what to do with their money, and if they would only invest it in institutions hie this there would be no unemployed,and he believed that the world would soon be Christianised and civilised,and that trade would flourish in our land. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The Rev.W. E. Cousnes,in the course of an interesting address, said his experience with theL.M.S. went so far back as 1862, and he expected in a few months to be back in Antananarivo, and though the present outlook was somewhat troubled he hoped they would still be able to go on with their work of helping and encouraging the many native churches there. In his experience in Madagascar there were six happy or red letter days standing out prominently, not that he bad only had six happy days, for he had had thousands, but these repre- sented some occasion for which he was truly thankful. In 1835 when the early missionaries were driven away they left some 200 members,and for more than a quarter of a century the Queen of the country had tried her best to stamp out the name of Christ frcm Madagascar, yet they, coming after those long years of darkness and dis- tress, found present at their re-opening celebration meeting just four times as many members as when the early missionaries left their work. That was God's message and encouragement to young missionaries just entering upon their work. After events had fully proved how correct those early antici- pations were. That day was his first happy day—His second came some eight months later on May 17th,1863, the first Sunday after the ascension of the young king. For about 10 days the capital had been in a state of riot and their minds had been full of dark foreboding' for there were many peopleln the land wishing to get back to the old days of persecution and to wrest from the Christians their newly acquired 'freedom. After the ascension of the new sovereign, however, there was an edict issued granting freedom of worship to all classes of the community, and he could never forget the thank- fulness with which the mission met that afternoon. They now prayed God that under the rule of the French Republic the churches might never loose freedom. The third "happy "day was the Bth September, 1869, seven years after their 'arrival, and that :reminded him of ;the burning of the national idols. That result of their work he did not think any of them expocted would come so quickly as in seven years of teaching. The burning of the idols he regarded as a great national event and landmark in the history of the country,and a great sten forward. The fourth day was Saturday, 30th April, 1887, when at a meeting of their committee at Antananarivo they realised that the work of 11 years—the revision of the Malagasy scriptures— was brought to a satisfactory conclusion. The new Bible had been in the hands of the people for about five years and be was quite certain that it conveyed to the minds of the people of Madagascar words that interested them and which they loved. His fifth red-letter day was the 18th May, 1891, when in their High School for boys in Antananarivo they had a meeting of about 400 young people,the last of a week of special services for young people connected with their High Schools. The 6th and last day was Sunday the 20th September, 1891, when a meeting was held in one of their memorial churches which was within a few yards of the ditch where the first martyr, a young woman, was speared to death in 1837. That day was a red-letter day because they were there to found the first Christian Endeavour Society. Of that society they now had 100 distinct branches and the movement seemee just to fill up the gap which came when the young people left school. Those were his six happy days and be always rernemteered them as marking some reminiscence or chronicling some definite step forward. They all knew now that French power was established in Madagascar. They could not see the future but the French had made a treaty which said that in the island of Madagascar the missionaries would enjoy complete protection. (Applause.) The Rev. W. N. LAWRENCE said he would endeavour to enable them to see the people of the South Sea Islands as they actually were to-day. It was a most difficult thing for those who had not been there to realise the condition of these people. There were so many anoma- lies in their life as they advanced as far in one direction as they went back in another. The rev, gentleman in a very interesting and amusing manner then related the manners and customs of the people of Tahiti and islands' of the Hervey group, especial!f the modes of worship as contrasted with ours. Among other things he mentioned that nearly all the congregation took notes of the dis- courses,and the preachers were thus unable to preach old sermons. Worship was usually begun at five o'clock in the morning when one of the deacons preached the ser- mon—(the Rev. John Yonge : "Hear, hear ")—after the missionary had revised it and made suggestions for improvement. While the people were in church three policemen went through the village from either end to see that all had gone to worship and those that had not if they were without reasonable excuse, were either compelled to go, or go to bed. (Laughter.) Mr. Lawrence also detailed the work of the missionaries, which from about six in the morning till four in the afternoon was constant, and included the teaching of children, native teachers, and also the instilling of a knowledge of carpentry and of blacksmith's work into the latter. All the outward signs of heathenism were gone from these islands, he added, but not altogether from the hearts of the people. They were however,grow- ing up slowly and surely in Jesus Christ. (Applause.) The Rev. W. J. KIRKLAND proposed a vote of thanks to their respected chairman who, he believed, was the right man in the right place. (Hear, hear and ap- plause.) The Rev. Joni.: YONGE seconded the vote, including' the names of the gentlemen who had given such good addresses.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. CROSFIELD,iII opening the show, said that it gave him great pleasure to be present that afternoon at the second annual exhibition of the Warrington Floral Society. At this season of the year the only flower almost that could be exhibited was the chrysanthemum. The chrysanthemum,he said, was arown by the Chinese 800 years ago. He believed that the plant was brought to this country from Japan and last year was the cen- tenary of ths chrysanthemum flower—that was to say, it was only brought to England 101 years ago. It was never brought to anything like perfection until about 60 years ago by a man named Salter and two of the best' specimens that he had raised were Fleur de Marie and Annie Salter. Those two varieties Mr. Crosfield had grown at his old house in Bank Quay, 50 years ago. (Applause.) He remembered the flower perfectly well as it existed when he was a lad ten years of age,and they had got the same flower to-day at Walton Lea. (Ap- plause.) He thought it ranked as the best white-quilled centre chryanthemum ever grown. 'I he growing of flowers was elevating and instructive to everybody (hear, hear)—but unfortunately the working class in a town like ours had very little chance of growing any- thing. We are afflicted he was sorry to say, with smoke, which would some day have to be cured. If the workingmen had gardens they could not possibly grow those flowers owing to the smoke. He was told on Tuesday by Mr. Worsley that in the east end of London there was a number of gentlemen who subscribed and gave hyacinth bulbs to the poor people in London and offered prizes for the finest flowers. He had spoken to their secretary about that land he would like it to be tried in Warrington and he would provide the bulbs and give the prizes if the people would try the experiment. (Loud applause.) Even a group of hyacinths in a cottage window was a lesson, a pleasure and an enjoyment, not to speak of the delicious I scent they got from the flower. Wherever they went, said Mr. Crosfield, they would never find a flower with the scent of theirs in England. In other countries they got flowers more brilliant in colour owing to the climate but they sacrificed scent for colour, and though they I gathered roses in California, one of the richest countries in the world for fruit and flowers, they would never find one with the deiicious scent of those we grow in our own gardens. (Applause.) Although we might noehave the most beautiful climate in the world, still old England could hold her own as regards delicious and beautifully scented flowers. That was some encouragement, and he was pleased indeed to see the beautiful exhibition they had there that day, not only of chrysanthemums but of those lovely orchids supplied by Alderman Bolton. He trusted the society might flourish and that every year they would see a still better exhibition, and he had great pleasure in declaring the present one open. The Rev. H. V. PIGOT proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfield for coming and making a speech which had interested them very much. Mr. Crosfield was a true lover of flowers and what was more a very unselfish lover of flowers. (Hear, hear and Applause). The MAYOR seconded the vote, and personally thanked Mr. Crosfield for coming that day and giving them the address be had.</p>
        <p>Mr. CIIO9F/ELD said he was much obliged for the vote of thanks and wished to add that it was a great pleasure to him to see the peonle of Warrington enjoying the gardens and flowers at Walton Lea. He thought it was the duty of every man, who possessed conservatories and camellia-houses and beautiful flowers, to let the people around him see the beauties of nature.</p>
        <p>This concluded the opening ceremony and luring the rest of the afternoon there was a continuously good attendance. _ . -</p>
        <p>During the afternson a selection of music on the organ was given by Mr. J. B. Caldwell, followed by the songs "Blue Ribbon Jane" and " Ta-ta!" by Mr. William W. Whittle.</p>
        <p>CONVERSAZIONE IN THE EVENING.</p>
        <p>In the evening, a conversazione was held in the Hall, presided over by Alderman Bolton. Mr. T. J. Down's well known orchestral band was in attendance, and per- formed a number of selections from many well known composers. The artistes who took part in the pro- gramme, were Mrs. Stevenson, Mrs. Atkin, and Mr. Fryar, who gave every satisfaction. Mr. William W. Whittle was the humoristnf the evening, and was very successful in arousing the risibilities of the audience. The programme was an excellent one throughout, and the conversazione was in every way a great success. Alderman BOLTON at the commencement of the pro- ceedings, observed that that show was the greatest suc- cess the Society had had yet, and he thought that there had never been a better flower show in Warrington. Ho hoped that they would be still more successful next year. (Applause.) During the course of the evening the Rev, Morley Stevenson gave a very interesting address on the chry- santhemum. He said that he ipoke a few words to them some twelve months ago, and, therefore, if his sermon was dry and uninteresting they must not'blame him,but blame the committee who had asked him. (Laughter.) When a person prepared a sermon the first thing he did was to turn about for a suitable text, and herefore, he (Mr. Stevenson) thought that he could not find• a more suitable text for that evening than that suggested to him by what he saw around him at that moment. His text would be "Chrysanthemums." The fret thing he would say about them was that he thought they all owed them a debt of gratitnde for two things. First of all, they knew that they came in all their beauty at a time of the year when it was extremely difficult to get other flowers. It was exactly 100 years since the chry- santhemum was grown in this country in the nursery at Chelsea, and if they had lived 100 years age, they could not have seen, and could not have had the chance of seeing such a magnificent sight as they saw around them that evening. Not only would they have had no chance of seeing such a sight, but they would have tn.d no other flowers with which to replace them ,and though they might have met together and have had a very pleasant evening,there would have been no flower show. In the dark and gloomy November, when almost all flowers were dead, and all the trees had lost their leaves, it was a happy thing that they should be able to bring together in such a town as Warrington such a collection of beautiful and lovely flowers as they saw there that evening. (Applause.) The other debt they owed to the chrysanthemum was that, although they could not get some flowers to grow in Warrington—he had never heard of roses being grown al- though they might get to that yet they could grow the chrysanthemum without the slightest difficulty. Some people liked smoking, and others didn't. The chryanthemum did like smoke, at all events it did not object to it. There was also another thing which he reSpected the chrysanthemum for, and that was that it had risen in life—very considerably in- deed from the ancestors from which it sprang. He dared say that many present would know that in reality the chrysanthemum was a magnificent, cultivated, well educated relation of the common daisy. If they took a common daisy and looked at the little white florets which encircled the golden netals which formed its centre,and then looked at the chrysantheinum before it had been doubled,they would see that was neither more</p>
        <p>After the vote had been heartily carried Mr. GUEST suitably responded, and the meeting shortly afterwards closed.</p>
        <p>R6)bert Ward, landlord of the Crown Inn, High Wy- combe, on Saturday murdered his housekeeper.a woman named Bannister, by shooting her while she slept, and then committed suicide.</p>
