        <p>nor less than a magnified edition of that daisy. They would also fled steps between the two. If they went on the moss in the summer titne,they would find the beauti- ful ox-eyed daisies, and the next stage was practically the chrysanthemum. One who could get on in the world like that,was worthy of respect,and he had there- fore great respect for the chrysanthemum. (Hear.hear.) But the chrysanthemum was also a bit of a politician. (Laughter.) If they looked at the flower, they would see that it did not consist of one single flower, as each one of those beautifully coloured " straps," was in itself a flower,and the whole thing was a collection of flowers massed together on one single stalk. He thought that the chrysanthemum was a bit of a trades unionist. (Laughter.) If they took one of those " straps" they would not think very much of it,but take a number of them together and they would find that with unity they had beauty. (Applause.) He also thought that there was another thing to be learned from the chrysanthemum. They all knew that the art of advertising had been carried to perfection in the present day. Well, the chrysanthemum had learned the art of advertising. It knew that to attract the insects to visit the flower, and take the pollen with which to fertilise other flowers,there must be plenty of colour. Insects were attracted by bright and pretty colours just as ladies and gentlemen were. (Laughter.) If they took another relation of the chrysanthemum—the common groundsell—they would then see a poor :relation of the family, which had never been properly educated and did not know its manners, and the result was that it was a very poor looking thing. But the chrysanthemum knew better and advertised itself well, like a great many politicians. (Applause.) He would now finish his sermon, and hoped that they had all enjoyed it. If they had not, he must again tell them to blame the committee for asking him a second time to give them an address. (Laughter and applause.) ' The Rev. H. BIICKTON, of Gateacre, also gave an ad- dress upon " Aphides, or plant lice."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
