      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SAD SHOOTING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SAD SHOOTING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A TRADESMAN'S SON.</p>
        <p>A shocking accident occurred on Thursday last week at the office of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s bedstead works, School Brow, whereby Percy Gandy, aged 15 years, son of Mr. John Gandy, grocer, Church-street, lost his life. The young man was talking in a jocular manner to his fellow clerks at about three o'clock and was toying with a small pocket pistol. Suddenly he said " See how I'll shoot myself," and raised the pistol to his head. He was smiling at the time, and it is thought that he accidentally pulled the trigger of the pistol which was loaded with a small cartridge. The shot, which was only about the size of a pea,entered his head near the temple, and the injuries received were so serious that the injured youth died shortly afterwards. The other clerks who, when they heard the shot, thought that the charge was from a blank cartridge, were much alarmed when they found that Gandy had been shot, and they rushed out for a doctor. Dr. Starkey Smith was soon on the spot, but he then pronounced life to be extinct. The body of the unfortunate youth was conveyed to his parents' home in Church-street, and an inquest will be held at the police station this morn- ing. The news of the sad occurrence caused much excitement in the town. It is supposed that deceased either did not know that the pistol was loaded, or that he accidentally pulled the trigger. Much sympathy is felt for the family of the deceased, as his father, Mr. John Gandy, is well- known in the town, both as a business man and as a public man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>ENTERTAINMENT AT WARRINGION.</p>
        <p>An entertainment was given in the Co-operative Ball, Sankey-street, on Thursday last week, in aid of the funds of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, Warrington Corps. There was a large attendance and the brigade would, no doubt, derive much pecuniary benefit from the proceeds. The enter- tainers for the evening, were the St. Helens Parish Church Darkies, who gave a very enjoyable pro- gramme, of humorous and sentimental songs. The artistes were :—Tambos, G. Ball, A. Lea and T. Gee; bones, W. Eccleston, J. Brownlee and P. Neville ; interlocutor,Mr. A. Leyland ; and accompanist, Mr. C. A. Newing. A humorous sketch was also cleverly taken by Fix Fiz (G. Hall), and Chin Chin (W. Eccleston), and the entertainment was brought to a termination by a screaming farce, entitled "The Black Judge." During an interval in the programme, Mr. T. Morris, F.G.S., who presided, said that he was sure they would all thoroughly understand that the St. John Ambulance Brigade was one of the finest institutions in the town of Warrington, and he was exceedingly pleased at seeing such a fine audience there that night. The funds would be considerably strengthened. The brigade could not be carried on without money. Everything that the brigade I did was done cheerfully and for the good of everybody who happened to suffer from accident either by rail or 'road. There were 50,000 inhabitants in the town of Warrington, and only about 200 holding the certificate for " first aid to the injured," and not half ot those kept themselves efficient to render assistance in time of need. There ought to be at least one in every street of War- rington, and three or four in every works. Tne doctors were willing and the Corps was willing ; some of the wealthy classes were giving their generous support, but they wanted the workinemen to come and learn how to render first aid. Speaking of medical men, he wished to mention specially, Dr. Adams. (Applause.) Dr. Adams had been very successful with his work and was well worthy all their patronage and respect. He hoped that many of those present would join the classes and thus be ready for any case of emergency which might arise. They would find it better to spend an hour at the ambulance class, than spending it anywhere else, almost better than spending it at home, for even at home first aid was required at times for scalds and burns. He concluded by proposing a vote of thanks to the surgeons, the secretary, and treasurer, and to their friends who were entertaining them that night. (Ap- plause.) The Rev. B. B. PRESCOTT, the chaplain of the corps, seconded the motion. He said that he took great interest in the St. John Ambulance work and they had a good number at Latchford who attended Dr. Adam's lectures. tie hoped that many would rally in obedience to Mr. Morris' exhortation and take a deep interest in the work. (Applause.) The motion was carried unanimously and Mr. LEY- LAND responded on behalf of th 4 minstrels. Dr. Analas also replied on behalf of the honorary surgeons of the Ambulance Association, Warrington centre. He said that in Warrington, the St. John Am- bulance Association had developed very much, and he hopedthat it would ultimately prove a very successful centre. Dr. Adams then proceeded to explain several matters with reference to the Association. He said that it was an institution to render first aid to the injured, and provided teachers and material for the classes. Ex- aminations were also held for certificates. Those who had passed the first examination were thereby entitled to become members of the brigade. Those who had passed three examinations won the medallion. The brigade was dependent entirely upon what it got. They had to charge a fee to the members, unless they were fortunate to obtain a grant from the County Council. Be was sorry to say that they had riot been able to get any grant in Warrington yet. If they coald do so, they would be in a much better position. He hoped many present would join the classes,and he invited all to come down on Thursday to the meeting room and see for themselves what was being done. (Applause.) The entertainment was then proceeded with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MR. SUSTICE DAY -5-N THE INCREASE OF PERJURY.</head>
        <p>MR. SUSTICE DAY -5-N THE INCREASE OF PERJURY.</p>
        <p>In charging the grand jury at the Lincolnshire Assizes on Saturday, Mr. Justice Day referred to two charges of perjury in the calendar, and said he was glad that endeavours were being made to check the terrible growth of perjury which prevailed in this country. Wherever he went he found that perjury was rife in the courts, and it was a mode of robbery and fraud which was practised very largely by great numbers of people. It was absolutely necessary that all persons having bccasisn to administer justice should be more diligent in the pursuit of offences of this description, and should follow the example of those judges and some magistrates, mostly judges in the county court, who had directed that proceedings should be taken for offences of that sort. , He thought if prosecutions were more frequently directed ' by presiding magistrates the crime of perjury would, perhaps, be checked. At present it seemed to be used to defeat justice in very court in the country. It was really shocking to think that persons were to be found in such large numbers who did not, from fear of punish- ment in another world, or from the consciousness that they were giving offence to their God, hesitate to call upon their Maker that they would tell the truth, when they knew that they had come to tell that which was absolutely untrue. Such persons were only kept in check by temporal punishment, and that was the only thing that such people cared about or considered. The administration of justice must be preserved pure if people were to secure their rights in courts of justice, and presiding magistrates and county court judges should take care that the persons who committed perjury should be brought to justice and adequately punished.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Portsmouth on Monday con- cerning the death of Ann (lawn, aged 22,a barmaid,who died suddenly on Sunday while visiting some friends. She had just waltzed round the room when she fell to the ground and died immediately.—Dr. Maybury at- tributed death to the rupture of a blood vessel, acceler- ated by tight lacing, and a verdict of death from natural causes was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Sunday was the day appointed by Conference for the purpose of making special pulpit references to the drink question. The pulpit at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church was occupied on that day by the Rev. W. G. Triggs,and in the evening he based his remarks upon the text, Genesis 9c. 21v., " And he drank of the wine and was drunken." He said that tiles early did they read of the ravages of strong drink. Thus early in the history of the race did drink appear as the enemy of man. It would not escape the notice Of his hearers that that was the first appearance of the thing in literature. and God would not allow it to appear in the sacred pages cloaking or dissembling itself. God would not allow it to appear there in disguise,but there stood the loathsome thing it- self with brazen face and brutal leer and the fell false- hood of hate. It had come to destroy, and there was something dramatic,tragic, and prophetic in that its first appearance. It entered with the stride of a conqueror. It did not strike at some poor weak wretched remnant of humanity,but it struck at the very head of the human race. By the way the drink was mentioned in the scriptures it warned the human race against it. It was the representative of the devil as the serpent was in Eden. The incident which the text was taken from was the second fall of man. Drink was the man-slayer; the devil in solution. Could God speak in louder terms or tones of warning, or so impressively as by the picture of Noah, the glorious saint,reduced to such a state. Up to that II me, Noah's saintliness and heroism shone out in the antediluvian world. He was a point of light in all the surrounding darkness. In the midst of the pre- vailing corruption of the antediluvian world, he was pure. He was the sole representative of the Deity in a wide, wicked, and lawless world, Throughout all the great troubles and trials of the antediluvian world, Noah's life had been beautiful, perfect, and without blemish. His life had been the life of a hero. And yet what all the forces of the antediluvian world failed to do. drink did. Drink blotted the clean lite; drink clouded the bright sky •, drink stained the soul ; drink disturbed the settled peece of this here,and he could not wipe out that blot." Noah planted a vineyard, and he drank of the wine, and was drunken. As they looked upon Noah on the floor of his tent and saw him be was not alone. That canvas tent disappeared, and there huddled together with him, were to be seen other sons of God ; prophets and priests were there ; judges and magistrates were there ; journalists and scientists were there ; authors and kings were there; frail and feeble women were there ; the cream of the human race was huddled together, lost to decency and shame—a revolt- ing spectacle. As he looked at that great host, he asked himself, could he fondle, could he touch the thing which had done that? Could he have any dealings with that cursed, loathsome thing that stole their modesty, their virtue, their honour, their chastity, and branded their names ; God forbid ! He had no denunciation for the drunkard, nor for the men who sold the drink, hut he had denunciation for the drink. He would give a few reasons for which, by God's help be intended to fight the I drink. First of all,and he would put the least reason first, it had injured himself. The second reason was that it had injured and destroyed numbers of his own friends and companions. The third reason was that he had rea- son to believe that it had injured and destroyed many thousand of his fellow men. And another, and the prin- cipal reason was that it injured and destroyed the work of the gospel and was the strongest and most deter- mined enemy that the preacher of the gospel had to contend with. Let them look at the sublety of the thing. Noah never contemplated when he planted the vineyard that he would be brought so low. No doubt be thought as many at the present time thought, that he could control himself and that he was proof against it:</p>
        <p>it-,L;w—e-ver,Proved the stronger,and Mr. Triggs appealed to all young men present not to deliberately expase themselves to danger, for it was clear that abstioenc6 was the only way of safety. Mr. Triggs then proceeied to give instances which had come under his notice per- sonally of the fell influence cf drink upon promising young men and successful tradesmen. In conclusion,he urged his hearers to be on the right side in that gigantic conflict and to strike a blow for their fellow men. Let them dash down the wine cup, and forsake the pewter pot. Let their influence ard their example tend to the salvation of men. There was no help fir tbe poor, sin- bound, drunk sodden man other than Jesus Christ, and he hoped that there all those who were bound by the in- fluence of the drink would find relief from their fetters.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P S.A. On Sunday afternoon WycliffeCo'agregational Church was filled almost to overflowing with a mixed attend ance, the occasion being the quarterly open meeting of the P.S.A. The Rev. John Yonge (president) took the chair, and the speaker was the Rev. F. G. West, of Liverpool. . Mr. WEST, in the course of a humormus and anecdotal address, mentioned that at his church the ladies were al- lowed to be present at the P.S.A., and always occupied the gallery, so that it was cuatomary for the men to say that the angels were above. (Laugh-ter.) The subject of that afternoon's address was " A religion for both Worlds." He said the question of another world was deep down in the hearts of men of every nation,even in the darkest part of Africa. Some men said the religion of Christ would not do for the workingman, but was for another world. They said that because the Bible told them to take no thought for the morrow, and a man who did that would not get on. When Scripture was quoted by open air speakers it was as well for them to know what it meant. The Bible said, "be not anxious," and that meant that they must not worry, and not that they must take no thought for the morrow, but go on quietly. Some people thought that to be religious they must become miserable. Peaple were miserable because they had not enough religion in them and too much worldliness. The P.S.A. movemnt showed that the workingman was getting a better opinion of Christ. At one time they thought that all par.ons were old women, and soft heads—(laughter)—but if they had any busi- ness to do with them they would find that they were not so soft as they thought. (Laughter and applause.) The P.S.A. movement was just scattering the falsehoods of the preachers at street corners. There was a statement made that the P.S.A. movement injured the other meet- ings. It had not done so at Liverpool, In fact, they thought it made all their meetings better. There was one man converted at their first meeting and they had had converts ever since. Wherever they turned they would find that the religion of Christ had been at the root of a large number of the blessings which were en- joyed to-day. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN announced that the membership of the P.S.A. up to date was I,llB—(applause)—and that he was endeavouring to form a temperance organisation in connection with it. The temperance cause in Warring- ton was net particularly alive,and wanted wakening up. and he did not know why they should not waken it up, and therefore they meant tp have a distinct temperance society of some kind in connection with the P.S.A. Those who had signed the pledge and those who were not total abstainers he invited to meet him after the service that night to talk the matter over. It was temperance Sunday that day "and the better the day the better the deed."</p>
        <p>Mr. WEST mentioned that at Liverpool they had tem- perance meetings every Monday and a number of P.S. A. men had signed the pledge. The weekly temperance meeting was one of the biggest blessings they had and he was pleased to say a word of encouragement for his friend, Mr. Yonge, in his new venture. During the afternoon Mrs. Pearson and Mr. W. U. Wallington sang a duet, entitled "Ever with the Lord." in a most harmonious manner. Mrs. Pearson also gave a finished and powerful rendition of" Entreat me not to leave Thee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</p>
        <p>BEACONSFIELD DIAMOND MINES,</p>
        <p>The General Manager of the Beaconsfield Mines. Kimberley, South Africa reports that there in already an accumulation of over (25,000) twenty-fivethousand loads of blue ground on the floors and work is being done which will increase the quantity undergoing the pro- cess of drying and maturing. a process which greatly adds to the returns of diamonds and the dividends. among recent rich finds at the Beaconsfield Mines have been stones sold for £l,OOO, £5OO and £3OO respectively. The Chairman in his speech stated that the deeper the mines were worked the greater became the number of diamonds found and the finer their quality. The value of the diamonds in the ground in hand is estimated at over £BO,OOO and the cash returns already received are sufficient to pay a dividend of no less than (40) forty per cent. The total capital of the company is only £85,000 and of this, £70,000 has been issued,the balance being reserved for additional working capital if required. Owing to the solid position of the company these shares of £1 each, fully paid, are now worth as much, even more than they were two months ago when they were selling at more than double the price at which they can now be bought on the London Stock Exchange. The shrewd investor who has the cash in hand to pay for and take them up can now purchase the shares at £1 ss. each, at which price they are a great bargain, taking into consideration the fact that the profits of the past six months will return to the shareholders nearly half their capital. According to the latest efficial returns the net profit fd. the four weeks just ended has been £1,300 not including the larger diamonds found. The Beaconsfield Company also owns some very valuable gold claims which form a valuable addition to the re- sources and assets of this successful enterprise. A rapid recovery in the price of these shares is sure to take place.</p>
