      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>INDIGESTION</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION</p>
        <p>THE whole medical profession theo world over is engaged in an earnest search for some remedy which will cure Indigestion, Constipation, and Dyspepsia. Statistics show that nearly half the deaths in civilized countries are caused by diseases of the stomach and kidneys. Hitherto the reme.liee prescribed by the medical profession for Indigcstion have 1 eon of little value, but the C. A. Vogeler Curative Compound does cure Indigestion. It is ' the most wonderful medicine in existence, a few doses gives relief— a few bottles cure the most' chronic cases. A trial bottle will convince the sufferer that we make none but true statements. Price 14% and 2,6, of all Chemists; or sent free by Parcels Post on receipt of 14 or 30 stamps by the Sole Proprietors, The Charles A. Vogeler Co., 45, Farringdon Road, London:"</p>
        <p>•1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH',</head>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH',</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MISSIONARY MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual missionary meeting was held at the above church on Monday. On the previees day. the Rev. W. J. A. Barber, of China, preached sermons suitable to the occasion to good congregations. There was also a good number present on Monday even- ing,when the chairman was Mr. J. Champness,of South- port. There were also present the Revs. W. J. A. Barber, Owen Watkins, G. D. Mason, and W. G. Triggs and Mr. John Bennett. The annual report of the Mis- sionary S.ttciety was given be the Rev.G. D. Mason,from which it appeared that £2lB Oa. Id. had been contri- buted from the Warrington circuit, and £l7 3s. 21. from Bewsey-road Chureh. He said that he hoped this year that there would be good collections at Bewsey-road Church, as there had been increases all over the circuit where there had been missionary meetings already held. The CHAIRMAN said that he thought when he received the invitation to come to Warrington that he would be poaching on their preserves. (Laughter.) He felt that they had in th.-ir midst zentlemen who were entitled to the position, who ought not to have been allowed to escape. He saw one of them on his right hand (Mr. Bennett). At his circuit at Southbank-street, in South. bank, they adopted the rule of having a local man for the chairman, and he recommended a similar rule in Bewsey-road. He was sure that they were pleased with the report. The tide had turned and they were not gn- ing to have any decreases for some time to come. The tone had also improved, and he was glad to see such a lgood meeting there that night, an I he was sure that they would not allow it to be said that Bewsey-road was the only place in the circuit to be" down." The Rev. W. J. A. BARBER then addressed the meet- ing, in Chinese costume. He said the country he repre- sented had been very much before the people of this country for the last year or two. Although war as a terrible thing God knew haw to use it for his purpose, and if ever a nation deserved the punishment she had received it was Cairn.. There were two sets of ideas about China. He would that night speak about the war and the riots with regard to the Chris- tian religion. China had had magnificent ideas in the past,but the worst was that they might have splendid ideas but not bs able to put them in practice. They had the idea that knowledge was power when we were savages. They valued education, and thus founded the examications of all positions in the public service. Mr. Barber then proceeded to describe the mode of examina- tion by which the governing Classes of China were formed. He said that the system of government made it impossible for a mandarin or governor to be honest,land as this had been going 0,1 for hundreds) of years, some idea could be formed of the morality Of the.. nation. The soldiers of the country were of similar calibre,and the leaders made as much as they could out of the public funds. The system was corrupt to the bottom. China deserved everything she got by the im- morality of her G overnment, and had led to her own degradation. With regard to the effect of the war upon missions, Mr. Barber said that he was not going to pro- phesy. Ha, however, mentioned that copies of the Bible had been received by the Emperor and the Em- press Dowager. The large indemnity which had been demanded by Japan had caused China to be more open to Western ideas, and the missionary who spoke the language and lived with the penple was the first man the Chinese turned to for advic,. H thereLtre, saw great possibilities for the Christian religion, and thus there were new chances for the gospel of the Lord Jesus Christ to enter into the nation. It might be asked, " Well, what about thesi rio s?" That was a fair question. His reply was that the riots were caused by litterati and mandarins, owing to the fact that there were so many new ideas introduced, and thus they formed a scheme of sending the missionaries out of the country. They first began by setting out horrible stories about the mission- aries,and this laid the train by which In time the terrible massacres were caused. Was this a hopeless prospect ? It might be from a human standpoint, but it was not hopeless in the eves of any Chinese missionary. He had seen too, many wonderful instances of the victories of Christian religion in Chico, to doubt the ultimate vic- tory. . . .</p>
        <p>The meeting Wa.4 brought to a termination with the usual votes of thanks proposed by the Rev. W. G. TRIGG9, and seconded by Mr. Joni.' BENNETT. During tha course of the meeting, Mr. James Bennett sang the well-known piece from Flandel's oratorio,Judas Maccabreus, " Arm, arm, ye brave."</p>
