      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1895.</p>
        <p>IE LIFE W OETH LIVING</p>
        <p>IN HELL</p>
        <p>Such was the subject of a celebrated sermon preached in Manshester,but the more practical problem of the day is how to make life worth living on earth. One of the important tri- butaries of the river of happiness is the possession of a com- fortable home. A home made bright and cheertul not only by the kind and loving dispositions of those living there, but made attractive by the good taste displayed In the furnishing. Furnishing is a very important factor in the matter of con- tinued self-satisfaction. So much jerry new London furniture is being now retailed by drapers that one needs to pause be- , fore rushing impetuously into the first draper's because of a I cheap red ticket. High-class makers, such as Lamb,Gillows, and Garnett,still exist, to whom the wealthy can still go with a long purse but to most people this would be a too expensive experiment. Again, the average man nowadays has ex strong liking for a bargain ; anything obtainable at about half or quarter the cost has a strong fascination f,r him. Is it possi- ble to obtain such an advantage In furnishing P Yes, and more than possible, it is an absolute fact. We cannot do better than recommend a visit to MR. WORSLEY'd FUR- NISHING- PANTHEON, GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>frllld Successful Establishment of any of the large ware- -1 houses of Manchester is always of interest to the citizens. One of the most brilliant examples of the value of perseverance, enterptise, and tact Is the now busy hive of industry GO well known as The Pantheon, which occupies such a prominent position in Gaythorn, near the Central Station, Blanchester. The building is a massive pile of modern design, 150 feet long, six storeys high, with show- room accommodation covering an area of over one million square feet. The plate-glass windows are each 12 feet square, each square if broken costing nearly £3O to replace. ten of these immense show windows form a portion of the frontage. This will give a faint idea of "The Pantheon" in outward appearance, but Rh l—here pen fails altogether in attempting to do justice to anything like an adequate description of the Interior. "The Pantheon" was specially built to give the largest and most convenient floor space for showrooms, and cost £28,000, the floors specially constructed tocarry enormous weight. To-day it stands as one of the smartest furnishing houses in England, containing by far the largest and most comprehensive stock of any two houses in this large city. Prom basement to roof, frorn floor to cei ling, "The Pantheon" is filled to overflowing with thousands of well bought, care- fully selected bargains, to attempt description of which would be as futile as Liverpool attempting to crush</p>
        <p>THE MANCHESTBR PULP CANAL. The destinies of' The Pantheon" are c ritrolled and pre- sided over by its sole absolute proprietor, Mr. W. H. Worsley, whose sole study is an ever increasing desire to satisfy and please the thousands of customers who daily patronise and support his efforts. We invite the reader to accenspany us in an imaginary visit to " The Pantheon to see what it con- tains and how it is managed. . .</p>
        <p>On arrival we are at once etruak with the magnificent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>STARTLING. APPRARANCR</head>
        <p>STARTLING. APPRARANCR</p>
        <p>_ of " The Great Pattheon," the immenie long line of stupen- dous show windows displaying for sale capital specimens of high-class Secondhand Furniture Oy high-class makers, the sale of which has made " The Pantheon famous.</p>
        <p>UPON ENTERING THE BUILDING we see at once that as far as stock is concerned there is sufficient furniture for nearly the whole of Manchester. We sea at once visitors walking round inspecting from Oldham, Bolton. Bury, Burnley, Birckburn,Bacup. Rochdale. Preston, Warrington and all parts of Lanca‘bire and Yorkshire, Cheshire, the Potteries. Wales, Sm. It is the cardinal rule of " The Pantheon '' to give the fullest facilities for inspec- tion without feeling compelled to bur. The ground floor is filled with the large specimens of bedroom furniture suitable for large rooms ; we are told the smaller suites are on the top floor, to which we can travel by means of the hoist, or walk. Bedroom suites on the ground floor we notice by Lamb. original cost £l5O, price £35 ; by Goodall, cost £llO, for £5O ; by Henry Ogden. cost £2OO. for £65. These are among the best suites, but one that strikes Us with special attention is a fine handsome Bedroom Suite by Behan. in solid American walnut. heavily cured in bets relief, with large wardrobe fitted with brilliant crystal bevel mirrors ; dressing table unique with neatly fitted jewel drawers, sur- mounted by large swing dressing glass ; washstand fitted with statuary marble and shaving mirror. three chairs, pedestal table de nuit. and towel horse ; what originally cost £6O you can obtain for £25. Over 200 Kedroom Suites are in stq.:k. on shew, from 355. to .2100, forming a display so impressive that weare almost</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>STRUCK DUMB WITH AMAZEMENT.</head>
        <p>STRUCK DUMB WITH AMAZEMENT.</p>
        <p>We now go up stairs to the Dining-room Showroom. Wkat a sight, long avenues of Sideboards Is the first impression ; hundreds of Sideboards, with hundreds of Suites, also Dining Tables, Overrnantels, splendid collection of Bronzes, Ormolu Clocks, Vases, etc. In this department we find ntagnificent Sideboards, original cost from £lOO to £2OO. at prices simply astounding, £lO to £2O. The next floor above is the Drawing room Furniture Showroom, which is simply a show of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ZZT T'vG BRAUTY</head>
        <p>ZZT T'vG BRAUTY</p>
        <p>dissolving one's sensitive appre3lation into impressive lovell- nese. No department is interesting to la,ies as the draw- ing-room showroom; a keen delight is experienced la selecting weird and, fantastic furniture for the drawing-room. /Esthetic, Moorish, and Bgyptian Cabinets, peculiar Green and nronzed Tables, Occasional Chairs of charming variety, Cosy Corners, Comfortable Lounging Settees and chairs equal to the highest class firms in the Kingdom at quarter the price. The largest showroom for Drawing-room Yurniture in Manchester, containing over 100 Suites, 100 Cabinets, 200 Overmanteis, 500 Tables. suites from £3 10s. Crt. to £.50. Cabinets from 30s. to £45. Marvellous value, worth in most cases three or four times the money charged. The next department adjoining is cases three or four times the money changed. The next de- partment is the Be istead rnd Deridng Department, which contains an enormous stock of Be.isteads. The brllhant- burnished brass displayed here is so immense as to present , almost a</p>
        <p>SEA OP GOLD. Magnificent speeimens of the brassworkers' art is Lem die played to perfection. Dress Bedsteads may be purchased solid and complete for an low a price as 355. to the best at £35. A clear saving of 50 per cent. it. effected In bedsteads and bedding. Brass and Iron Bedsteads, strong and durable, m,y be secured from 6s. fd. to 30s. The bedding is all new, manufactured on the premises everything is . new in this department. tlae work Isere giving employment to over 50 hands. Nothing Is allowed to be used here except it has pre- viously been washed and petrified. All bedding sold at IVor- sley's may be guaranteed as of SPOTLRSS PURITY.</p>
        <p>Wool Beds complete, gmwanteed all wool, tram Bs. ed., Hair Mattresses from 18s. 6d.. Feather Beds from 225. 6d. The next place of interest to visit is the Bedroom Vurniture Department, which is displayed In a showroom. 68 yards by 25 yards. larger then most of the combined showrooms of most furnishing estealishments. One is bound to admit that The Pantheon is certainly far ahead of any rival establish- ments in point of size and convenience. The showrtons visited so far are lofty, well lighted, and of immense area. The Bedroom F-rniture Showroom is magnificent ; 300 Bedroom Suites are 'lsplayed In avenues, the finest collec- tion extant, at prices simply incredible, astonishing every one. pulverising and exterminating all competition. Com- plete Bedroom Suites, solid throughout, at b". 3 10s. up to £3O, magnificent in style and finiso. Enamelled Suites trom 30s. to £7. The next departmeiit is for Kitalien Furniture, the varied stock of which Is quite sufficient to satisfy the most exacting. Thousands of chairs; hundreds of tables sad dressers, every requisite for the kitchen at half the usual prices charged. A department is also provided for Iron- mongery. But we must move on, our examination and in- specthas is taking to long and occupying ton much spsce. We now are shown into the Carpet and Oilcloth Department, which contains thousands of new rolls of carpets, Brussels from ls. Hid , Tapestry from 100.. Axminster from 28.1101 and so on. ;hen we see hundreds of Second-hand Carpets. If you want a carpet to beat creation, buy one of Worsley's second-hand carpets ; they knock the competition of Jew and Gentile into smithereens. You can get a second-hand Brussels carpet, no tapestry ; no, real. genidue Brussels, fr, m 4 to 5 yards by 3to 4 yard,. for under a sovereign. Hugs of im- mense vArlety, lovely Skin Hugs, Axminster, in fact of every conceivable design, shade, and colour, forming a gorgeous display. RIVALLING THE RAINBOW.</p>
        <p>Oilcloths and Ltneleums are here out to pieces; thousands of yards are here daily slashed end cut into all shapes to tit the various houses they are intended for. The prices are also cut., In fact, cut so fine that you would require a magnifying glass to discover any profit made out of this department. We saw Oilcloths from lid. per 'vs d and Linoleums from 6d.per yard. Ihe best Scotch Oilcloths and Llnole ms are displared from ls. 3d. per yardito 2s. 11d. We should like to ,peak of all Use w,nderful things displayed for sale, but we must be pre- paring to le-tve The Pantheon in order to catch our train. We, therefore, bu,ry throng': the Office Furniture Deportment, the Brass Feeder and lirebrass Department, the Miscellane- ous Department quickiy on to the latest addition, the Music Department. Hark I What do we hear?</p>
        <p>ZfrprieS, tagstnettai, earriageO,</p>
        <p>artmentg, ,ffurniObrb 3Pousto, Brt.</p>
        <p>COLWYN and NORTH WALES COAST. For Furnished houses to let, apply to V. DEW, Land Agent, Colwyn Bay.</p>
        <p>BLACKPOOL.—Perfons recruiting health, comfortable APARTMENTS; reduced winter terms ; bath, piano.— Mug. BALL, 49, Woodfield-road, South Shore.</p>
        <p>IlusStral inaruments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>mt7SIC. HHAVNNLT AND SUBLIMB.</head>
        <p>mt7SIC. HHAVNNLT AND SUBLIMB.</p>
        <p>The " Hallelujah Chorus, Handers masteralece, is being rolled along in glorious harmony by a gentleman who tales Iva dlseoyered to be" The Proprietor." American Organ are here displayed In creak variety, mostly seoond-hand : for I.ga/we, a Mason and Hamelin organ can be purchased for 10 guineas which cost £4O. The pianofortes are in strong evidence, and a great Credit to Mr. Worsley, who buys everything for " The Pantheon' himself. Pialloll by Brard. Steinway, Broadwood, B‘chstein, Justin Browne, Col- lard, Brinsmead. and all the best makers are displayed at prices simply amazing. limed pianos, the king of all in- struments, are priced at £l5, £2O, and £2B. Marvellous value. Who would boy a cheap German piano which in 12 cubits would be worie than a Jew's harp, when you can obtain a fine second-hand piano by one of the best makers for a fourth of the original price ? New pianos by all the best makers are here 6(3.0 for a profit of 10 per cent ; Wore- ley's will procure any instrument, bysiny maker, for is simple 10 r.er cent profit, and pay carriage. Neumeyer pianos, quite new. op endid pianos, for 281 guineas, list price 50 gn, neat; ; Kirkman's steel upright piano• £33, list £6O Brinsmead's £3O, Collard's £3J, Hopkinson's £25, and so on. showing a marvellous saving on the ordinary InftSie. sellers' price. Magnificent second-hand pianos at £3, £O, £5, up to £lO, wonderful bargains.</p>
        <p>IN CONCLUSION. we find that "The Pantheon' is undoubtedly three times the size of any other furnishing house in Manchester ;We have visited every rival establishment, but none can hold a candle to "The Pantheon." The universal courtesy extended to every visitor, whether a buyer or simply inspecting, is a strung recommendation to all to pay a visit to "The Patheon" before purchasing furniture. And Although A terrific cash trade Is daily in evidence, yet Mr. Worsley is such a strong opponent be the iniquitous hire system that to combat these evils, which mean a serious loss to those starting in life (see "Tit Bits," for October 12th). Worsley's now give credit be all respectable buyers at exactly the same price charged for cash, but make a direct charge of 10 per cent above the invoice price. This is a boon, preventing thi extortion- ate prices usually charged on the hire system. Now our visit is ends I, and after thanking the saleman for his courtesy in showing us round, we cannot help but marvel, As we wend our way to the station. upon the marvellous skill and ability displayed at" Tire Pantheon" in the building up of what pro- phetically promises to become one of the largest concerns in England. DON'T BUY FURNITURE anywhere until you have visited "The Pantheon," Gaythorn, Manchester, near the Central Station.</p>
        <p>WH. WOKSLEY, The Pantheon, Gaythorn, • Mauebester. Close to Central ntation. HOURS OF BUSIZIESS EVERY DAY 9 to 7.</p>
        <p>' ,„ -.2'</p>
        <p>,o</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Amlo bp Zurtcan.</head>
        <p>Amlo bp Zurtcan.</p>
        <p>Eibc--Rptork, &amp;r.</p>
        <p>SUPERIOR SECOND-HAND FURNITURE, by all the noted and very beat makers, removed from halls, man- sions, hotels, offices, private houses of every description, and which uudergo a thorough examination and ate placed in condition equal to new. I may here remark that the stock I offer is considerably larger than at any other house in the city, and is not being placed in the market because it is soiled or damaged in any manner; but am pleased to state that in consequence of the great increase in my trade, and the large number of orders I have to deliver every week, I find it ab- solutely necessary and therefore decided to make extensive alterations which will incur an outlay of some hundreds of pounds. And have arranged with the abntractors to clear the showrooms previous to the workmen commencing opera- tions, and in order to do so I have decided to give my cus- tomers the benefit of the enormous reductions rather than store the stock. which embraces furniture of every descrip- tion ; in fact, such au assortment of bedroom suites, dining- room suices, drawing-room suites, office furniture, bookcases. desks, sideboards, dinner waggons, cabinets, overmantels, davenports, card, work and fancy tables, carpets, rugs, bed- steads, bedding, fender, pianos, and every article for com- pletely furnishing the mansion or cottage. Can nowhere be seen except at GERSHON'S Universal Furnishing Arcade, 44, Oldham-st, In fact, when the alterations are completed. which will consist of</p>
        <p>NEW OFFICES, NEW COUNTING HOUSE, NEW DINING SALOON for the employes,</p>
        <p>NEW REFRESHMENT-ROOM for convenience Of customers NEW CARPET SALOON, NEW SHOWRO9MS ;</p>
        <p>, And other internal Alterations, all local and outside shops I will sink to insignificance.</p>
        <p>following will convey but a faint idea of the Mar- l_ vellous Bargains on v ew, any of which may be stored free of charge till required. TERMS BUBINESS DURING THE SALE,</p>
        <p>GOODS EXCHANGhD IF NOT SATISFACTORY.</p>
        <p>EXPERIENCED MEN SENT TO F-IX-Xiiii-iiTiii.. GOODS PACKED AND DELIVERED FREE. CARPETS MADE FREE.</p>
        <p>LOCK-UP STORE ROOMS FUR CONVENIENCE OF CUSTOMERS. _</p>
        <p>And, lastly, If you pureh.ase.on my Deferred Payment System</p>
        <p>Cliarie a Simple Five per Cent Ex.it a.</p>
        <p>Every customer attended to by a member of the firm. which prevents the annoyance of a 6alesman who charges high prices in order to make a big commission. . .. „ -</p>
        <p>Bankers—Manchester Joint-Stock Bank. Telegrams—"Univerpal," Manchester.</p>
        <p>DURING the Sale, Magnificent Solid Oak DINING- ROOM SUITE, comprising a grand couch, two noble easy chairs, and six superior spoon-back chairs, all in splendid condition, not soiled; originally cos; £55, sale price 9gs. Grand Sideboard to match, 7gs., worth £4O; Dining Table to match. 635. _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>In addition to above, we have a truly magnificent Col- lection of Dining-room Suites, over 200, with Sideboards, Dining Tables, and Overmantels to match. An inspection will prove the fact that nowhere can such an assortment be seen except at GERSHON'S, the Universal Furnishing Arcade, 44, Oldham-street, next to the Ade(pill Bank, Man- chester.</p>
        <p>Note.—The above may be had on my deferred payment evatem for a simple five per mot extra added to invoice. QPRCIAL ATTENTIuN to Lot 166. which is In Pollard Oak kJ and without a ciiiubt the best and handsomest lot in , Manirhester. The Sidebosrd, which is 7rt. long and very lofty, can only be desetibed as simply magnificent and the most unique in design we have ever seen. The Suite of 12 Ismail chairs (may be had with a less number), two large I Lounge Chairs, and large Couch, are all stuffed with hair and I covered in morocco leather, guaranteed. The Dining-table, on carved supports, is very hsndsome, having a number of loose leaves. We offer the lot for 145 gs. ; cost over £4OQ. We may state that figs. each will be allow est for any of the small chairs not required. We wish to impress upon the public that every lot advertised is on our premises and hundreds of others. Remember address, ORRSHON'S Old-established Universal Furnishing Arcade, 44, Oldham-street (next to the Adelphi Bank), Manchester. Note.—lf on the deterred payment system only five per cent, extra added to invoice.</p>
        <p>LOT 237 is In Oriental Saddle Bags, carved walnut frames. consisting of half-dozen small chairs, two easy chairs, and couch; sriee £25, re.tuceol for the sale to £9 155., Side- board to match, with bevelled-glass back, a perfect beauty; sale price, £6 155.. usual price 12gs. Dining Table, 38e. 6d. Three other Suites in Saddle Bags, real Persian, everlasting were, splendid patterns, among which Is a water lily, most charming and pleasing ; the wood work in these suites is American walaut, brown oak, an mahogany, and the pi ices are 18, 25, and 36gs. ; they each cost £lO, £l5, and £2sa suite more. Sideboards Tables, and Overmantels to match, all on view in our showroom;. Hundreds of other Suites on view. Call and Inspect for yourselves ; you will not be un- duly pressed to buy. We have no salesmen or walking gen- tlemen who work for a commission. Every customer is attended to by a member of the firm at GERSHON'S Univer- sal Furnishing Arcade, 44, Oldham-street (next to Adelphi Bank), Manchester. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>Noe.-1f on the deferred payment system only five per cent. extra added to invoice.</p>
        <p>097 11 a Charming DINING-ROOM SUITE, in embosseel 1i leather, very handsome and stylish, frame work beauti- fully carved; the six standard chtirs have padded backs, the two easy chairs are large amid comfortable, and the couch is most luxuriously upholstered. This suite is In solid American walnutwood and is suitab e for high-class dining-room. Usual price, 255. ; sale price thge. Another Suite, in Solid Mahoony. in good hard grain leather. Cromwellian style ; sound in every respect; not soiled. Recently been recovered. &amp;marvel of cheapness ; only 9gs. Another in Walnut, coy, red in leather, reduced to sgs. Sideboard, to match, 42'. ; tab'e, 21s. These bargains are on offer during the sale and before alterations are commenced. Inspection invited.</p>
        <p>SWEEPING isEDUCTIONS. MUST CLEAR PREVIOUS TO ALTERATIONS. LOT 51 is a BEDROOM SUITE. Those wishing to furnish a bedroom in high-class style and good taste should inspect it. for style, workmanship, and design it cannot be equalled, the massive large size wardrobe with two bevelled glass doors of rare brilliancy reaching from plinth to cornice; the full-size pedestal dressing table, double washstand to match, pedestal bedside cupboard, towel airer, and three chairs, the whole are in rosewood, richly inlaid with tulip, box, olive, and other rare wooJs. T 1 e cost of this noble Suite was £l5O. bale price sFgs. Another Suite in walnut cost £125 tor 60gs ,end another in Spanish mshogany cost £55 for 27gs. Special attention is requested to Lots 6,7, Band 9, which are Suites in walnut, silver birch, mahogany, and ash, each Suite hasa large wardrobe, with bevelled glass door dressing table with large b yelled glass attached, supported by carved brackets restingon raised jewel drawers; it also forms a chest 0' drawers. being enclosed by four drawers; the large wash staxd is also enclosed, and has a marble slab, tile back, and bevelled shaving glass above, towel rail, pedestal beside cunbosrd, and •three chairs. These suite ale guaranteed solid, no veneer being used. Side price, 15igs. Otkv r suites smaller from lbs. Inspection Invited. Any of the above may be stored free of charge till required. GERSHON'S NOTED UNIVERSAL FURNISHING ARCADE, _</p>
        <p>44:OLDHAM STREET (Next to Adelptd Bank),. MANCP &amp;STEIL Note.—lf on the deferred pa3ment ystem only 5 per cent. extra added to invoice.</p>
        <p>MUST CLEAR fer the Contractors. Such a chance seldom occurs.</p>
        <p>STUPENDOUS BARGAINS•</p>
        <p>We now enter the Miscellaneous Department. Here we can inspect a splendid assortment of second-hand hair-seated Furniture. A first class solid mahogany sofa 505,, cost per- haps £6 ; another less in size 38s. Spanish Mahogany couch. in hair seating, 47s ; ditto 635., cost £6. Very fine solid Spanish mahogany sofa. very heavy and massive, bean re- upholstere 1. cost when new £B. for four guineas. Others in American leather from 16s. Now we see over 50 pairs of easy chairs in stock. Several gents' easy chairs at 15s. each, mahogany easy chair, in hair, for 285.. cost 60s. Superior pair of cromwell easy chairs, large and massive, cost £B, for four guineas the pair. Nothing better can be made. Lady's rocking chair, in hair, 283. A very large quantity et hal:- seated chairs from 5a., ach to 245. each. An enormous stock of kitchen furniture is here displayed at marvellously low prices. For an instance. strong kitchen chairs 2s. each,kitchen tables fra,rn 6s. 6d eaelt ; gr ,nrl Spanish mahogany, dresser, with high carved back, cost £l4, for 6 guineas ; another. cost £ll. for 5 guineas ; painted dresser, with thick top, 28s. ; another, very handsome high back, 60s. ; and others at all prices at ERSHON'S</p>
        <p>UNIVBBSAL FUINISIHN7FA—it.oADR, 44, OLDHAM-ST MANCHPSTHR. (Next to he Adelphl Bank.)</p>
        <p>T E GREATEST SALE ON EARTH DON'T MESS IT. Marvellous Complete House ef Furniture for 24 guineas, or Five per Cent. Extra on my Deferred Payment Sye.tena. CALL AND SES IT. All Competitor 4 stagger. Read It. See it.</p>
        <p>NUMBER 1, 1- the marvellous complete House of Furniture. wkich includes dining-room, bedroom, kitchen. all bedding, blmkets, sheets, pillow-cases carpets, floorc'oths, fenders. tea and breakfast set curtains, and everything to make a niers home. Every article is guaranteed Others advertibed a so-c.died house of furniture tor a trifle lees. I contend this lot is hone.tly worth £lO more than those advertised by others. My price for the complete house is 24 guineas. or fiVe p-r cent es tra for credit --which mean s 255. more. You need nit be afrent to call and inspect them. If they are not what you want, don't purchaSs.</p>
        <p>GERSHON, next to the ADELPIII BANK, 44, o DJIAM-STREET.</p>
