      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>AS"WOCHR POR McDOTTGALL'S PATENT SELFR A ISING FLOUR THE EXAMINER SATUR! AY. NOVEMBER 30. 1895.</p>
        <p>troversy but in that he was greatly mistaken. It is an influential party among his own supporters who are hastening this crisis, which, when it comes, may easily have a disastrous effect upon the Unionist party.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS' SUPERANNUATION.</p>
        <p>Sir John Bru;;;;,-M—.Ps..,-rePl-yTr;i.o-reriolution passed by the Runcorn and Widnes Teachers' Association, pro- mises his hearty support to any carefully considered pro- posal for State-aided pensions for teachers. Mr.lliat, M.P., says he will have much pleasure in devoting his best efforts to this good aim. RUNCORN TENNIS CLUB.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER is making an ener- The Canal. getic attempt to capture the Aus- tralian meat trade. On Tuesday a banquet was held to celebrate the arrival by way of the Ship Canal of the first direct shipment of frozen meat and other colonial prOduce, at which some encouraging and hopeful things were said. Mr. J. K. BYTHELL, the chairman of the Ship Canal Company, expressed the opinion that the voyage of the Timaru—the vessel which brought the colonial products to Manchester— might be regarded as emblematic of the fortune 3 of the Ship Canal. The vessel made a good start from the other side of the world, but soon there began a period of suspense and anxiety. Nothing was heard of her for a considerable time, and many people were beginning to form the conclusion that she would never reach her destination. After a while the cloud of anxiety was dispelled and the "good ship Timaru" safely arrived in Manchester docks. She had in the course of her passage been severely buffeted by the wind and the waves, but in the end she rode triumphantly into a quiet harbour. It was under the circumstances perfectly natural for Mr. BYTHELL to draw a comparison between the voyage of the Timaru and the fortunes of the great undertaking at the head of which he has been placed. The canal was opened amidst bright predictions of success but it has had to encounter some severe waves of financial depression, and, unfortunately, it has not yet, like the Timaru, reached a safe anchorage. But apart from the more or less unreal and unprofitable analogies of this kind, it is quite certain that the Australian trade is worth culti- vating and is being cultivated with some success by the Manchester importers. The Australian exports—of frozen meat in particular—are rapidly increacing, and the colonial authorities seem to be desirous of making Manchester a chief centre of their trade. This is shown by the fact that a steamer will shortly be despatched from Melbourne to inaugurate a direct trade between that port and Ma nob ester, and this vessel, it is announced, will cont.in amongst its cargo five hundred carcases of mutton sent by the Victorian Minister of Agriculture to the Mayor of Manchester for distribution amongst the poor of the city. On the whole it now seems fairly car tam n that Manchester has got something more than the thin edge of the wedge into the traffic between the mother country and her Antipodean colonies, and it is to be re- membered that that is a traffic which will go on increasing at a very rapid rate.</p>
        <p>The first socialia:Ch-e;ing-licc-onnntion with this club was held on Friday evening in the National Schools, Church-street. Between Ms+ and sixty prsons were present. Dancing was kept up with zest until the fol- lowing morning. Mr. A. Grounds contributed several songs, and altogether the gathering was a success. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS.</p>
        <p>_ There was only a small attendance at the meeting of this class on Wednesday evening. The programme con- sisted of the reading by a number of the members of " The Combat," in Scott's " Lady of the Lake." An interesting time was spent. Mr. T. Simister occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>By the death of the Rev. Peter Mackenzie, the Wesleyan body loses a minister who will be much missed :a!1 the country over. Mr. Mac- kenzie was, we should think, the most popu- lar of contemporary lecturers. He could always be relied upon to draw a large audience, and, what is more, the audience was always well pleased with the lecturer. Mr. Mackenzie's popu- larity is shown by the fact that his list of lectures were filled up for nearly three years in advance. His style as a lecturer was peculiarly his own. It wan, in the opinion of some people, occasionally tinged with coarseness and exaggeration, but it is quite certain that the oftener he was heard the better he was liked. This was due to the fact that the style was natural to the man. If Mr. Mackenzie had ",spoken and acted in any way different from what he did he would have been affected and unnatural, and would certainly never have enjoyed the boundless popularity that be won</p>
        <p>Another victim has been added to the already lone list of fatalities due to the incautious handling of firearms, and what makes the occurrence more deplorable is the fact that in this case the third son of a well-known and respected Warrington tradesman—Mr. John Gandy—has lost his life. No unusual circumstance appears to be attached to the tragedy as the deceased, Percy Gandy, 16 years of age, was toying with a small revolver at his place of employment and playfully put it to his bead, when the weapon exploded, fatally wounding him. The sad experience of others does not, unfortun- ately, seem to deter youths from posiessing and carelessly handling weapons which are so deadly. This deplorable accident gives still further point to the arguments for an Act of Parliament restricting the sale and use of firearms. If such an Act was placed upon the statute book there would be much less likelihood of parents being suddenly plunged into mourning by the sudden loss of a promising son. Mr. and Mrs. Gandy have our deep sympathy in their bereavement and also that of the. towns- people generally,</p>
      </div>
