      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>FORTHCOIIiNG TEMPZIIA.NCE MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting in connection with the Runcorn Circuit Wesleyan Temperance Society will be held on Monday evening in St. Paul's Chapel. Addresses will be delivered by the Rev. Sampson Weaver, temperance secretary of the Liverpool District, Rev. 'rhomas Brack- enbury, and the Rev. C. de Patron Gliddon. The Chair- man will be Mr. John Simpson. RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB "SMOKER."</p>
        <p>A very jolly evening was spent by the members of this club at their headquarters on Wednesday evening when between sixty and seventy were present at the " smoker." Songs were contributed by Messrs. Shinglar, Parry, Clare, F. Lewis, E. Harrison, 0. Whiny:ates, .J. Parker, J. Ayres, and T. Hunt. Mr. J. Ayres, jun.,gave a capital ventriloquial entertainment. BILLIARD MATCH.</p>
        <p>A billiard match was played between the members of the Runcorn Volunteer Social Club and a team selected by Mr. H. Golborne, of Liverpool. Several very inter- esting games were witnessed,and in one or two instances the contests were of an exciting nature. The vi9itors, however, secured a victory by 1,318 points to 1,088.</p>
        <p>METHODIST FREE CHURCH, ELLESMERE-STREET. ThO annual sermons in connection with this church were preached on Sunday by the Rev. C. E. Yates, student of Free Church College, Manchester. Special music was rendered, the vocalists being Mrs. Newall, Mrs. Stubbs, Misses Guy, Cousins, and firilford, and Mess.s. Wesley Cooke Joseph Newall, and J. flolford. Mr. John Jones presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>--- In many places of worship in Runcorn on Sunday, Tsmperance Sunday was observed, and sermons with special references to temperance were preached. A large number of temperance friends and members of the Rechabite tents, attended a special service held in St. Paul's Church in the afternoon, when a sermon was preached by the Rev. C. de P. Gliddon, from the words "Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself." These WAS a good congregation. The minister and brethren present wore their regalia. Mr. Morris sang a solo. CAMDEN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE. . .</p>
        <p>A very successful and well attended entertainment in connection with the above mentioned band of hope was held on Monday evening. Mr. J. B. Clarke occupied the chair. The programme consisted of glees by a choir; songs by Miss J. Woodward, and Mr. J. Bazley ; recita- tions br Messrs. Arthar Worrall.F.Brown,and J.Britland. Ewen Holt contributed a pianoforte solo, and J. Ratcliffe read a piece entitled " Lecture on Medicine." A chil- dren's quartette party consisting of Bessie Littler,Walter Littler, Nellie Temperley. and 'John Hardy sang very sweetly " Footsteps in the Snow." A dialogue was well rendered by eight members. Miss Ternperley acted as accompanist during the evening. DROWNING OF A SEAMAN OF THE "EMILY MILLINGTON.? On Tuesday an inquest was held in London relative to the death ef John Welch, aged twenty, a seaman on board the "Emily Millington," of Runcorn. It appears that deceased was helping to make the schooner fast in the Victoria Dock, London, on Saturday, when he fell into the water and was drowned. It transpired at the inquest that Welch who belonged to Flint, could not swim, and four of the crew who gave evidence confessed , themselves in the same position.—The Coroner said , every sailor should be compelled to learn to swini.—The jury was of the same opinion.—A verdict of "Acciden- tal Death" was returned. SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, RUNCORN. There was a large attendance at the weekly entertain- ment provided at the above-mentioned Institute on ' Tuesday evening. Mr. Shaw (missionary) presided. A miscellaneous programme was submitted. Songs were given in a most creditable manner by Messrs. Temple, Orme, T. Wood, J. Shaw, Kinsey,Miss Temple and Mrs. Shaw. A humorous reading by Mr. Waterworth was much enjoyed. Miss Marsh recited,and Miss Shaw per- formed a pianoforte solo. Duets were well given by the Misses Temple, and Mr. Crossley and Mr. Temple. A number of sailors also contributed songs. The accom- panists were Mr. Foster and Mr. Crossley. A vote of thanks was paseed to those who bad rendered assist- ance.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS, MS.—For Chi-Rants Presents cr any other Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DEBERT'S, 10, CHURCH-STREET, LIVERPOOL, where yeti can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Electra-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc., at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chrongraph Watch, 6s. 11d., etc.), on page 7 of this Paper. -Advt. A. RUNCORN GIRL CHARGED WITH THEFT. ' "</p>
        <p>A girl, fourteen years of age, named Elizabeth Ann Martin, Gilbert-street, was charged before Mr. James Handley (chairman), and Mr. E. Imison, with stealing a coat, value 103. 6d., the property of William Henry Lightfoot, High-street, and she was further charged with having broken nine t anes of glass at the Police Station, doing damage to the extent of 23. 9J.—Frederick Allen, assistant at Mr. Lightfoot's shop, gave evidence as to hanging the coat outside the shop on Monday morn- ing. He remembered seeing the prisoner standing on the opposite side of the street.—Mr. Craddock, pawn- broker, spoke to the girl bringing the coat to his shop, and offering it in pledge. She said it was her brother's and he gave her 53. for it.—P.C. Gregory said the pris- oner broke the windows with her clog.—As no one appeared on her behalf, the magistrates remanded her for a week. PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH, RUNCORN. . .</p>
        <p>At thi-s.-c7;;Ch-o-r; Sunday, salmons' on behalf of the Primitive Methodist Horne, Colonial, and Foreign Mis- sions were preached by the Rev. J. Dudley, of Middle- ton. In the evening the ehoir gave an excellent render- ing of the anthem, "Come unto me," and Mr. Shingler sang" The Holy City." Miss Wilson presided at the organ in the morning, and Mr. William Grounds in the evening... .</p>
        <p>_ The half-yearly meeting was held an Wednesday even- ing. Mr.T.Raynolds occupied the chair, and there were also present the Revs.T. Bramall, W. Moore (Frodsham), and J.Dudley (Middleton). The Chairman in the course of a few remarks said their home, colonial, and foreign missionary society deserved their earnest support, as it was doing valuable work not only in this country,but in fereig,n fields. The Rev. W. Moore read the general re- port,which was of a satisfactory nature, and showed that there was an increase of membership at the respec- tive stations. It was only a quarter of a century since they sent out their first miasionary, but they had now 11 ministers and 1,040 members. Ad- dresses were also given by the Rev. J. Brarnall and the Rev. J. Dudley. The collection on Sunday amounted to ZI 19s. 4d., and the sum of £2 fle. Bd. had been ob- tained by the following scholars of the Sunday school : Mark Upton, J.a.mes Sayle, Richard Gough, Harry Barlow, Beatrice Fleet, M. Quayle, Florrie Hill, and F. Lewis. ST. PAUL'S BIBLE CLASS, RUNCORN.—OPEN MEETING. At the monthly open meeting in connection with the above-mentioned class there was a crowded attendance. After the bible lesson had been read, and a short in- teresting address given by Mr. B. Kniveton, a musical programme was rendered. Two choruses from Handel's oratorio "Judas Maccabaeus," entitled "Oh Father whose Almighty power" and "We never will bow down," were admirably sung by a choir of thirty voices, being accompanied by several stringed instruments and the organ. The choir also sang Gounod's "Come unto Him." Mr. Charles Leathwood sang "Arm, arm ye brave," from Handel's "Judas Maccabaeus," in splendid style. Two string qnartetts were beautifully performed by Messrs. Kay, Flesketh, Marlow, and Simkin. Mr. Hesketh (Manchester) also played Raff's " Cavatina " on the violin, with great feeling. Miss Bessie Ilarrop sweetly sang "The passing Bell." a solo by Claribel ; and Messrs. J. Parry and A. Shingler sang the duet "David and Goliath" in a descriptive manner. After the bene- diction, the march from "Judas Maccahaeus " was played with the strings and organ. Mr. R. Crosby ably conducted the choir, which was composed of singers drawn from the different choirs of the to vn, whilst Mr. William Cooke with his accustomed ability presided at the organ. RUNCORN BRANCH OP VTR BRITISH AND FOREIGN BIBLB</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SOCIRTY.</head>
        <p>SOCIRTY.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual meeting of the Run- corn Auxiliary of the British and Foreign Bible Society ' was held in Greenway-road schoolroom. Mr. P. Speakman, J.P.,C.0 occunied the chair aed there were also present the Revs. Grant Bird, Dr. Pieston, T. Brackenbury and W. George. Letters of apology for enforced absence was read from the RPI7S. T. Bramall,J. Stafford and C. de P. Gliddon. The Chairmaa said that when it was his privilege, some five pears ago, to go to Palestine the Bible became to him an illustrated book,and it now had a greater charm for him than ever before and this would continue to the end of his life.—The Rey. Grant Bird, who attended as a deputation then delivered an address on the work of the society.—The Rev. Dr. Pres- ton submitted the report of the treasurer, Mr. John Simpson. which showed that the collections at the meet- ing in 1894 realized £1 is. 2d.. sale of R.bles, £ll 104., and annual subscriptions. £27 Bs. After plying for Bibles and meeting all the expenses incurred, there had I been forwarded to the parent society £2l 7s. 111 The smeunts received from the variou• churches in 1894 and 1895 were respeetively :—Parish, £2 18s. and £3 is. 61.; Holy Trinity, £2 ss. and £1 6s. ; Wesleyan, £l2 2s 81. , and £ll Bs.: Sr. John's, Presbyterian. £3 4s. and £3 ; Bethesda Congregational, £3 94. and £3 13.. 61. ; Primi- tive Methodist, £1 9s. and £1 12s. ; Mrthodi.t Free 'Church. a and 17s. ; Halton Church, £1 101. 61. and £1 7s 61. ; fialten Wesleyan. £1 74. and £1 4s. ; total £29 ss. and £27 10s. 61. The collectors were—Miss Smith, Miss Preston, Miss Warner. Mrs. A. and Miss Norman, Miss Pritchard, Miss fleatherington. Mrs. Reynolds,Mrs. Collier.Mrs.Stefford,Mr.Buckley,and Miss Gamon.—The Rev. T. Brackenbury, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman, expressed a hope that the interest in the society would greatly increase in com- ing years.—The Rev. W. George seconded the motion, which was unanimously carried —During the proceed- ings an interesting musical programme was gone through. Mr. Worrell song " The Lost, Chord " Mrs. Temperley, "My Rod in !leaven ; Miss Johnson. The Lord is mindful of His own ;" Mr. J. Rooth, "0 Rest in the Lord." Miss Preston and Miss Johnson were the ancemnaniate,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>4i-EXANIINER" STEAM PRINTING WORKS BEWSET-STRBET. WARRINGTON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WELL SINKING BY A LOCAL FIRM.</head>
        <p>WELL SINKING BY A LOCAL FIRM.</p>
        <p>The " Eves-h-a-m-gt;r;d;r(l7' of last Saturday contains a report of the successful sinking of an artesian well at Stratford-on-Avon which has just been completed by Messrs. E. 'Timmins and Sons, of Runcorn. It is the first of its kind in the neighbourhood and the yield pr hour —without pumping—is at the rate of 1,850 gallons. The cost has been a little over £2,000. Messrs. Timmins have now in hand a similar work at ilainsborough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Sarah Hussey, 38 years of age, of 22 Hope-street, Run- corn, died somewhat suddenly on Wednesday afternoon. It appears that about three weeks ago she was obliged through illness to take to her bed. Dr. Robinson was sent for and he attended her up to last Saturday. She appeared to get better and continued so until noon on Wednesday. She suddenly took worse, and died at four o'clock the same day. It is thought that " heart disease" was the cause of death. The facts have been communi- cated to the Coroner.</p>
        <p>MIT+ICAL SERVICE,: AT BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL Canaca. At this church on Sunday evening, a cantata entitled "Faith triumphant, or the Healing of Naaman," was well given by an augmented choir. There was a large congregation. The principal vocalists were the Misses Heatherington, Pope, M. Heatherington, Hayes, and Harrop ; Messrs. Worrall, 4lowanlock, E. Lunt, J.Leath- wood, J. Lunt, Thompson, G. W. Yates, and Littler,and they acquitted themselves in a very creditable manner. Mr. C. Le Couteur presided at the organ with his usual ability. CAMDEN SUNDAY SCHOOL GIRLS' PARLOUR.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of this society an entertainment was given in Camden School on Thursday evening. The object was to augment the fund which has been formed for the purpose of purchasing a piano for the "parlour," and despite the unfavourable weather a good audience assembled. Mr. R. 11. Issnett presided. The following interesting programme was gone through :—Song, Miss Emma Handing ; sang, Miss S. In ; song, Miss Bessie Harrel); recitation, Miss Davies ; song, Mrs. 'remperley ; violin solo. Miss Lilly Temperley. A aeries of magic-lantern slides,illustrative of half-hours at the ssaside," was displayed by Mr. J. Robinson, and the descriptive readings were given by Mr. John Newburn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>In the Board-room at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon,a meeting of this Council was held. Mr.Mcln- tyre J.P.. occupied the chair,and there were also present Admiral Parry, Messrs. H. E. Heywood, J. Brandreth, S. Woodward, J. M. Williams, C. Roylance, T. Baxter, C.C., J. Parr, T. Lewis, W. Bradford, J. Derbyshire, T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, J. Spruce, E. Wallington, J.W. Baguley, John Whitlow, E. Bretherton, J. Weir, W. Pickering, C. W. Davenport, J. Stokes, W. Holt, and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors), and Mr. Farrington (inspector of nuis- ances).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>TIM SEATHAOId OP =UM.</head>
        <p>TIM SEATHAOId OP =UM.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board forwarded to the Coun- cil a loter they had received from Mr. F. B. Mason, of 40, St. Werburgh's Mount, Chester, complaining that notwithstanding previous representations no improve- ment had been made in the sewerage of Helsbv.—ln answer to a question by Mr. Brandreth as to the 'health af Helsbv, Dr. Adams said it was particularly good. Mr. Farrington also in answer to a question said he hadn't recoived any complaints from Helsby. Be had two certificates of analyses of water from Helsby and they were returned as first-class waters.—A committee was appointed to consider the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ROAD IMPROVEMENT AT WESTON.</head>
        <p>THE ROAD IMPROVEMENT AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board gave their formal sanc- tion to the borrowing by the Council of a sum of £l3O for the construction of a sewer in South-road, Weston. As regarded the proposed widening of the road the Board inquired whether it had in fact been irrevocably dedic- ated as a highway, so that the owners of property abutting tbereon would not be legally empowered to deprive the public of their right of way over it.—The Clerk explained that the land had been bought from Captain Clarke for the purpose of a public highway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WESTON NIGHTSOIL DEPOSITS.</head>
        <p>WESTON NIGHTSOIL DEPOSITS.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Collier-, clerk to the Weston Parish Council, wrote that his Council recommended for acceptance the tenders of Messrs. Thomas Ford and John Jeffs, for the provision of a nightsoil deposit in the Weston and Weaver wards, the asbpits to be emptied twice a year, autumn and summer. The tenders were subject to such conditions as the District Council might think necessary. —Mr. Chadwick in moving the acceptance of the tenders said they were very reasonable.—Mr. Stokes seconded, and the recommendation was approved of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LETTER FROM SIR JOHN BRUNNER,</head>
        <p>LETTER FROM SIR JOHN BRUNNER,</p>
        <p>Sir John T. Brunser, Bart., M.P.. wrote "Pressure of affairs has hitherto prevented my replying to your letter advising me of the resolution passed by veur Council in favour of legislation for facilitating the construction of Feht railways. I shall heartily support any well con- sidered proposal for such legislation, though I am strongly inclined to the opinion that from a national point of view it would be more economical to develop the existing system of inland navigation. The two ideas happily do not conflict with each other, and I trnst we shall have legislation in favour of both. I have now before me a draft of a carefully detailed letter to the President of the Board of Trade upon inland naviga- tione, which I hope to forward to him shortly."—Com- munications were also road from the Local Government Board, and the Board of Trade acknowledging receipt of a copy Df the same resolution forwarded to Sir John Brunner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported a case of scarlet fever at Moore; three at Weston; and a few cases at Stockton Heath. As measles and mumps were epidemic at Stockton Heath, the schools had been closed for a month. Measles being epidemic in Grappenhall he suggested that the schools be closed for a month. One case of diphtheria had occurred at Booth-st., Frodsham ; two cases of al- leged scarlet fever in Church-st., Frodsham, and one of diphtheria on the same premises, and also one at Prod- sham Lordship. SERIOUS COMPLAINT FROM FRODSHAM LORDSHIP.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. John Ockleston, Oakdale, Frodsham. It ran RS follows As the epi lemic of diptheria still continues in Five Crosses, through the in- sanitary condition and absence of a proper watersupply, facts which seem to be of no serious import to your Board, I write to say that unless something is done by your Board, on Tuesday,to remedy this disgraceful state of affairs, I shall feel It my duty, as a householder with diptheria on the premises, and no proper water supply as well as defective drainage to report the matter to the Local Government Board."—Dr. Adams stated that an inspection had been made, and suspicion was directed to the drinking water which had been used. A sample had be,n taken and it would be analysed.—The Medical Officer was requested to fully investigate matters, and prepare a report. On receiving it, Mr. Ashton was in- structed to convene a meeting of the Froasham Lord- ship Parochial Committee to consider the report.</p>
        <p>HALTON SEWAGE QUESTION._</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board acknowledged the re- ceipt of the Council's letter of the 5111 inst., embodying cop' of a resolution passed by the Parochial Committee of Halton, which was adapted by the Rural District Council on the 29th ult., with reference to the proposed a.rangement for the treatment of the sewage flowing into Balton Brook. In reply the Board stated that they retained the opinion that the proposal was not one which would meet the necessities of the case. The Board did rot consider that it was necessary to direct an inspection to be made in the matter by one of their officers. The Board requested the Rural Council to further constder the subject with a view to the adoption of a scheme which would prevent the passing of un- purified sewage into the brook.—lt was derided to refer the matter to the Halton Parochial Committee. NUISANCE INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrington reported that he had had an interview with Mr. Borthwick, of Widnes, with respect to the im- provement of cottages at Weston Point, and some offen- sive privies,and on the suggestion of the Inspector, the matter was again deferred.—An application was made on behalf el the ffelsby Athletic Club for an alteration of a sewer passing through land that they had taken for re- creation Dtirpows. The matter was referred to the com- mittee appointed to considier the drainage of Flelsby.— A certificate of occupation was granted for three new houses erected by Mrs. Bowden at Overton on the re- commendation of Mr. FArrington.—ffn also reported a nuisance under the Quarries' Act of 1887, arising frqm the disused and unprotected portion of the quarry ad- joining a frotpath at Helshy. This was also referred to the committe appointed for consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DILAPIDATED BRIDER AT V7HITLET.</head>
        <p>A DILAPIDATED BRIDER AT V7HITLET.</p>
        <p>A r.tport wail submitted by Mr. Swinton respecting Brook Bridge between the townships of Whitley Inferior and Little Leigh, and between Runcorn and Northwich Rural District Councils. Re stated that he met the sur- veyor of the Northwich Connell, and together they in- spected the bridge, and found that two thirds of the south end were in a dilapidated state. The north end had been improved and was in good order. They agreed that a committee should bony pointed from each council to meet and consider the advisability of rebuilding the old portion as early as possible.—A committee was ap- pointed to meet representatives of the Northwich Dis- trict Council.</p>
        <p>This was all the business of public interest.</p>
        <p>..::14.:7,.7*..i l'i.,.,:...i.t.',.,;.:!.'.,-,,,,,, !4,..;:;1'.... l''',,.::....,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SUPPOSED TRAGIC DEATH OF A</head>
        <p>THE SUPPOSED TRAGIC DEATH OF A</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ENGINEER.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF INQUIRIES BY RELATIVES. Early in October we received through the post the following letter signed by "Thomas B. Arm- strong,"of San Francisco, California,in which it will be seen the details are given of the tragic death of an engineer supposed to belong to Runcorn. The letter has upon the face of it an appearance of enuinenemand as a matter of fact within twenty- four hours of its publication in the "Examiner " the father of the missing engineer, Mr. Thomason, of Runcorn, called at our office for the" inspection card" mentioned in the letter, and he remarked in the course of conversation that he re- membered the departure from Liverpool of his son in the vessel, Lord Gough, named on the card. It will be seen, however, from the subjoined letters that Mr. Ince, of Lostock Gralam, the father-in-law of the engineer in question, has been making inquiries the result of which he seems to think tends to discredit the account given in Armstrong's letter. Needless to say we should be very pleased to hear that Mr. Thomason has not suffered the very terrible death which is described, though unfortunately his relatives do not seem to have heard from him for a considerable time past. The following is a copy of the letter which ap- peared in the" Examiner "of October sth : Sir,—Will you kindly publish in your paper the facts that I here send to you ? On November 13th the last year (1894) while on our way from Bombay to San Fran- cisco Mr. William Thomason, the engineer on duty, was examining 000 of the boilers which had begun to leak. He had just opened the furnace door, and was looking in the furnace when one uf the tubes burst blowing the fire out of the door. Between the steam and the fire he was terribly burned, so much so that we could not take off his clothes ; both his eyes were burned out, and all the hair off his head. He only lived for two hours and the same day he was buried at sea. I have tried to find his friends but could not de so, and as he had a wife living somewhere in. England, I write as a last trial to you. The reason I write to you is this. On our last trip home I had to clean out a tool chest in the engine room which had not been touched since his death. and at the bottom I found an old waistcoat belonging to him, also part of a newspaper which was the "Runcorn Examiner." In the pocket of the coat I found the en- closed ticket, which has his name on it showing that he left Liverpool on, July 26th 1693. Seeing that by the ticket he came from Runcorn and that a Runcorn news- paper was with his clothes I thought the best thing I I could do would be to write and ask you to publish the facts. I enclose the ticket and if any of his friends apply for it please give it to them. We were four days sail out from Bombay when the accident happened. If you will do this it will be a great deal of satisfaction for me as I have done what I could to find his friends, as he was one of the best of friends to me.—Yours truly, THOMAS B. ARMSTRONG. Steamer Colonna,Syn Francisco, California, September 13th.1895, I have no address of my owe but letters sent to me at the Central Post Office; in San Francisco will find me.</p>
        <p>Ths letter which we have received from Mr. Inca this week is es follows</p>
        <p>Sir.—ln tour issue of the sth October, 1895, y,pu pub- lished a letter purporting to be from Thomas B. Arm- strong, S 1!) Francisco, giving an account of the death cf Mr. Thomason (my son-in-law). As the result of en- quiries from Ist, the Liverpool underwriters'association; 2nd. the examination of Lloyds list ; and 3rd, from the British Consul at San Fransisco, (whose letter I enclose for publication), such a steamer cannot be found, and I am of opinion, th4it, (pending any further information coming to hand) the report is not true, and I must de- cline accepting it as correct. I shall be obliged by the insertion of this in your next issue, as I think it only right that such a cruel hoax as the above appears to be, should not be allowed to pass ,unnoticed,—l am, sir, yours truly, JOIIN INCE. Ivy Terrace, Lostock Gralam, 25th November, 1895.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of the Consul's letter :—"Her Britannic Majesty's Consulate General, San Francisco, 7th November, 1895. Sir,—l only received your note of the 22nd ultime enclosing me a cutting of the "Runcorn Examiner" of the sth October, 1895, regarding the re- ported death of one William Thomason, said to have oc- curred on the steamer Colonna on a voyage from Bom- bay for this port. In the first place, no steamers run between Bombay and this port. There is a line running from here to Hong Kong, and all freights from and to India is handled by the P. 0.0. in connection with such line. No such steamer as the Colonna is known here, either by any one in this office or at the Custotns House, where I sent to make special inquiries. It is my opinion that the story of Mr. Thomason's death is made out *1 whole cloth.—l am,dear sir,yours faithfully, WELLESLEY MOORE, Actg. Br. Cons.-Gronl.—Mr. John Ince, Ivy Terrace, Lostock Gralam, Northwich, Cheshire, Eng- land."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TABLEAUX 'VIVA NTS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>TABLEAUX 'VIVA NTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>With the laudable object of assisting local charitable institutions, an interesting and highly successPul enter- tainment was given in the Public Hall, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening before a large and appreciative audience. The chief feature of the evening's programme was the tableaux ritants given by a number of local ladies and gentlemen with Miss Pierpoint as instructress, and the manner in which these pictorial representations were given reflected credit upon all concerned ; and that Miss Pierpoint's efforts were appreciated was manifested by the loud call at the close of the performance for her appearance before the curtain, and the hearty reception she was accorded. .</p>
        <p>...„ It would be impossible here to describe seriatim each tableaux—for there were nearly two dozen in ad—but some of the chief may be mentioned. The representation of "The Relief of Lucknow "in four scenes was indeed an impressive sight. The first tableau vivant introduced I " Jessie " dreaming, and in the succeeding scene she was the foremost figure in a group of the besieged whire the sick are being attended to, and full of expectancy and excitement you can almost hear her saying " Dinna ye hear it? Dinna ye hear it? Av I'm no dreamin' 'tie the 'slogan' is' the Hiehlanders ! We're saved ! we're saved !" During the third scene, the orchestra struck I up "The Campbells are coming," and Jessie to the as- tonished group is saying "Will ye no believe it coo ?" In the last scene the besieged garrison are relieved, and tie performance of "Auld Lang Syne" brings L. to a fitting close. Two rather amusing tableaux followed. In the first, it is seven o'clock at night and the gentleman is leaving home for his club, and he is receiving a parting request from his wife—"l pray you know me when we meet again," and in the next tableau, the clock ha a worked round to four a.m., and the gentleman is returning much the worse for liquor, and his wife is I supposed to be greeting him with the words " Thou comest in such a questionable shape." The inhuman trade of buying slaves was admirably and feelingly pour- trayed in the three tableaux entitled" An Eastern Slave I Market' A most attractive spectacle was that in wh.ch an illustration in dumb show was given of the nursery rhyme "Queen of Hearts." "Housewifery in high places" was the first, in which the queen aided by a retinue of servants was enabled to make, in queenly style, sem pastry. "Knavish !Tricks " was the second, and in the succeeding scene, the knave was receiving his punish- ment for exciting the kingly wrath. It concluded with a happy gathering representing " All's well that ends well." The dresses in these four scenes were very appropriate, a fine effect being produced by the gowns worn by the respective queens of hearts, of diamonds, of spades, and clubs. There were two scenes representing the parable of the ten virgins. In the first the virgins were slumbering and sleeping during the tarrying of the bridegroom,and the succeeding scene I showed the five wise virgins with their lamps lit,and the five foolish beseeching the wise to "Give us of your oil for our lamps are gone out," &amp;c. The concluding item was a tableau representing "Psyche before Venus." The whole of the "living pictures " were strikingly !and vividly presented, the dresses, colouring, and posing of the figures being all that could he wished for. The lime light effects, ton, added not a little to the success of the psrformance, and this department was in the charge of Mr. Clement Handley and Mr. Jesse Rheries. Miss Pierpnint was assisted in preparing the artistes by Miss Ada Timmins. The stage scenery had been kindly lent for the occasion by Mr. Caddick, of Runcern theatre. The following is a complete list of the performers :—Misses E. Bantam!, Barker, M. Barker, F. Briscoe. Cameron, M. D•akin, Forrester, E. Gregory, A. Gerrard, N. Johnson, T. Kirkham, N. Lea, Pritchard, M. Pope, 0. Price, G. Raynor, A. Riley, M. Smith, C. Stokes, C. S Won, G. Scraggs, Ivy Tate, Taylor, Timmins, F. Timmins, P. Timmins, Mr. W. Crosby, Mr. l. Lea, Mr. J. Pritchard, (Farnworth), and Mr. T. B. Pierpoint. . . . .</p>
        <p>A choice selection of music was rendered by a well-rquipned orchestra under the conductorship of Mr. It. W. T'ierpnint, and they interspereed very agreeable items during the intervals between the tableaux. A cornet solo by Mr. Murray Wl4l rostlsr appreciated, and the performance of the descriptive piece " The Deride's Dream " was loudly anplauded,en encore being complied with. Mr. Cyril Chancier is a capital reciter,and his two contributions given by desire, were recited in such a manner as to enhance the repu- tation he has gained. In response to a recall he gave n 'theft excerpt from Portia's plea for mercy in the well- known trial of Antonio in Shakespeare's " Merchant ef Venice." Mr. Branch was the only vraillie.and his two songs were" The Powder Monkey" and " Jack's Yarn," which were g'ven in A most creditable manner. The orchestra was composed of the following :—First violins Mr. `V. B. Kay. Mr. H. Bantock, Mr. Yarwood, Mr. Tonke. Second violins.: Mr. Di*chtield. Mr. G. Frith, Mr. Clare, Mr. Hanley. Viola : Mr. Marlow. 'Cellos: Mr. W. Simkin, Mr. Balmford. Basses : Mr. W. 7- Yarwoo I, Mr. Whitfield (Warrington). Flutes : Mr. Yarwood, .Mr. H. P.)l.SlClarionettes : Mr. W. Outb-</p>
        <p>waite, Mr. A. Outhwaite. Oboe : Mr. F. Davies. Cor- nets: Mr. Murray, Mr. P. LeCouteur. Trombone : Mr. F. LeCouteur. Drums : Mr. E. Moorfield. Accompan- ist, Miss Crosby. The singing of" God Save the Queen" concluded a!most enjoyable evening's entertainment. Mr. W. Crosby fulfilled the duties of secretary, and Mr. J. B. Holland of treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>IT is now quite evident that the TheQ Education re-opening of the education con- troversy which the present Gov- ernment seems to be bent upon, at the bidding of its Church supporters, will not find the Noncon- formists unprepared for resistance. There are indications on everylhand that practically all the Nonconformist bodies are thoroughly alive to the importance of this question, and that they will be closely united in their actions in connection with it. As the 11317. J. Elresr HOLLOWELL said on Tue- day,when speaking at a meeting of the Manchester District Congregational Association on " The nature and importance of the education contro- versy," the differences, estrangements and splits on the Irish and other questions would not re-ap- pear in this education controversy. Another point was made by the same speaker which LORD SALIS- BURY of all men should be able to appreciate. This was that the education policy which it is under- stood the Government will adopt was not formally and separately submitted to the country at the last election. That has been in times past a favourite argument with the Tory leader in rela- tion to several questions—and particularly that of Home Rule—which have been inscribed upon the Liberal programme. Measures in which these questions were raised have been proposed by the Liberals when in power and have been opposed and even rejected in the House of Lords on the plea that they had not been specificallY approved by the country. At the same time it is not likely that those who will oppose the education policy of the Government will rely too much upon that pre- cedent. It was a useful argument for LORD Sims- nuay simply because it was a pretext upon which the House of Lords could act in opposing the House of Commons. But during the present Parliament the House of Lords will be practically non-existent as a "revising "power. What the House of Commons passes at the instigation of the Government the House of Lords will most cer- tainly endorse, even though it be a scheme for upsetting the compromise of 1870 which has never received public approval. Further evidence of Nonconfcrmist alertness and energy was given on Wednesday when the deputation appointed by the last Wesleyan Conference waited upon the Plums MINISTER to lay before him the views of the Wesleyan Methodists of the country. Sir HENRY FOWLER, who introduced the deputation, referred to one fact which shows how ',important is the education question to the Wesleyans. There are, he mentioned, at the present time about a )F million scholars attending the Wesleyan Sunday 4 schools whilst there are less than two hundr ed k thousand attending the Wesleyan day schools. What becomes of the other eight hundred thousand on the week days when*by law they are compelled to attend school? The answer is obvious. A considerable proportion of the eight hundred thousand are attending day schools ' which are under the exclusive control of Church- men although almost entirely supported by public money. The children attend these schools simply because in many districts there are no other , schools which they can go to. Yet an influential section of Churchmen are not content with the advantages they at present enjoy in regard to the ed.ucation of the rising generation. They are de-' manding further privileges and more State aid which if granted will place Nonconformists of every ° denomination in a worse position than at present. Therefore, the deputation which waited upon ' Lord SALISBURY voiced the feelings not only of Wesleyans but of all Nonconformists when it de- manded that there should be no increase in the grants of public money unless provision was- • made for public end representative management of the schools. Lord SALISBURY in his reply . could not dispute the fact that the Nonconfor- mists have a serious grievance to complain of, but as usual he was barren of remedies. At the same time his remarks seemed to show that in one respect he is under a process of enlightenment. Now that he is in office and at close quarters with the question he admits that it is " one of the greatest political problems that ever beset any community." He was apparently disposed to blame the Nonconformists for reviving the con-</p>
        <p>• , • 4.^.•</p>
        <p>et.*</p>
      </div>
