      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER COMPROMISE. The Chairman of the Runcorn School Board (Mr. J. Pritchard), convened a meeting of the gentlemen nomin- ated for the School Board and their supporters, in the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Friday. The purpose of the meeting was to discuss matters relative to the elections and if possible to come to some arrangement and thus avoid a contest. The meeting was largely attended. among those present;being the following nominated can- didates :—Mr. Pritchard (chairman), Rev. Dr. Gaetaldt, Messrs. E. Imison, J.P., S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., and P. J. Norman, „EP., Rev. T. Brackenbury, Messrs. S. C. Beckett, John Clarke, Walter Collier, William Handley, Benjamin Kniveton, John Meadowcroft, R. H. Posiaett, Joseph Pritchard, Timothy Simister, C. A. Timmins,and John Wilson. Others present included the Rev. Dr. Preston, Messrs. T. Lea, Thomas Dean, John Capper, Robert Wright, James Kirkham, Edward Taylor, D. H. Jones, J. R. Frith, Joseph Leadstone, J. M. Frith, A. Lightburn, John Littler, William Banks,William Mellor, William Hankinson, W. E. Lea, Jonathan liorsfield, George Hazlehurst, George Hill, J. H. Hamilton, John Morris, Thomas Kennedy and John Quinn. The Chairman in opening the meeting explained why he had brought them together and said it was in accord- ance with a practice which had prevailed for a number of years. He had called the candidates and their sup- porters together to consider the situation,and if possible to arrive at a compromise. He stated that be did not understand that there was any dispute with regard to the work of the Board, or any complaint respecting the policy pursued. There seemed every prospect of the meeting being futile in bringing about a compromise, as many appeared determined that there should be a con- test, and at an early part of the proceedings Mr. Walter Collier stated that the meeting was practically of no use as he intended to go to the poll. During a prolonged and animated conversation which followed some of the speakers expressed themselves in favour of the number of members being increased, and It was unanimously agreed to recommend that the Board , should apply for an increase in the number of members of the Board by two,and that Mr. C. A. Timmins should take the seat placed at the disposal of the Church party, and that the other seat should be filled by the repre- sentative of the Nonconformist or Progressive party,and this it is understood will be Mr. Walter Collier. It was further stated that in the event of a contest being forced for the two additional seats, both parties would unite in securing the return of the nominee of either party. Under these conditions the gentlemen nomin- ated, with the exception of the present members, all signified their intention to withdraw,and signed a docu- ment to that effect. In the meantime, however, Mr. Meadowcroft had left the meeting, and it was decided to wait upon him on the following day and obtain his signature. On the motion of Mr. J. R. Frith, seconded by Mr. Taylor, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman. In accordance with the decision arrived at, notices of withdrawal for the gentlemen in question were handed in to the returning officer, Colonel Ashton, on Saturday afternoon.</p>
        <p>FREE LIBRARY LECTURES AT THE RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The first of a series of short lectures on hooks and their authors to be given in the Technical Institute under the auspices of the Runcorn Free Library was de- livered on Friday evening when the Rev. C. De Putron 'Gliddon, Wesleyan minister stationed at Halton, was the lecturer and spoke on " Whittier," the American poet. In the unavoidable absence of Mr. F. J. Norman' Mr. J. D. Jones, the librarian, briefly introduced the lecturer, and in doing so stated that the lectures were given with the object of helping the library. Last ses- sion they were delivered in the reading roorn,but it was found so inconvenient for the purpose that the Library Committee asked the Technical Instruction Committee if they would allow them the use of a room in the Techni- cal Institute, and this permission was given. The ap- plications for some books from the bibrary he was able to trace distinctly to the lectures given last session, and he hoped the lectures which were to be given would have the same effect.— The Lecturer said he claimed ne special qualification as a critic, no quality of judgment resulting from exhaus- tive study ; hut he confessed that Whittier was his favourite poet, and had struck chords in his heart which no other writer had touched.and helped him In his work as no other poet had done. To maks such a confession in some literary circles would possibly provoke a smile, and it would be difficult, if not impossible, to render satisfactory reasons for that preference. But it was not to be interpreted as meaning that Whittier was to be ranked with such world-poets as Homer, Virgil, Gcethe, Dante, or Shakespeare. nor yet that he held a premier place among his own English poets. But in the brother- hood of illustrious American authors whose works were read on both sides of the Atlantic, Longfellow, Bryant, Emerson. Hawthorn, Lowell, and Holmes; Whittier might with confidence be advanced to front rank. In his own line he was inferior to none of these, and per- haps was more distinctly American than anyone of them, except Bryant whom, however, he exceeded in health of subject and intensity of feeling. Whittier was essentially the Quaker poet, clad in homely sombre gray, but withal of sterling honesty and kindli- ness of heart. His language was plain,almost to a fault, Sometimes his choice of words seemed careless, some- times he neglected the technique of his art, contented ic the words conveyed ths thoughts, without making effort to clothe them in the purple and fine linen of the most perfect expressions. Simplicity was always a predomin- ant feature. Thera were no stanzas of sweltering polysyllables, nor any of that perfectly chiselled, musical style which was so characteristic of Tennyson's best work. There was noth,ng of the elaborate decorated Gothic nature, all was of the simple, solid, severe Norman order. The selection of expressions was instinc- tive rather than studied. Some of his religious poetry more than any other in the English language, resembled the style of St. John. As an example the lecturer read the poem " Our Master," published in 1867. To some extent, he said, it might be explained by the strange and exciting experience of his earlier days. He was yet a youth when with Lloyd-Garrison he consecrated his talent to the cause of emancipation. His connection with the Liberation Society kept him poor, when he might have been rich, closed all around him literary and social doors and frequently exposed his life and pronerty to the furious violence of lawless mobs. Such times needed the hot fervour, the ready phrase, to rouse the pity of the people for the oppressed, And wake the con- science of the North to duty. His war poems often sounded with the harshness of the buele,rather th n the dulcet notes of the flute. But only in the qoiet of retired leisure could the poet weld the graceful, tuneful cadence to the deep thoughts of the soul. His national poems were faithful, fresh and beautiful pic- tures. without striking originality of conception or ex- pression. but they were a mirror of outward nature in which he did for his country what Burns did for his. As a specimen a portion of " Snowbound" was read. But, he continued, it was as a writer of ballads and re- ligious poetry that Whittier had captivated and en- chanted so many devoters. Hers was his unique and peerless charm. His " Maud Muller," and" Yelling of the Bees" were miniature classics, and reached the very summit of the art ef ballad writing. In his religious priory, he spoke from his heart to the reader's heart. That was the secret of his power. He opened the win- dow of his soul, and you saw the man loyal, pure, hum- ble, strong and you loved hint. Here his note was truest and sweetest. His mind, like that of Ruskin, Tennyson and Shakespeare was attired with biblical allusions and phrases. Lester found 450 distinct biblical allusions and quotations in Tennyson's works. hut a minister of the gospel named Smith, counted 820 in Whittier's poems. In all the treasurers of sacred poetry we knew nothing more exquisite, more soulful than "The External Good- ness," and with the reading of a portion of it, the lec- turer fittingly cencluded.—The lecturer was 'thanked for his services on the rnotisn ef Mr. Jones, seconded by Mr. Pope.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WIGAN MURDER.</head>
        <p>THE WIGAN MURDER.</p>
        <p>TWO COLLIERS SENTENCED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>William Kearsley and Elijah Winstanley, colliers, were tried at the Liverpool AfiqiZ.fl on Tuesday on the charge of murdering Robert Kidd. a detective in the employ- ment of the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany, at Wigan, on September 29. The evidence showed that the prisoners, alone with another man, were on the railway for the nurpose of stealing goods from waggons standing on a siding., when they were discovered iv Kidd and a second detective named Osborne. A severe struggle took place, in the course of which Kidd. held down by Kearsley, was stabbed all over the face, heal, and neck by Winstanley, receiving injuries from whieh he died shortly afterwards. Osborne, who was also severely handleiLwas one of the wi'messes for the prose. colon on Tuesday, and another important witness was a man named Flalliwelf. who was with the prisoners at the time and turned " Queen's evidence." While Haiti- well was in the witnass-hox he fainted and fell to the floor, and some time eIAMPIi before he had sufficiently recovered to permit of the remainder of his evidence being taken. The jury found bcth prisoners guilty,and they were sentenced to death. At the Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday, before Mr, Justice Collins. William Fialliwell, cnllier, who was in- dieted for having eau.ed griernos herlilr harm to Detn,- ' tive Osborne at Wigan on the 29-h 9,,ptemher. was dis- charged. Halliwell gave evidence on Tuesday against his two companions Winstanier and KPArSter, who were I sentenced to death for the murder of Detective Kidd,and on that account the prosecution did nc,t proceed with the charge against him.</p>
      </div>
