      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>AN OWNER WANTED FOR A WATCH, OVERCOAT, AND</p>
        <p>W EIGHTS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, before the Mayor and Alderman Harrison, William Edwards, a disreputable locking young man, was charged in cus- tody with having in his possession a silver watch and three brass counter weights, and unable to give a satis- factory account of them.—The Chief Constable said that the facts were briefly these. On Saturday night, about eleven o'clock, Detective-Sergeant Monaghan visited a common lodging-house in Bank-st., and seeing the pri- soner, of whom he entertained some suspicion, began to question him. The result was that he searched him and found upon his person a silver lever watcn,attached to a chain, and three or four brass counter weights. Defend- ant cculd not—did not attempt to, in fact—account for the weights. With regard to the watch he made three contradictory statements. The first was that he bad bought it from a man in Deansgate, Manchester ; the second that he had bought it from a man (of course an unknown one) in the street; the third was that he had obtained It from a man in Altrincham. Proceeding, the Head Conssable said he was assured that not one of these statements was correct ; the prisoner was not the kind of man to have such a watch as the one produced in his possession. It was very probable the police would find an owner far it in a few days, and he would, there- fore, ask their worships to remand the prisoner for a week. An overcoat bad also been traced to the prisoner, which he had sold at a beerhouse for 53. When he next made his appearance that would probably be the subject matter of the charge of larceny.—Detective-Sergeant Monaghan said that the coat was a new one, and when asked if it fit the defendant, said, "No ; it fits him too rauch."—Prisoner was remanded for a week. WORKINGMEN AND THEIR FOWLS.—.AN ALLEGED</p>
        <p>NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Before the magistrates at the Warrington Court on Monday, Walter Bancroft, Napoleon Bayley, and Joseph Robinson, all residing in Albert-terrace, Howley, were charged with keeping poultry under such conditions as to be a nuisance, on the 14th November.—lnspector Norman said the defendants bad henpens in three yards, and kept a number of fowls. The nuisance complained of was that excreta was deposited in the yard, and was allowed to remain there. People passing from out of the yard carried the objectional matter into the house on their boots. The Medical Officer (Dr. Gornall) and him- self were both of the opinion that it was quite time to stop these nuisances. Several persona had been induced to abate the nuisance; but the defendants would not.— Mr. F. R. Roberts,who appeared on behalf of the defen- dants, said that they admitted this fou(w)1 decd. (Laughter.) Bancroft had kept fowls for 23 years and they all thought it was hard that they should be called upon to murder them in two er three days. -- What they asked for was an adjournment for seven days as they were willing to abate the nuisance,and if at the end of that time they had not disposed of the fowls the bench could do what they thought fit.—Mr. Roberts' request was acceded to.—lnspector Norman intimated that they were going to commence a sort of ' crusade against the owners of fOwla to make them abate these nuisances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Furndlr.--Before Alderman Harrison, Dr. Gonaall, Alderman Wright, and Councillor Roberts. DRUNK AND DISORDEFILY.—WiIIiam Brindley, Stocknort, was charge with being drunk and disorderly in Bank-st., and dismissed on promising to leave the town ; Rose Burns, Friars' Green, similar offence in Friars' Green, was also dis- missed on promising to go to the Workhouse. IRREGULAR Bosom. ATTENDANCE Jones. Sankey Bridges, was charged with having on the 11th November and during the past six months neglected to send his three chil- dren to school.—Mr. Shearer prosecuted, and said the parents were entirely to blame.—Defendant was fined 58. in- cluding costs. WORKING •iLAKE HORSE.—John William Richmond, 2. Herbert-nt., and Miny-lane. Wigan,was charged with having on the 16th November ill-treated a horse by working the samelwhile in an unfit state.—lnspector tuff said that he saw the defendant lo Winwick-road on the date named with a grey gelding attached to a wagon. It was lame in the off fore leg, which was swollen and tender. The horse was in pain.—Sergeant Taylor and Mr. Dixon. veterinary surgeon, eorrohorated.—A fine of ss. and costs was imposed including Mr. Dixon's fee.—A similar charge was brought against John Wood. 1, Wharf-at.—Sergeant Cheney stated that on the Bth Inst. he SAW the defendant in charge of a roan horse drawing a street brush. The animal flinched in turning a corner and witness on examining it founds sore on the lower part of the shoulder about the Rise of a nhilling.—lnspoctor ralff corro- borated AS to the condition of the horse, and defendant was fined ss. and costs. TFIRAPHING A Hoase.—Altert tongshaw (14). Mill-street' was charged with having on the 9th November cruelly ill- treated a horse by beating the same. inspector Luff said that he was in Sankey-street on the date named when he saw the defendant in charge of a horse and cart. The lad was lashing the animal all the way down Sankey-street and when witness overtook him at Bnnk Quay he was standing by it lashing it round the legs.—A tine of 55. including costs was inflicted instead of the birch as suggested by the Inspector. A PROVOKED ASSIXT.T.—Thomas Shaw, 3, Grey-street, was Charged with assaulting And beating filizabeth Bibby, 27, Repwood-street, on the 16th inst. There was a cnns-snm- mons charging Bibby with assaulting Shaw.—Mr. F. R. Roberts appeared for Shaw.—Complainant said that Mr. Shaw whippen her boy and 'stre-went op to him and threatened what she would do for him were she a man. She tried to strike him, whereupon he struck her in the eye and knocked her down. When her husband came he was under the hum, and would not come out. else perhaps there would have been no' law at all. (Laughter.)—Con3plairtant's son said defendant struck her when she' attempted to strike him. —Mr. Roberts said that on the day in question about2.ls, Shaw was sitting in his house having his dinner with his horse and lorry standing outside the house on private land. 'The animal had its none-bag on and a number of boys in- cluding complainant's son were playing about the horse, and he 'Mr. Roberts) understood that a nervous animal could not eat when disturbed in that manner. Defendant told the lads to go, and some did, but complainant's son remained and defendant, just " tickled" him with his whip to make him move off. He was not hurt but his mother, the com- plainant, came out and after endeavouring to strike defend- ant succeeded, ant broke his pipe In his mouth. Then be lost his temper and struck the woman. He did wrong, but be was provoked and she had assaulted him first. Mr. Roberts called a witness who asserted positively that she saw complainant strike defendant and break his pipe in his ynoulh.—The magistrates fined defenlant 55. and costs and dismissed the cross-summons. A SQUABBLE ON TowN HlLL.—Catherine Kilcourse and Annie Corrohoy, Town Hill. were charged, the former with committing damage to the door of 5, Town Hill, and the latter with breaking a window in the same house.—Annie Davies. tenant of 5, Town Hill, said that on Saturday night Kilcourse followed her frora the market and when she went in she kicked the latch off the door. Corroboy came up at the same time and threw a brick through the winiow.—A fine of 55. incind- ing costs was inflicted on each defendant. --•- -</p>
        <p>McatDaY.—Before the Mayor (chairman) and Alderman</p>
        <p>Hai•rison. DRUNKEITIVESS.—Mary Burke, of no settled abode, Was committed to prison for 14 days for baying been drunk on Saturday night : Thomas Connor fer haying been drunk and disorderly in Knutsford-road on the 16th November wall fined ss. Including costs. BREACH OE THE PEACE—Peter Cummerford and John Whittaker for Slatting trgather in Market-street, on the sth</p>
        <p>HUPTllHl3.—lnsportant Notice.-9. J. BEIERMILST, Hernia Speeklist, still remains at the old address His only address In London—namely, 64, Chancery Lane, W.C. Send for hie new book of lull informatlon,7o. poet free.—ildf.</p>
        <p>November were bound over to keep the pease for 6 months in a suriety of £5 and pay the costs. SEREET OBSTRuCTION.—John Chandley was charged with obstructing the tree passage of Peter-street by leaving a horse and cart standing there an unnecessary time on the 20th November —P.C. Delahay said the vehicle was stand- ing in Peter-street for 20 minutes.—Defendant was fined 25. 6d. —A similar offence was proved against Alfred Wilcox on the same day and place, and a fine of Is. inflicted. BOY Gereinams —William Chadwick, Henry Shaw, John Clark, and Sydney Morgan, were all charged with playing "pitch and toss" near Elizabeth-street on the 17th Novem er. —All pleaded guilty and after P.C. Barlow had stated his evidence showing that the boys were playing with half- pence. each was fined Is. WILLING TO FORGIVE.—Dayid Whiteley. 21. Greenall-at.. was charged with being drunk and disorderly and also with assaulting his wife Ellen on the previous Saturday night in Butterm+rket.street.—P.C. Delahay deposed to having seen the defendant in a drunken condition quarrelling with hie wife near to Scotland-road. Whilst witness V/Re going towards him he struck his wife with his fist in the face, the force of the blow knocking her against an adjacent wall. When risked why be so 111-treated the woman defendani re- plied "She is my wife, and I will do what I like with her." Witness pushed him away from her and tried to induce him two or three times to go home, but he refu.ed. Ile was accordingly taken into custody.—The Chief Constable r, markei that defendant had been bailed out on Sunday morning, and he believed a reconciliation had taken place between them.—Defendant had been to Manchester races and lost money.—Complainant came forward and said defendant had not hurt her. She hoped the bench would be lenient with him.—Defendant was ordered to pay 10s. to the poor box and then discharged. TUYSDAY.—Before Mr.. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. E. J.</p>
        <p>Guest and Dr. T. Starkey Smith. BEGGING.—Thomas Rose, a stranger, pleaded guilty to begging in Church-street, on the previous night. P.C. Clr ugh said there were three men together begging. Two of them ran away. The Chief Constable said there were a great many complaints at the present time about people begging in ihe streets. The prisoner was evidently an habitual vagrant. Prisoner was committed to gaol for seven days hard labour. A DESERTER,—John Johnson was charged with being a deserter from the 14th Hussars, and was remanded to Strangeway's gaol to await e,cort. SCHOOL ATTENDS NCR CASES.--Joseph Caldwell, 152, Winwick-road. was charged with neglecting to send his daughter to sehool. Mr. A. Browne represented the defend- ant. Mr. W. Jones. school attendance officer, said that the attendance of the girl was very b ,cl. indeed. He admitted that she had been ill. Mr. Browne asked for a remand in order to obtain a dJetor's certificate and this was granted. Charles Higginson, 16, Ellesmere-street, was fined Is. 6d. for not sending his child regularly to school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>DEATH SENTENCES.</head>
        <p>DEATH SENTENCES.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Monday sentence of death was passed by Mr. Justice Collins on Edward O'Brien, 26 years of age, a labourer, for the murder at Liverpool in July of Sarah Jenkinson, a domestic servant to whom he was engaged; and on William O'Neill, labourer, for the murder at Liverpool in August of Stephen-Dougherty, a scavenger, by striking him with a brick on the head, and causing him to fall from his window, a distance of thirty feet, to the ground. At the Lincoln Assizes on Monday Hannah Wright, 23 years of age, a domestic serv'ant, was convicted of the murder of her illegitimate two-year-old child by drowning, but was recommended to mercy. Mr. Justice Day, in passing sentence of death, expressed his sympathy with the recommendation, and it will, as a matter of course, be accepted by the Home Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Government of the colony of Victoria is about to send, by a steamer which will leave Melbourne for Man- chester direct, a consignment of 500 carcases of mutton, which will be made over to the Lord Mayor for [distri- b ution among the poor of that city,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>• I . . • , I • • w – . • I. . • .•• – .. , .._. ___ .. – . – – ....... i • , ' • ' -- -- . – . – -- .. .- – _ ___.......--. -..- . . , .</head>
        <p>• I . . • , I • • w - . • I. . • .•• - .. , .._. ___ .. - . - - ....... i • , ' • ' -- -- . - . - -- .. .- - _ ___.......--. -..- . . , .</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TtlTSDAY.—Before Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman), Messrs. W. Long, and C. H. Broadbent. INTEMPERANCE.--lidWin Radcliffe was fined 53. including costs for being drunk and disorderly In Appleton on the 2n41.. November; Thomas Gordon for being drunk In Grappenall the 4th November 2s 64 casts ; Joseph Robinson, drunk and disorderly in Appitton on the 26th October, ss. including costs.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED ASSAHLT.—James Smith was charg.d with having on the 17th November, assaulted and beaten John Buckley in Whitley.—As defendant did not appear the case was adjourned cid Buckley's request to the next Court day. A Doo-Owstru FINED.—AIf red Harris was charged with having on ths Bth October, in Stockton Heat, kept a dog without a license; he was also charged with haviug on the same date allowed a dog to be at large.—The magistrates inflicted a penalty of 2s. 6d. and costs in the first case and dismissed the second. IRREGULAR SCHOOL ATTENDANCE —James Curbisblev was fined 2s. 6d. for not sending his child regularly to school.— Mr. F. A. Jenkins prosecuted. A VAGRANT.—Henry Shaw, a disreputable-looking charac- ter, was charged with having wandered abroad and slept in an outhouse on the 25th November, in Appleton. without means of assistance.—P.C. Brew said that he found the prisoner sleeping in a shed on the farm of Mr. Percival, on the 15th.—lie was fined 2s. 6d. or in default of payment seven days' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. John Brundrit (chairman) and Mr James Handley. . .</p>
        <p>OCCASIONAL LICENCE. —Mr. Charles Dutton, Wilson's Hotel, applied for an occasional licence to selt at an assembly ball to be held in the Public Hall on the 4th December. from 9 p.m. to 3 a.m.—Superintendant Okell said he had no ob- jection. provided the applicant confined it to persons attend- ing the ball and not to any outsiders. The Chairman said that was the Bole idea in granting the licence, it was not meant for out eiders.</p>
        <p>Scaom, ATTENDANCE CIS-Ea.—Edward Hague, boatman, and William Ellis, Surry-street, were each fined for neglect- ing to cause their children to attend school regularly.—Mr. T. Bowyer prosecute I. MAINTENANCE ORDER.—Sarnuel Lunt, engaged at the Manchester Ship Canal Company's docks, Salford. was sum- moned for deserting his wife, and an application fore main- tenance order was make against him. Defendant did bot appear.—The magistrates made an order for the defendant to contribute 12s 6d. per week. A COILDEALER IN TROUBLE.—Harry Rimmer, 37, Blantyre street, Runcorn, a coaldealer, was summoned on the informa- tion of William Dunlow Laird, chief inspector of weights and measures for Cheshire County, for seling coal in bags of less weight than that specified on the metal label attached to the sags.—Alfred Timmins, inspector of weights and measures, stated that on the 11th elovember he saw the defendant's cart hawking coal in Irwell-lane. He accompanied the cart to the depot in Waterloo-road, and of four bags Labelle i 561b5., he found that one contained 511b5. and three others 551bs. I each. In cross-examination by Mr. Allea, wh represented the defendant, witness admitted that one sack labelled one cwt. was one to. over-weight. On re-examination witness said there were three other bags which were under-weight,— Mr. Allen said he bad to admit that the sacks were under- weight. He submitted that it was carelessness. When the coal was weighed it was very wet and the sacks had absorbed the moisture, and the coal was consequently short weight.— Aline of ss. and 9s. 6d costs was imposed. CRUELTY TO CEILDREN,—A MOTHER SENT TO PRISON.— Mary Albiston, 4. Back Mill-st., Widnes, and Thomas Wood were summoned at the instance of Inapector M'Ateer. that on the 20th June and divers other dates, having the custody of certain children, viz., William. Elizabeth, Agnes; and Joseph Albiston, and Charles Wood Glllgitlmatel, did unlaw- fully and wilfully neglect such children in a manner likely to cause unnecessary suffering or injury to their health. Mr. Allen, solicitor, prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.G.C.— Wood did not appear.—Mr. Allen said it was a very bad case, and it was one in which the male defendant was not as much to blame as Mrs. Albiston. The woman in June lived at Mill Brow, Runcorn, with Wood. She had four children of her own, and also a child who was the son of the mart Wood. 'the Inspector on visiting the amuse found the children in a most neglected condition. Their bodies were dirty, and their heads tilled with vermin. The neglect was chiefly through want of soap and water. • There was neither furniture nor food in the house, and the children presented a woe-be-gone appearance. On the 25th of February she was sentenced to one month's imprisonment for neglecting the children, but it did not seem to have had a g.iorl effect.—ln- Inspector M'Ateer gave evidence of his visit on the 20th June. He found the children in a very bad state, and de- tailed their condition.—P.C.'s Gregory end Richardson also gave evidence.—The magistrates committed the prisoner to prison for one month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>FRODSH AM PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY,—Before Messrs. A. Thomas and T. Rigby.</p>
        <p>-- . - WORKING A HORS IN AN UNFIT STATE.—John D„ugdale farmer, Alvauley, was summoned at the instance of Inspector Luff of the e:ociety for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals for working his horse whilst in an unfit state. The case was proved by Inspector Luff, and the defendant, who pleaded guilty was fined 105. including costs. RIDING WITRouT REnss.—William Pletcher, farmer, Alvanley, was fined 108. and Ss. 61. costs or 14 days imprison- ment for riding without reins on a Cart drawn by a horse at Alvanley on the 19th inst. The Chairman characterised the practice as a very dAngerous one. AN INCORRIGIBLE.—AIice Gayter, Newtown, was snm- moned for not complying with a school attendance order, made on the 30th ult. It was pointed out that the lad was 10 years of age, and had only been present at school once since the order was made. It was the wish of his mother and of the School Attendance Committee to get him away. He set his sister's hair on fire a day or two ago, and previous to that be ignited the window blinds.—Mr. Williams said he attended at the request of the School Attendance Committee to ask the Bench to step out of the ordinary course if possible. The defendant was a Rohinson Crusoe, Jack blieppard sort of I lad, and was utterly beyond his mother's control.—A fine of is. was imposed, to enable the Bench to send the lad to an Industral School on the next occasion when the matter was brought before them. CRUELTY To HORSES. --At the instance of the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Anima's, George Fernley, 29, Hamilion-st , Warrington, and John Melia, 11, James-st, Warrington, were summoned for cruelty to two horses: by working them whilst in an unfit state, on the 7th inst.. at Prodsham. and Joseph Mounfield, sen., Warrington. and Arthur Mounfield, Marsh-House-lane, Wallington, were also summoned for causing them to be so worked.—Sergeants Clays and Hunt. and James Jackson, of Dunham-olh-11111, proved the charges. It was stated that Fernley and Melia were stopped in Main-st , Prodsham. in charge of two horses attached to a furniture van. The animals were very much distressed. The bly mare in the shafts had a sore on the back under the saddle, about the size of a shilling. It was In poor condition. Matter was on the lining of the saddle. The grey mare in the chains had a sore on the left side as large as the palm of the hand, and the chain Was rubbing the sore. The two wounds on the let. shoulder were as large as a shilling, and one on the off-shoulder as big as a florin. The load it was said, was too heavy for them.— In- spector Luff spoke to having since seen the horses,and found wounds as stated ty the previous witnesses.—Sergeant Chantler, of Warrington, gave corroborative evidence.—For the defence Pearnly and Melia stated that the animals were all right when they left home. They did net consider the load too heavy for them.—Joseph Mounfield stat?d that he looked after the horses Rs well as he oossibly could. He had not the slightest wi:h that his horse should be overworked.— Arthur Mounfield, owner of one of the horses, stated that the horses were in good condition, and he was sorry to differ wit la the officers. Whatever was found wrong with hi. ani- mal must have happened during the journey to Mold. Everything was don t to enable the drivers to secure the well-being of the ant Is. the men in charge being given money to obtain assistance, proper food, and good stabliug.— The magistrates fined Fearuley and Melia 10s. and 1, s. 3d. costs each, or one month's imprisonment, and Joseph and Arthur Mounfield 20s. fold 18s. 4&amp; costs esc..or in default one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>SHIPBUILDERS' DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>THE SHIPBUILDERS' DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>It was stated in Glasgow on Tuesday that the compact ' between the Clyde and Belfast master engineers had been extended officially to embrace the North-east of England and Barrow employers. The London Trades Council,with which are affiliated some 150 societies with a membership of nearly half a million,on Tuesday.issued an appal on behalf of the engineers involved in the dis- pute on the Clyde and at Belfast The appeal describes the dispute as "an organised attempt on the part of i the employers to crush trade unionism n this country."</p>
        <p>Green tea, although prepared from young leaves as supposed, owes its colour to the method employed in its preparation, of treating the leaves with a mixture of Prussian blue,turrnorie root, and gypsum, sometim es ctf indigo and copperas. All tea drinks black, and the Chinese never dye tea for their own consumption. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>( I 80. G. HUSSEY ,t CO., are the leading makArs of appli- ances for Football, Hockey. Golf, Lacrosse and all Sports and Games,also for Gymnasium and Physical Exercises. Illustrated Catalogue may be obtained from Local Dealers or the SPORTS MAN yip...crony. London ILE.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>These two clubs met on Saturday for the first time under the auspices of the Northern Rugby Union, at Wilderspool. Neither club had been doing very weillin the League though Oldham had by far the better record, but as they are prime favourites at Warringtdn, there was a gate of upwards of 7,000 to witness the match. It is a singular circumstance that Oldham, whenever they meet Warrington, are almost unvariably able to placiatheir strongest team on the field,and Saturday was. no exception, for the team was that which has done such yeoman service for the club for several years past. More than 1,000 spectators hailed from Oldham, and conse- quently each team had plenty of supporters to shout when anything was accomplished. Mr. Smith,of Widnes, was the referee,and the follewing were the players for the 'respective clubs : Warrington : Heesom, full back; Barber, Cross, Bur- ton, and Broady, threequarters ; Nevins and Foden, half backs ; Burrows, Thorniley, Thornton, Hill, Fairhurst, Andrews, Barrett, and Houghton, forwards. Oldham ; Eagland, full back ; Hurst, Taylor, S. Lees, and McCutcheon, threequarters ; Varley. and A. Lees, half backs ; Bonsor, J. Lees, Wyllie, Etchull, Merrill, Childs, Furness, and Edwards, forwards. There was a pretty strong breeze blowing in the direction of the pavilion, and consequently when Valley won the toss, he set Warrington to defend that goal. Hill kicked off and Merrill failing to ret in his kick, he was promptly floored, and the first scrimmage took place in the visitors' quarters. This visit, however, proved to be only a flyine one, for Varley early showed his agility by securing from the scrimmage and running beyond neutral territory and his final kick vcry nearly ended disastrously for Warrington. Heesom failing to reply. A scrimmage took place on the Warrington goal :ine,and some anxious moments were experienced, but the dan- ger was at length averted, and safer quarters were reached. Oldham, however, withstood all the attempts of Warrington to break away and kept them penned in their own portion of the field, hut a free kick falling to IcCutcheon was only poorly taken advantage of, the oall being screwed into touch. 9 good pass by Foden to Burton at length enabled the latter to reach half way, and though Oldham quickly regained their advantage, Burton by intercepting a pass from Varley to Taylor, again relieved Warrington. The attack was immedi- ately resumed, and at length a minor was conceded by Warrington. Broady's kick out was too strongly returned by Varley, and another minor was conceded. Broady again kicked off, and the Oldham backs immedi- ately commenced their well-known passing tactics with the result that Hurst get over, but as the final pass from Taylor to himself was forward, the point was not allowed. From the ensuing scrimmage, Arthur Lees was very dangerous, and 13( ughton was instrumental just in time in preventing a score. This let-off was only very short, for from a scrimmage near the quarter line, Varlev shined and passed to Sam Lees, the latter parted to McCutcheos, and he by an irresistible run got over at the corner, Flees= being just too late to pre- vent the score. Eagland's attempt at goal was coin- pletely "off." From the kick out, Arthur Lees made a splendid dribble,and had thehnisf ort ne to send theball in touch in goal. Another round of passing ensued between Varley, Sam Lees, and McCutcheon, and Broady was only just in time in preventing a nem This was fol- lowed by a drop at goal by Sam Lees, which just failed to come off,and another minor was conceded. Following the drop out, Hill broke away finely, but his pass to Thornton was a shade forward, and thus a good chance of relief was lost. Another round of passing between Varley, Arthur Lees, Taylor, and Hurst gave the latter an opportunity of getting over, but he was too anxious to score in a favourable position, and in attempting to get from the corner flag to behind the goal posts,he went beyond the dead ball line, and only a minor was allowed to his intense chagrin. Burton and Cross managed to afford a little relief,but another round of passing among the Oldham backs ended in McCutcheon scoring a second , try, which Sam Lees was unable to augment. A short ' spell of uneventful play followed this, until Burrows by a dribble found the centre.and Nevins by a well directed kick caused the Oldham lines to be invaded. Nothing , further was scored before half time, when Oldham were , leading by two tries to nil. Varley restarted the game, and the Warrington for- wards followed up the returnfso well that they were soon on the offensive. A fine round of passing between Nevins, Foden, Burton, and Cross might have brought disaster to Oldham had Cross been able to give a good pass to Barber. As it was he passed wildly and the ball went in touch. Following this,the Warrington forwards beaded by Hill, Fairhurst,and Thornton took the ball to the Oldham line, and Eagland was tackled before he could get the ball away. Burrows and Fairhurst each came within an ace of scoring. Grand passing between the Oldham backs at length relieved the minds of the Oldhamers. Warrington again returned to the attack, and Thornton charging down Taylor's kick was enly tackled in time. A moment later a fine dribble by Bur- rows,Thorniley,and Thornton took the hall over the line, and the last mentioned player had very hard lines indeed In not scoring, for he was only just beaten by Ragland for possession of the ball. Following the drop out flee- seam made his mark,and Hurst fa ling to return the ball, it fell in touch near the line, and from the ensuing line out Peden made his mark, Burton made a good attempt at the goal, but just failed ; and Sam Lees securing the ball, he staved off the danger, but he was too well watched by Barber to get the ball very far away. Hill was again prominent by a breakaway, but he found the way too securely blocked to get through, and Oldham now paid a visit to the Warrington quarter, hut they were soon compelled to beat a retreat: During the final stages of the game, Wirrington showed up sur- prisingly, and it is astonishing however the Oldham lines were kept intact. Burton during the last three minut s had hard lines indeed, and had he had the slightest amount of luck, he might have pulled the game out of tha fire. An attempt at goal from a very diffimlt angle missed the mark by a few inches only, and the following minute, by a grand dribble he got the ball ov• r the line defeating all the Oldham players. Unluckily just before he could ground the ball, it went over the dead ball line, eknd only a minor was allowed. Had these two scores which were all but made, been successful, Warrington would of course have won, but as they did not, and as the whistle blew immediately afterwards, the game ended in favour of Oldham by two tries to nil. Oldham were undoubtedly the better team on the day's play, the department they were greatly superior to Warrington in being at half back. The Oldham forwards were also superior in heeling out the ball, and thus they afforded their backs a better opportunity of securing it, and in- dulging in the passing for which they are justly famous.</p>
        <p>=Tball</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN v. STOCKPORT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>There would be between four and five thou- sand people present at Stockport on Saturday when the teams entered the enclosure. Stock- port played their strongest team, while Runcorn were short of G. Robinson and Moores, which caused w. Faulkner to take up the vacant position at centre,and F. Richardson took Mo,re's place in the forwards. Stock- port won the toss, which gave them the advantage of the strong wind blowing from goal to goal. Farmer started for Runcorn, and early on the visiting forwards forced the home team to adopt defensive tactics, and Allen was penalizad on three or four occasions for off- side tactics. Stockport relieved the pressure by kicking, Saville gaining ground, which caused the Runcorn de- fence a lot of trouble, but Warder was particularly smart. Passing by the Runcorn backs eased the pres- sure, and J. Faulkner broke away finely, and exchanges between W. Faulkner, Myers, and W. Gayter nearly endad in disaster for the borne team. A moment later the Runcorn forwards wheeled the scrimmage, and the ball was taken to the Stockport line, and Myers scored between the posts,but an infringement for off-ship nega- tived the point. From the free kick which followed Jolley missed the ball, and things looked ominous for Runcorn until Warder dashed acrcss and got the ball to touch. Runcorn again got down, and an injudicious kick by Bailey sent the ball to the Runcorn thrPequar- ters, who fairly went for the line, and looked certain scorer.. when Robinson intercepted and saved a certain try. Stockport now had a turn, and after pressing Run- corn were compelled to kick dead. The interval arrived with a clean sheet. On resuming, it was thought that with ths wind Runcorn would quickly score, hut Stock- port played up very strongly, and made no mistake in their tackling. There was certainly a slackness in the play of the Runcorn forwards,and they did not pack with anything like their usual combination, Stockport rushing them on several occasions. The game was keenly and closely fought, and was mostly scrimmages until well in the second moiety, when Warder made a ;fine run, and Rotting to Saville he drew him to the touch line. J. Faulkner was close up, and had the ;line at his mercy, but Warder passed wildly, and an easy chance was missed. The ga--....e-eventually ended in a draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WIDNES v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>At Whines, on Saturday, before 2.500 specta- tors. Brighouse were short of two of their usual team. The visitors kicked off, and at once and pressed, but midfield play followed, after which Brennan punted up the field. Clarke made a mull near the line, and smart passing between the Rangers' for- wards was beautifully foiled by Drummond. At the other end Abbey executed some fine saves, but Widnes failed in front of goal. There was a series of attacks concentrated at the other end, but a stern resistence was offered, and the local team quickly carried, by smart passing. hostilities to the Rangers' 25, where Wadding- ton kicked into touch. Foster grassed that player the next moment, but Abbey received a pass and removed the danger. By good passing Brooke got into danger- ous quarters,and Hughes receiving his pass got through, thus registering the only try of the match. Roused by this reverse, Widnes came along at a rush, but the stiff breeze was a powerful factor to deal with, and progress</p>
        <p>was slow. Both teams endeavoured to gain something further, but up to bat time there was no mor e inceare The second talf was characterised by a vigorous attack on the Rangers' goal. Widnes in this half had by far the greater part of the play,and it was with the greatest luck that Brighouse kept their hold intact. For the last 20 minutes Widnes pressed continually. Result : Brighouse, one try (three points) ; Widnes. nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>YORKSHIRE v. CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE v. CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Under the Northern Union auspices these teams met on the Headingley Ground, Leeds, on Monday. The ground was very soft, and the attendance numbered not more than 2,000. Yorkshire at once pressed, and Firth ran over, Cooper placing a goal. Then Saville landed a goal from a penalty, whilst Worsley scored an unconverted try, and Saville added another penalty. Half-time score: Cheshire, twe penalty goals and one try ; Yorkshire, one goal. With the advantage of the wind It was expected that Yorkshire would make a bet- ter show in the second half, but Cheshire quite held their own, Saville and the three-quarter backs tackling splendidly. Cooper and Firth made great efforts to cross, but without avail. A splendid contest ended, there, a victory for Cheshire. Final score : Cheshire, 2 goals, 1 try ; Yorkshire, 1 goal. Yorkshire, C. E. Lori- mer (Manningham (back) ' • F. W. Cooper (Bradford),W. H. Keeping:, and F. Firth (Halifax), and A. Boothroyd (Huddersfield), three-quarter backs ; A. Rigg (Halifax) and R. Wood (Liversedge), half-backs; R. E. Sugden (Brighouse Rangers), 0. Walsh (Hunslet), M. Sutcliffe (Huddersfield), W. Sugden (Bradford),R. Greig (Leeds), W. Walton (Wakefield), J. Riley (Halifax), and A. Bar- raclough (Manningham), forwards. Cheshire : Saville (Stockport), back; Warder (Runcorn), Worsley (Stock- port). and Robinson and Myers (Runcorn), three-quarter backs ; J. Faulkner (Runcorn), and Norris (Stockport), half-backs ; W. Faulkner, Langley, Farmer and Taylor (Runcorn), Wrigley, Gibson, Mottershead, and Morton (Stockport), forwards.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A" V. OLDHAM "A."—The match be- tween the above teams was played at Oldham on Satur- day, before a fair number of spectators. The teams placed on the field were :—Warrington : Full back, Buck- ley ; thteequarters, Carey, Scott, Lawless, and Foster; half-backs, O'Brien and Price ; forwards, Boyle, Monks, Swift, Hampton, Hughes, Scholtze, and Taylor. Old- ham : Full back, Lanionby; threequarters, Knott, Fil- lingha m, Bolton, and C. Bradbury ; half-backs, Rhodes and Morton ; forwards, Harrison, Neild, Sellars, Phillip, Whittle, Cunningham, and W. Bradbury. Warrington played a man short all through the game, and up to half-time Oldham were without the services of C. and W. Bradbury. o:dham kicked off against the wind and the ball was seon returned to centre, where it was kept for some time, neither side being able to break away from the scrimmages. Knott at last managed to break away but had not gone far before he lost the ball. Rhodes got possession and passed to Knott, who got dangerously near the line before he was thrown in touch by several Warrington men. Rhodes had an- other try and failed, but Morton getting possession from a scrimmage, rushed through the pack and was on the line before he was held. For a con- siderable portion of the first half, Warrington had hard work to repulse the repeated attacks by Knott and Rhodes, both of whom were near scoring several times, but the condition of affairs was altered by the Warrington team rushing the hall to Oldham quarters, where they continued to press, until Rhodes and Neild relieved and took the ball back to the Warrington goal line, where Rhodes was stopped by Buckley. From that point up to half time Warrington pressed, but the whistle blew with neither side scoring. On resuming,Oldham were joined by the two Bradbury's and this made a great difference in the play, as they travelled along the field with long strides, making tack- ling difficult. Some good passing was started by Rhodes and after the ball had passed through the hands of Bol- ton to Fillingham, the latter passel to Charles Bradbury who scored a try. Lamonby tried for a goal but the ball went too far. Warrington were set to work to defend their goal and they were in danger for some time until Lawless relieved by some fine passieg, assisted by Taylor (and one or two others. Morton and Whittle attempted to get over the line with the ball, failing however, but the attempt assisted Fillingham in scoring his try immediately after. Bolton took the kick for goal and missed, but to the disgust of the Warrington team the referee allowed him to have another try, because Warrington charged toe soon The second attempt was successful, the crowd cheering lustily. Oldham made frequent attempts to sc,,re again, but the Warrington defence was a strong one, and they managed to keep their opponents from scoring any further. The game ended,with the score,Oldham one goal one try (8 points) to nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN "A "V. STOCKPORT" A."—At Runcorn on Saturday. A late start was made,the result being that the latter portion of the game was played in semi-dark- ness. The visitors started the ball. and West in return- ing had his kick charged down, but, regaining the ball, raced down the field and passed to Dunbavin who punted to the centre. Stockport immediately got in the home half, where the game was stopped for a few minutes owing to Butterworth being hurt. After a scrimmage Stockport looked like scoring,but the defence was sound and the homesters by good dribbling got out of danger. Flere Runcorn were awarded a free kick for off-side play, sad Dunbavin tried for goal, but the ball went wide. Play was located in mid-field for a while, till the home- sters were awarded another free kick, which, however, did not gain much ground. Runcorn, with a series of rushes gat into the visitors' half,and forced them to touch down. Lord was soon afterwards kurt,and had to leave the field.- Play was now uninteresting and half time arrived with no score. After the interval, Runcorn pressed and got well in the Stockport half by good for- ward play, by Wilson and Shaw. Butterworth soon afterwards found touch near the visitors' goal line by a long kick which was loudly cheered. Richardson fol- lowed a kick up, and getting possession raced over the line,but the ball was brought back,and scrimmaged near the centre. Immedia ely afterwards West got well away, and was collared,but passed to Richardson, who scored. Dunbavin took the kick,but the ball went wide. Run- corn now had all the play,and made desperate efforts to inci ease the score, but the whistle sounded soon after, leaving the result—Runcorn s• A " one try; Stockport .6</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>AS IOCIATION.</head>
        <p>AS IOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WESTON V. RUNCORN.—This match was played at Weston on Saturday. Weston kicked off, and soon scored a goal but Runcorn equalized and scored two more before half time, which arrived with the score—Runcorn, three ; Weston, one. In the second half Runcorn added a further goal after nice passing. Weston then pressed, and playing well together scored three more goals, and the game resulted in a draw of four goals each.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER WANDERERS V. ST. ELPHIN'S.—The matches between the above clubs have always been pro- ductive of a stiff encounter,and the game at Manchester was no exception to this rule. St Elphin's turned up with their best possible team, and the Wariderere were also well represented. In the first half the Saints,though playing against the wind, soon showed their superiority their passing and kicking :leaving nothing to be desired. After doing meet of the pressing the visitors crossed over, with the score—St. Elphin's 2; Wanderers, 1. With the wind behind them the Saints were much the better team and though the Manchester goal had several near shaves, it was only captured once, the final score being—St. Elphin's, 3 ;Wanderers, 1. Tnrner, Dugdale, and Duck- worth scored the goals for the Saints. _ .</p>
        <p>Rugby RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. PENLYTH.—This match was played at Runcorn on Saturday, before a good number of spectators. Shortly afecr three o'clock the respective organisations appeared, the following representing Run- corn :—Goal, Bough ; full backs, Bloor and Carter ; half backs, Pye, Payton, Burrows ; forwards, W. Walker, Jones, Blylie,S.Walker,And Peacock. The home skipper won the toss, and Penketh started against the wind. Paton secured and sent to his forwards, who soon were pretty near to the visitors goal and the Walkers soon secured a goal each. Penketh now did their utmost.but were seldom able to break through the home defence,Car- ter and Blom- playing safely, Carter by a huge kick sent up field. Pye secured and sent in a beautiful dropping shot, which quite heat the Penketh custodian. Ralf time result : Runcorn,3 goals ; l'enketh,nil. Baylie restarted but could not make much headway, the wind being al- most a gale,but Paton and Pye worked hard,and brought relief time after time. A long shot was put in the Blues' quarters, Bloor heading back, and the custodian, in en- deavouring to clear,sent the ball against the upright,and through the goal. This success gave Penketh a little more dash,and the Stripes' inside right soon obliged with a real scorcher from a good centre by Smith. Penketh POW forced corners in abundance but could not put it between the posts, and had exceedingly hard lines. Smith now had a pop and the affair looked all over a goal, but his partner nrideavouring to make the goal sure, lifted the ball over the bar. The equalising goal, however, soon followed during a bulley in goal. Play was now hard and fast, both teams indulging in ques- tionable tactics. Final result, Runcorn, 3 ; Penketh 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ASRAN TEE WAR.</head>
        <p>THE ASRAN TEE WAR.</p>
        <p>The Ashantee Envoys left London on Tuesday night for Liverpool where they embarked on Wednes- day on board the steamer Roquelle for Cape Coast Castle. Mr. Chamberlain has written to Mr. Sutherst stating that he sees no advantage in continuing the correspondence on the subject of the reception of the Envoys. 'the issue, he says, rests with king Prempeh, as to whose intention he has at present no means of judging with certainty. He adds that infor- mation received from other sources tends to throw doubt upon the attitude of submission latterly adopted by the Envoys, as not being in harmony with that assumed by them at an earlier period of their stay in this country. A detachment of the Scots Guards left Windsor for Aldershot on Wednesday to join the Ashanti expeditiln. Prior to leaving Windsor they were received by the Queen, who in a briof address wished them God-speed and assured them that her prayers would go with them. According to an Accra telegram of Wednesday the king of Coomassie is still defiant.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHTTIICIT.</p>
        <p>Special services were held in the above church on Sunday, and collections were taken in aid of the Sunday schools. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. H. V. Pigot, and in the evening the Rev. S. Wilkinson, vicar of Timperley, who preached to a large congregation. He expressed the pleasure it afforded him to see his old friends at St. Paul's once more. It had been a great comfort to him, as it was no doubt now to their Vicar,to have such faith- ful superintendents and teachers who Sunday after Sunday devoted themselves to the education of the little ones. He hoped they would be spared for many years to work in the parish. _ _ _</p>
        <p>If °lrakHold, LINE , hemmed and marked free of charge. and W. Dutton, Sankey-s.. Warrington.— Adnt. WARRINGIONAVOLIINTEEIt'S BALL. . . .</p>
        <p>The annual non-commissed officers' ball in con- nection with the Ist Volunteer Battalion Prince of Wales Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) was held on Monday evening, in the drill Hall. Danc- ing, the music for which was supplied by the band of the regiment, under the direction of Bandmaster Armstrong, commenced at 5.30, and was kept up with spirit until two o'c:ock on the following morn- ing. There were about 200 persons present. The officers in attendance w3re Colonel Ridgway, Cap- tains Appleton, Ridgway, Fairclough, and Lieuten- ants Fairclough and Fletcher. The room had been very tastefully decorated for the occasion by Ser- geant Hesketh and others. Refreshments were sup- plied by Mr. Headland, the caretaker of the hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Si. PAUL'S PCYPITLAR CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>Si. PAUL'S PCYPITLAR CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening one of the series of penny popular concerts in connection with the Parish of St. Paul's was given in the Infants' Schoolroom, Hendrick-street. The chair was occupied by Coun- cillor Smethurst who contributed solos as also did Miss Minnie Smethurst and Mr. Ratcliffe. Violin solos and duets were contributed by the ,ilisses Par- sonage, Lee, and Pierpoint, and a pianoforte sole by Miss Parsonage. The accompaniments were played by Mrs. Pigot and the Misses Pierpoint and Bessie Chantry. There was a good attendance, and a very enjoysble evening was spent.</p>
        <p>LAMPS —J.S Garland Iroginonger.Warringtou. BANK-sTREET MISSION. A soiree was held on Tuesday evening in the As- sembly Rooms at the Conservative Club, in aid of the Bank-street Mission. There was a good atten- dance, which included the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), Mrs. and Miss Willis, the Rev. A. A. Mark- ham, Miss Pierpoint, Councillor Woods, and Cap- tains Buckton and Appleton. Mr. Percy Randall's band was in attendance, and dancing was indulged in until an early hour on the following morning. The M.C. was Alderman Wright, and the proceed- ings passed off in a very satisfactory manner under the management of Messrs. A. Simkin and W. Bennett. BrvaNG MacrancEs, Hand £2 ISs. 6d, Ireadle £4 4s.</p>
        <p>war hAndsome walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON', 16 and 18, Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt. BIICICLEY-STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Monday night an entertainment consisting of limelight views, &amp;c., was given in the above school, after which a presentstion was made to Mr. Simpson. by the members of his Young Men's Bible Class. 51r. J. Adams,in making the presentation on behalf of the class, referred to the capabilities of their teacher,and hoped Mr. Simpson would receive the present as a token of the respect and esteem in which he was held by his scholars, his daily life Veing to them and others a guide to Christianity. Mr. Simpson, in responding, said it gave him great pleasure to know that his class held him in such esteem, end thanked them from his heart for the beautiful picture presented to him. The presenta- tion took the form of a photograph of hi scholars, and every credit must be given to the photo- grapher, Mr. Wilkinson, for tte manner in which he had completed his work.</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOLD Lin ENS. hemmed and marked free of chair —3. andW. DurroN,y-st., Sankey Warrington.—Advt. N.S.P.C.C. BALL Al' TICE LION HOTEL.</p>
        <p>The annual ball in aid of the funds of the War- rington and District Branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children was held on Monday evening, in the Assembly Room at the Lion Hotel, when there was a good attendance, which included the Mayor and Mayoress,Councillor and Mrs. Fairclough. The music for dancing, which commenced at 8.30 and was continued until 1.30, was supplied by Mr. R. It Pearson's band. The decorations, which were much admired, had been attended to Messrs. S. E. and R. Johnson,of Bridge- street. The refreshments were supplied by Mrs. Taylor, manageress of the hotel, and they, as well as the ball arrangements generally, gave much satisfaction. (mg.—GARLAND, Importer, 1 ;51,1 Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO CONSTABLE MILL/NGTON, LATCHk ORD.</p>
        <p>After the ordinary business of the Daresbury Petty Sessions was concluded,on Tuesday,the chair- man of the Bench (Mr..). C. Parr). presented Con- stable Millington, late officer for the Latchford dis- trict, with a purse of £2O in gold which had been subscribed for by the inhabitants of his neighbour- hood.—Mr.Parr said that it was his pleasant duty to make Constable Millington a presentation or testi- monial, for his good conduct, and the satisfactory manner in which he had conducted the business of the neighbourhood of Latchford and Grappenhall. Be must say that Constable Millington during his stay had earned the confidence of all parties by his impartality and the firm manner in which he car- ried out the duties asssigned to him and they hoped that hOwould carry with him the best wishes of the neighbourhood,and succeed in his new position. —P.C. Millington responded, and said the present would give him something to think about in the future, and he was thankful to Mr. Parr and the other magistrates for making the presentation, and he extended his gratitude to the subscribers generally. SEWING MACHINES Hand £2 10a.tTreadle £4 4s with handiome w,dnut coyer.—J. and W. Durrox, 16 and 18, Saakey-st., Warrington.—Advt. ST. PETER'S CHURCIT.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, in the John-st. School, the members of the Young Women's Bible Class of St. Peter's Church gave a service of song entitled " Alone in London." This class, consisting of up- wards of 80 members, is conducted by Mrs. Perci- val, who had carefully trained the members to give this entertainment, the object of which was to pro- cure prizes to distribute for good attendance on Sunday. The attendance was good,the room being tilled, and the audience showed their interest in the piece by quiet behaviour and close attention. Mrs. Percival was the reader, and Mrs. Pendlebury presided at the piano. A magic lantern, manipu- lated by Mr. Downes, illustrated the progress of the story. The hymns were well chosen, and their ren- dering was greatly admired. The solo and duet parts were well sustained by the following young ladies :—Misses A. Harper, Holbrook, Wells and Lawless. At the conclusion the Vicar said be was pleased that a service like this should be so thoroughly appreciated by that large assembly, for after all true religion was the most lasting of all joys. It alone produced lasting happiness. He I proposed votes of thanks to Mr. H. Paylor,who had I lent the slides. to Mr. Downes, who manipulated, Ito Mrs. Pendlebury for presiding at the piano, to I Mrs. Percival for reading, and to the girls for sing- ing, and lastly to the audience for good listening. All these votes of thanks having been heartily responded to, the National Anthem closed a most enjoyable entertainment.</p>
        <p>• - Szwrzna MACHINES, Hand £2 18a. 6d. Traadle £4 4. With handsome walnut cover.-7. and W. DUTTON. 16 and 18. Sankev-st., Watrington.—Advt. SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
      </div>
