      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>iLtlttro to tin Mitor.</head>
        <p>iLtlttro to tin Mitor.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED WARRINGTON CATTLE MARKET.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am glad that my temerity in suggesting a site for a Warrington Cattle Market has at least had the effect of drawing public attention to the question, and if, as one of your correspondents remarks, the Corporation waits for public opinion to declare itself before beginninx operations,it may now have an opportunity of becoming acquainted with some of the opinions prevailing in the town on the matter. Both correspondents last week, seem to prefer the Old Militia Barracks as a place for holding the horse and cattle fairs. To this,the residents in the vicinity have a decided objection,and expressed it at the last municipal election, for the cardinal question before the electors of St. Austin's ward was certainly this vexed question of the utilisation of the Barracks as a cattle market ; and their returned representative is stoutly opposed to any such thing. To the site I pro- posed in Froghall-lane in the previous letter, the chief objection raised against it was its size. But I am yet to be convinced that it is not larger and quite as suitable, if not more so, on account of its surround- ings, which I have already pointed out, than the Old Barracks' yard. For drainage purposes—a very impor- tant matter—it is certainly better than the Barracks which lie low and the sewers often flooded from the river. Expenditure would, of course, be entailed in Levelling, and boarding the Froghall side, but some expenditure would be required at the Old Barracks, and the residents on the premises would in all likelihood re- move, and that income would be lost. As to the long unpaved road near the Barracks mentioned in" Tosh Dayfrank's " letter, as suitable for " trials of speed and action," the writer cannot surely mean Slutcher's-lane where a level railway crossing would have to be traversed, but if it is Museum-street he refers to, it should be remembered that a day school Li there, and furthermore that the vacant land near to, may before long, be built upon. But apart from all these considerations, it would be a mistake to establish a cattle market in the immediate vicinity of a residential quarter of the town. We hear sometimes remarks about leading townsmen residing in the cauntry districts, but if we are to keep them in the borough it will be necessary to have residential districts free from such a nuisance as a cattle market would certainly be.—Yours faithfully, Whitecross Ward.</p>
        <p>J. H.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PISTOL FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE PISTOL FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>REPORT OF THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Brighouse, county coroner, on Saturday morning held on inquest in the Police Parade Room, Irlam-street, to elicit the circumstances attending the death of Percy Gandeewhich occurred on Thursday after- noon. The Chief Constable was present on behalf of the police, mid Mr. Petford, manager of the works where deceased was employed, also watched the pro• - ceedings. Mr. John Gandy, grocer, of 23, Church-street, said he was the father of the deceased youth, who was sixteen years of age,and who, at the time of his death was em- ployed as consigning clerk at the bedstead works of Messrs. Monks. Hall, and Co., School-brow. He had seen his son alive, in good health and apparently cheer- ful, at home about 1.45 on Thursday afternoon. Witness asked him if it was not time for him to return to work, but the youth said he had left the office a little later , that day than usual,and was consequently not due at the I office just then. He, however, put the book he was reading elide, and went off to work. Only a few minutes seemed to have elapsed when a clerk from the works entered he shop and informed him that his son was in- jured. Witness went immediately to the works, and found his son lying upon the floor, near to the telephone Chamber. He was breathing very heavily, and was un- conscious. Replying to the Coroner,wittaess said he had not the slightest idea that his son possessed the pistol produced. His son had never said or done anything which would lead him (witness) to think that he would take away his life. He was a cheerful lad. John Winstanley, clerk, engaged in Messrs. Monks, Hall, and Co's bedstead works, School-brow, testified to having known the deceased for twelve months. He had seemed to be cheerful in his position and had never, to witness's knowledge, either said or acted in a manner likely to make one believe that he would take away his life. The pistol produced witness had seen before the day on which deceased was shot. He had heard firing in the yard, but did not know at what the deceased had been firing. Witness saw deceased come into the office on Thursday afternoon, from dinner, pass into the tele- phone office, and two minutes afterwards he heard a shot. He ran out to know the cause of the report, and found the youth lying in the telephone chamber upon the floor, bleeding from a wound over the right ear. Witness at once communicated with the police authori- ties, and also sent for medical assistance. Dr.M'Carthy came, as also did P.C.Hollineworth. When witness heard the report it would be about the time when the youth had gone to change his coat in order to resume I work. The pistol was lying by the deceased's side. Frederick Platt, a lad of about the same age as deceased and employed at the same place, deposed that he had known the latter ever since they went to school together. He was aware that he had a toy pistol and had had it two or three weeks. He had seen Gandy firing at pig-iron in the works' yard with it. He always bad the pistol with him in his waistcoat pocket. On Thursday he passed witness, and said something as he did so. Witness could not remember anything of what be said except the word "shooting." Deceased thee went into the telephone chamber, and almost simultane- ously with his entrance was heard the shot of the pistol. Deceased's office coat always hung in the telephone closet, and he usually changed it there before going to , dinner and after coming back. The Coroner (to Mr. John Gandy) :" What was your son reading, sir, before leaving home? Mr. Gandy : "Pickwick," I believe. The Coroner : Was that the class of literature he had been in the habit of reading? Mr. Gandy: He had been in the habit ef reading papers like Answers," the " Golden Penny," and I think other little things of that kind ; also hooks from the Library. But the honk he was readieg at the time I saw him at home was' Pickwick," and he seemed to be enjoying it much. Some time ago he had been reading little stories which I disapproved of, and I have tried to break him off this and persuaded him to read something MOTO healthy. By the Chief Constable to witness: I do not know whether the deceased used blank cartridges or not. I have fired the pistol myself once or twice. I did not know that had taken the balls out of cartridges. P.C. Hollingsworth said that he was called into the office of the works on School Brow.alsout 3.30 on Thurs- day, and found the deceased lying on his back with his feet inside the office and the rest of hie body in the tele- hone box. There was a small wound just over the right ear. He received the pistol from Dr. M'Carthy and it had a spent cartridge in it. The weapon could net be loaded unless the trigger was at " full cock," and when the breach was put back the pistol remained at "full cack." The Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot) observed that almost the very shock of closing the breech would " send it off." The Coroner said that the jury would, of course, be satisfied that the death of the youth had bsen caused by a gun-shot wound, but asked them if they were pre- pared to say in what condition he was when he received that wound. The facts were that the yurith had this Pistol, which was a very dangerous weapon indeed. The Letrielature had tried, by introducing a Bill to regulate the sale of such pistols, but he believed it had been "blocked "by some members of Parliament. the same as many other useful measures had been. He thought the evidence showed that the deceased had not got into a morbid state of mind by reading unhealthy novels but _</p>
        <p>Was in the habit of readin.• first-class literature like "Pickwick." At the name time it was somewhat re- markable that deceased should go into the office and make use of the word shooting,and almost immediately a shot was beard, and before he had changed his coat. If it had occurred in tha yard, where he hadibeen in the habit of firing at pig-iron, one would have been inclined to say that it was accidental. If they could consMently say that the wound was accidental Ist them say so ; but if they did not think that they would be justified in returning such a verdict then they might say that he died from a pistol shot wound, but that there was no evinence to show how it was inflicted. After a short private consultation the foremen of the jury said that he and his fellow jurymen were agreed that the youth had come by his death through misad- venture. They at the same time were wishful that the Coroner should express their sympathy with the parents, and that a rider be added to their verdict to the effect that the indiscriminate sale of toy pistols ought to be subject to legislation. The Coroner then added that he wished personally to join with the jury in their expression of sympathy with Mr. Gandy and his family in their sad bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LYMIT.</head>
        <p>LYMIT.</p>
        <p>AN INTOXWATED DRIVEL—At the Lymm Police Court, on Friday, before Dr, Brabazon (in the chair), and Mr. James Gregson, a Warrington joiner named John Potter IVIS summoned for having been drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cart, at Broom-edge, on the previous evening.—P.C. Sandlonch said he saw defendant stop at tcrm public-houses to get beer, but was refused at each. Derendant was very drunk, and witness took him into custody. Witness lodged the pony and cart at the Fleece Liotel.—Defendant was fined 51. including</p>
        <p>STOVEs.—lron Merchant, GARLAND, Bridge-street. Warrington. Sawrin MACRINFS Hand £2 18s.f3d. Treadle £44s. with ttandson walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON 16 and 18. Sardiey-st.. Warrington.—ildvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of this Board was held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. W. Knowles Presided, and there were also present in addition to the members of the Rural Council, the Rev.J. Simpson,Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. A.R. Norman, T. Lea, S.Yarwood, and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk),Mr.G. Ashton (deputy clerk), and Mr. Cunnah (workhouse master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CONFERENCE ON VAGRANCY.</head>
        <p>THE CONFERENCE ON VAGRANCY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk of the Cheshire County Council forwarded reprints of the conference on the question of vagrancy, and drew special attention to the opinion of those pre- sent the that number of vagrants could only be dimished by the existing orders of the Local Government Board being strictly and universally enforced, and alio expres- sing the strong hope of the Council that the Board would take steps to compel Boards of Guardians to put into operation to the fullest extent the existing or- ders of the Local Government Board as to the treatment of vagrants in the workhouses of the county.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>COUNTY COUNCIL RATES.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL RATES.</p>
        <p>A communication from the Clerk of the County Coun- cil stated that it had been ordered that the following raies be levied for the half year ending March 31st, county rate 2id.in the 2,general police rate a of a penny in the 2, and local police rate of a penny in the 2.—lt was stated by the Clerk that the rates were practically the same as last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NEW APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>NEW APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the committee appointed to consider applications for the offices of schoolmistress and nurse, and for the appointment of a person to assist in cooking and laundry work and generally to assist the matron,it was reported that two applications for the office of nurse and two for the past of general servant had been re- ceived, and none for the office of schoolmistress. The persons for the former were not considered suitable. It was decided to notify the applicants for assistant to attend the Board meeting.—The Master informed the Board that in accordance with the committee's decision he proceeded to Manchester and visted the Northern Workhouse Nursing Association, and he found that a nurse was at liberty and would be able to come at once. She was a duly qualified person,but he had not instructed her to attend that meeting, as he had no authority to do so.—Mr. Lea propesed, and Mr. Tarwood seconded, that the nurse be requested to attend at the next meeting.— Mr. Heywood gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that they subscribe £2 2s. to the Northern Workhouse Nursing Association.—lt was decided to ad- vertise again for a schoolmistress.—Mise Tones, of Frod- sham, was appointed as assistant to the matron.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS AT THE WORKHOUSE. It was unanimously decided that the inmates of the workhouse be provided with the usual Christmas fare on Christmas Day ; and that the men be provided with an ounce each of tobacco, and the children with oranges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>AN ALLEGED BURIAL SCANDAL.</head>
        <p>AN ALLEGED BURIAL SCANDAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cooke presented an account of £3 2s. 6d. for ex- penses paid for the burial of Michael Ford,farm labourer, who was suffocated by accidentally falling into a ditch whilst in a state of intoxication on the 10th Nevemb.r. it was explained that the account was made up of coffin, 13a., bearers, 43., preparation for burial 34. 6J., grave fees £1 73., and hearse 153.—Mr. Wallington stated that he had been approached, to complain that the body was tumbled into the coffin without the clothing being re- moved, and that a rope was passed around the body in order to keep it in the coffin.—Mr. Cooke explained that the undertaker had assured him that the body was treated with all respect. The deceased might have been in- terred at a far cheaper rate at Aston churoh-yard where the service would have been conducted by a Protestant clergyman, but as the man was a Catholic,he was taken to Halton Cemetery where the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, of Run- coln, performed the obsequies.—Mr. Wright spoke to the conscientious and trustworthy character of the undertaker.—A committee consisting of Messrs. Wal- lington, Stokes, Wright, and Heywood was appointed to enquire into the.subject, and report to the next meeting. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A RUNCORN*</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A RUNCORN*</p>
        <p>PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>The magistrates at the Runcorn Police Court on Mon- day morning were engaged for over three hours in de- ciding a case in which Henry John De Korte, of the Holyhead Harbour. High-street,was charged with selling on his licensed premises during prohibited hours ; and also with unlawfully keeping his premises open for the sale of beer during part of the time they were required to be closed,to wit 12.20 p.m.Peter Stephenson and Peter Mangor, of the barque Annette ;were charged with hav- ing on the 15th inst. unlawfully been found on the pre- mises of the Holyhead Flarbour,during prohibited hours. Mr. Churton, solicitor. Chester, prosecuted, and Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warrington appeared on behalf of the defendants.</p>
        <p>In opening the Case, Mr. Churton pointed out that on thesight of the 14th November about a quarter to twelve, a constable was oppeeite the Holyhead Harbour when he noticed two men come out of the house by t is back yard door. They were very drunk, and created a noise, and he had to go and speak to them. He let them go, and continued to watch the house. Soon afterwards he saw the landlord go into the bar, turn up the gas,and work the engine which pumped the beer from the cellar. It was then three-quarters of an hour beyond closing time, and the officer had go el reason to suspect that an improper thing was going en and he informed the Sergeant. He continued to watch for some time,and at length two other men were seen to come out of the house by the yard door. He (Mr. Churton) would ask the magistrates to draw their own inferences from that. It was a very strong feature of the cise. If they were doing nothing wrong, why should the men come out by the back door ? The two men were the defendants Stephenson and Menem., and they were spoken to. The police went hack to the house ;the gas was still burning but at the very moment they knocked at the door, the gas was extinguished. Their worships might draw another inference from that fact. On being admitted, the police sergeant asked defendant what the men were going out of his house at that time for, and he replied that they had been to ship his boarders. The sergeant then said what about those other two men that left at a quarter to twelye,and he replied that they had stooped with the lodgers for a bit of supper. When the officers went into the house there was nothing in the shape of a supper on the table,but there was a quantity of glasses as if drinking had been going on. There was another element in the case. It appeared that last Monday night the constable who saw the first two men come out of the house, met the defendant at a quarter pa.t eleven at night. The landlord stopped him and told him that there were some other b---s that could do it and why should not he ? He should have winked the other eye at his case. There were some people just gone in the New Inn,and if he (De Korte) got done, then others must be done. The constable reported the matter and it would be the subject of an investigation next week. There was also another statement made by the two men when they were served with summonses on the 21st inst. One of them told the constable that if the police had been a bit quicker they would have caught them in the house.</p>
        <p>P.C. Kendrick then gave evidence in support of Mr. Churton's statement. Sergeant Brewster also gave similar evidence. _ .</p>
        <p>For the defence Mr. Browne said that was a case which on the face of it looked peculiar. But it was a case of the most ample explanation as to how the men came on the premises. Mr. De Korte was a man of inde- pendent means, not depending on the sale of a glass of beer or even three glasses of beer, and it seemed absurd that a man in his position would go and endanger his license. The house fronted a main street where the police passed frequently, and it was impossible to believe that h 3 should do what the police said, respecting his supplying beer at the time stated. He had kept the house about three or four months. He had six boarders in the house at the time, and it was_a well known fact that offilers from different ships that came into Runcorn went to this house for the purpose of engaging hands for discharging cargo. It was the first time the two de- fendants had been to the house, and Mr. De Korte had never seen them before. They came to the house about 7.30 on the night in question, but as none of the boarders were in at the time they called again at a quarter to eleven, for the purpose of engaging them. They asked for two bottles of stout, and that was all they were sup- plied with In the house. The boarders were then hav- ing their supper, and the two defendants were told to wait. They did so, and a few mFnutrs to eleven they were toll to go to the kitchen and see the men. At eleven o'clock Mrs. Be Korte locked the bar up, took the key with her to bed, and he would swear that it never went out of her possession. With reeard to the statement that Mr. Be Korte went into the bar to draw beer, that w-s a pure invention, to put it in a mild way. The two defendants were at least half-an- hour in coming to terms with two of the hoarders for unloading a vessel, and during the whole of that time no beer was supplied whatever. They sat talking until after twelve o'clock, when they left, and Mr. De Korte let them out. Those were the only two ?nest valondeft, and he (Mr. Browne) bad never heard a word about the ether two 'men mentioned by the police constable. That was also an invention. Mr. Be Korte bore out Mr. Browne's statement. He denied emphatically that two men had come out previ• ously to the two defendants by the bark door. The men engaged by the defendants went to the work at six o'clock the next morning. He characterised some of the constable's remarks as pure inventions, and denied that hesaid anything about th? New Inn to the constable on the previous Monday. Mrs. De Korte gave evidence as to locking the bar door at eleven o'clock. It was impossible for her husband to go into it. Mr. Hope, Miss Alice Conway, Miss Lilly Richardson, Captain Gronebere, and t',e defendants (Stevenson and Mangor) also gave evidence for the defence, supporting Mr. Browne's statement. The magistrates alter a long deliberation in private, considered there was an element of donbt,and dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>The appointment of Mr. Francis Salisbury, Postmaster of Leeds, to the Postmastership of Liverpool, in succes- sion to :dr. Rich, was officially announced on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>OFFICE-BREAKING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>DETERMINED ATTEMPT.</p>
        <p>During Tuesday night the offices of the Warring- ton Slate Company, Bold-st., of which firm Coun- cillor Henry Roberts is the managing director, were broken into in a particularly daring manner. After an ineffectual attempt at entrance by a window facing Wilson Patten-st. the thieves tried conclu- sions with a larger one looking out on the Arpley yard, and this they were eventually able to open by drawing back the catch, from a small "pay- window' close to. Having succeeded in entering the premises the thieves apparently lost no time in getting to,.work. A small cash drawer in the outer office was forced but nothing much obtained there- from, so the men, apparently three or four in num- ber, entered Mr. Roberts' private office with the object of tackling the safe. This latter had only recently been placed in the position which the marauders found it, and had required the strength of four men to convey it there. It was at this point that the office-breakers most glaringly be- trayed their amateurish inexpertness. Not being satisfied with the manceuvring space afforded in the ' private office the men carried the safe bodily—not rolled it—to thedoorwhere they could work atit com- fortably. Their means of lock-picking, &amp;c., were apparently so limited however that the would-be despoilers went outside and uprooted a large corner stone which had stood at the yard gate for years. This instrument, so hastily improvised, weighed about one hundred weight,and it was conveyed into the office and repeatedly " bashed " on the safe un- til the plates were stove in. Another disappoint- ment now met the robbers, for the receptacle they had expended so much labour in opening, contained nothing but office books and papers. Perseverance seems however to have been a quality largely pos- sessed by these men, for nothing daunted they at- tacked the newly constructed strong room with a heavy crowbar, and after what must have been considerable labour they made a hole of some 18 inches wide but still too narrow for them to enter by. Apparently at this point their operations must have been disturbed and they at once decamped for nothing further was done though the thieves were within a few moment's work of hav- ing complete possession of the strong room. In the latter was a cash box containing about £B. On the arrival of Mr. Roberts, who was summoned from home by telephone at 7.30 on Wednesday morning, and his head clerk Mr. W. Hayes,this money was found to be intact. Some anx- iety WAS displayed before the cash-box was reached, especially as the door of the room had been so damaged that it could not be opened, and the hole made in the wall had to be enlarged for the clerks to effect an entrance. Naturally the offices were in a state of disorder and there were traces of blood almost everywhere making it pretty evident that at least one of the law breakers had cut his hand or been otherwise injured. The oniy article which disappeared from the office was the coat of the cashier. Councillor Roberts estimates the damage as about .£lo,and he has issued a notice offering £5 reward t 3 the person other than one of the thieves, who shall give information that will lead to their apprehension. On the attempt being discovered, the police were likewise communicated with, and the Chief Constable, Mr. Talbot, and several other officers were early on the spot making investigations. Very little in the nature of a clue could be obtained, and it 13 thought probable that even the cashiers' coat would be thrown away atter the robbers had got safely from the neighbour- hood. A crow-bar whizh had been used to break the wall of the strong-room was left behind, but this has been found to have been taken from the Arpley yard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1895.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>APPLETON PAItOCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the committee was held at the London Bridge Inn, Stockton Heath, on Friday evening last week. Mr. H. Eyes occupied the chair, and there were also present—Messrs. C. W. Davenport, Ashton Hayes, Pearson, Coppock, Hedgecock, Hampson, Mr. Ashton (clerk), Mr. Swinton (surveyor) and Mr. Farrington (in- spector of nuisances.) The Clerk said he had received a letter from Mr. Reginald Potts, clerk to the County Council, a4ing the proposed agreement with regard to the sewer had been made, and, if so, to forward the dates cf the com- mencement and the completion of the sewer. He (Mr. Ashton) had replied stating that the Town Clerk of Warrington was preparing the agreement. Ele had also Sent Mr. Potts' letter to the Town Clerk of Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Davenport, in accordance with notice of mo-tion, proposed that so far as regarded the townships of Apple- ton-with-Hull, Latchford Without, Grappenhall and The!wall, the Sanitary Inspector he relieved ot his duties and that they should be given over to Mr. J. Swinton, who should be made the sanitary inspector for those townships with the stipulation that he resides in the dis- trict ; also that Mr. Swinton be inspector under the Dairies and Cowsheds Order for those townships. He (Mr. Davenport) had not taken this step without mature consideration, and the proposition he had made seemed to him to be the best. There were lots of nuisances and they needed attention, and more attention than Mr. Farrington was able to give to them. He wanted it to be understood that he was not wishful of increasing the expenditure nor of decreasing Mr. Farrington's salary, for he thought he did quite enough for what he got. Indeed, there was some truth that he was not getting what he ought. Of course Mr. Swinton's salary would he increased if these additional duties were assigned to him. At present it stood at £l2O, and it would hardly be fair to expect him to perform the extra work without allowing him additional remuneration.- -The Chairman : There is no doubt the district is growinz, and it is necessary for someone to be at hand. —Mr. Pearson seconded the proposal, and Mr. Richard- son spoke in support of it and it was carried unanim- ously. Mr. Pearson referred to the condition of the highway between the church wall and the swing bridge as " dis- graceful." Not only had the sewer burst underneath it, but the road.through not being made properly in the first place,was full of holes. He proposed that the Clerk write to the Manchester Ship Canal Company requesting them to put it in a state of repair. He also suggested that the mud should not be brushed against the side of the road and then left there for people to walk into at nights but that it should be properly removed.—Mr. Daven- port seconded--Mr. Richardson observed that the road wanted raising eight inches.—The motion was carried. The Sanitary Inspector submitted particulars and summary of the cost of the construction of Gaskell-st., sewer in Stockton Heath, giving also the accounts of the apportionments upon the various property owners from the top to the bottom of the street. These were accepted. Mr. Farrington further reported that a property owner had made two connections with the committee's sewer in contravention of the Public Health Act, and which rendered him in each case liable to a penalty of £2o. The Committee decided that proceedings should be taken against the offender if the Clerk was satisfied that the committee had a clear case. The minutes of the above committee were approved at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council on Tues- day. On the proposition of Mr. Davenport, seconded by Mr. Bradford it -WaS decided that the highways and roads committee with Mr. Davenport and Mr. Brad- ford, should consider the question of the transference of the duties of Mr. Farrington to Mr. Swinton and report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>GRAPPENFIALL PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENFIALL PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>An ordinary meeting of this Council was held on Monday evening, in the schoolroom, Grappenhall. Mr. William Long occupied the chair, and there were also present—Messrs. Alan Lister-Kaye, W. Lomas, T. Burrows. R. L. Warburton, F. Ellison, R. Boardman, and J. Woodcock (clerk). The minutes of the previous meeting were read and confirmed, after which a lengthy .discussion took place con- cerning the Bratt Charity. It was finally decided that Mr. Kaye, the Chairman, and the Clerk should go to Frodsham to inspect the books, which were at that place, with reference to it.—The other businesq was of a routine character. TEMPERANCE SUNDAY AT PADGATE. The Wesleyan Conference having set Sunday apart for special appeals for the cause of temper- ance, appropriate sermons were preac:md at the Wesleyan Chapel by Mr. J. Summersgill and Mr. Moss. In the afternoon a special service was held in the schoolroom, when addresses were delivered, Mr. Nightingale being the chief speaker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>-SUCC-ESS OF WARRINGTON LADY.</head>
        <p>-SUCC-ESS OF WARRINGTON LADY.</p>
        <p>Miss S. Agnes Calvert, daughter of the late Mr. Peter Calvert, of Warrington, has obtained her degree of B.Sc. of the University of London. ORGAN RECITAL AT WALTON. In celebrstion of the 2001-11 anniversary of Pur- cell's death, an organ recital was given at Walton Church on Sunday by Mr. Thomas Lane, The pro- gramme inductee the anthem "Rejoice in the Lord," usually knnwn as the "Bell Anthem," and also a "Toccata" for the organ, both compositions of Henry Purcell. There was also an organ sonata by Mendelssohn, march on a theme of Handel by Guilmant and pieces by Wely Tienselt, &amp;c. A melodious " Gantilene," by Jules Orison and the march by Guilmant are deserving of special men- tion, if choice be made out of the well selected programme. Previous to the recital a chapter out of the Bible was read by the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall. There was a large congregation present. The collec- tion was in aid of thechoir fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE REV. W. H. DRUMMOND ON TEMPERANCE REFORM.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the Rev. W. H. Drummond preached an eloquent sermon on the above subject at Cairo-street Church. There was a moderate attendance. After dwelling upon some of the more personal aspects of the question, he pointed out that great social evils required organized movements if they were to be over- come. Reform depended upon perception of the evil, perception of the remedy, and an organization earnest and energetic enough to press both the evil and the remedy upon public attention. The temperance move- seemed to satisfy many of these conditions. There was partial unanimity among earnest-minded men who had studied the question that our present licensing system involved grave dangers to national prosperity and national character, that the facilities for drinking were far too numerous, and that opportunity tended to make drunkards ; that legislation was earnestly called for in order to protect children from the dangers inseparable from the habit of frequenting public-houses. Why then, it might be asked, did they remain so stationary in that matter of temperance reform? Mainly for two reasons. The first was that it had become largely a matter of party politics and dependsd upon party expediency. That meant that the subject was oiten handled not with candour and gravity, but with bitterness and de- sire rather to score off an opponent than to promote the public welfare. Secondly,retormers were at war among themselves as to the precise nature of the remedy, and the,way in which it should be worked. If some zealous men had in the past been more ready to promote ex- periments in licensing reform with which, perchance, they did net not happen to agree, they would by this time be in the possession of much valuable knowledge, and be, nearer a satisfactory solution. There wirre,how- ever, many cheering signs of the growth of a larger un- attimity of feeling and action among our temperance re- formers. The conference recently summoned by the Bis- hop of London was very signitics.nt. The leading clergy of , every denomination, differing as widely as it was possi- ble for man to differ upon theology and politics, agreed upon a programme of reform which was immediately necessary and practicable. When Anglicans, Roman Catholics, and the various sections ef English Noncon- formity said with one voice that. such things as the more the restriction ion of children eo fff escut nu da la yt r teraat dmi ne gn t no f Ehnagbl t nu da them e bpr ri oa tt ee t, and young persons, and the granting of liberty to each community, within lines laid down by Parliament, to choose between various methods for the control of licensed victualling within its area, were ripe for legisla- tion they began to have new hope. The English democ- racy advanced not by leaps and bounds, but by inches. At the present moment they had to help it to advance the next inch in temperance reform, and thus to reduce the intolerable pressure of temptelon, and to convince those who still needed convincing that that was a way in which they might win the possibility of better and richer lives for their people.hlr.Drummond said the temp- tations of the public-Muie and the growth of the drink habit depended partly upon men's surroundings and the conditions under which they lived. In many dark and narrow streets the public-house was the only purveyor , of brightness. Could they wonder that it was well , patronized ? They must take a great deal more interest in the provision of brightness in elevating ways, better dwellings, bright amusements, good music, more play- , , grounds and open spaces. They ought not to be con- tent simply with closing badly conducted houses and diminishing the excessive facilities for drinking ? something better must be.put in their place. There ' would be more chance of having a sober population in their own town when they awoke to the need of spaci- ous and pleasant places of public resort in the parts of the town where workingmen lived, good reading-rooms and social institutes where men could come together to smoke their pipes and discuss their politics and form themselves into brotherhoods for the pursuit of common interests. These things would, of course, cost money, but could there be a finer form of beneficence for many of their rich men than that they should give back a portion of the wealth which the town had helped them to earn in order to promote and develop the best in- terests of their fellow citizens, and provide for them the possibility of a satisfying and ennobling life? In con- clusion, the preacher urged his hearers to bring to the question of temperance reform a spirit of earnestness and self-devotion, and through good and evil report to keep true to their faith in the final overthrow of what- ever debased hucrian nature and sullied the fair name of religion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FORMATION OF A BRANCH OF THE NATIONAL LEAGUE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>FORMATION OF A BRANCH OF THE NATIONAL LEAGUE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Irishmen of Warrington was held in the eld Masonic Rooms,Sanltev-st.,on Sunday afternoon, for the purpose of establishing a local branch of the Na- tional League. There was a very good attendance, and Mr. J. C. Dalton was voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in his opening speech said that the ob- ject of the meeting was to form a branch in Warrington of the Irish National League. It had been felt for some time that it was absolutely necessary that such should I be formed, so that at times of elections the Irish vote should be used then for all that it was worth. The Irish- men needed organisation. It was true that at the present time the Liberal party was fighting their battle, but it was the business of the Irishmen to look after their own affairs, as they were a distinct party and would fight the Liberal party just as they were fighting the Conser- vative party if they did not keep true to their princi- ples. (Applause.) They must see that all Irishmen who were eligible to be on the register,were put on the regis- ter,and he urged his hearers to do their utmost to realise the dream of the Irish race—that of haying a Parliament in Dublin. He said that the vast majority of Ireland was in favour of Home and even the majority of Ulster was in favour of it. There were one cr two things which were essential te bring about success, and one was bro- therly love. Irishmen should love one another,and work together for the rand old cause of Ireland, and let them remember that that day was the anniversary of the deaths of the Manchester martyrs. A branch of the National League formed on such a day could not fail to be successful and to prosper. (Applause.) Dr.O'Kente,of St. Helens,then addressed the meeting. He urged the necessity of unity among Irishmen, and said that he was delighted to be able to come from St. Helens: to help in the formation of a branch of the Irish National League which he was sure would do much to the advantnee of the Irish in Warrington. (Applause.) Mr. KILOANNON then made a vigorous speech in favour of Flonse Rule, and moved the following resolu- tion: " That we, the Nationaliste of Warrington in publicaesembly, hereby pledge our unalterable devotion to the principle of Home Rule for Ireland, as desired by the great majority of the people of Ireland, and further pledgeourselves to use every constitutional means in furthering the same by establishing a branch of the Irish National League in Warrington." (Applause.) Mr. JOHN MULLINS seconded the resolution, and said that he could show some reasons why the establishment of a branch was necessary. The population of the town of Warrington was 56,000, and the voting power was one in seven. The Irish population in Warrington was 9,000, and he found that their voting power was only one in 18, so that the non-Catholic vote was one in five. That was not as it ought to be. (Hear, hear.) They ought at least to have one in nine, and instead of being of a voting strength of 800,they ought to have had 1,000. (Applause.) Continuing. Mr. Mullins said he was not coin:, to bee member of the branch unless it was an independent society. They wanted no tinkering with Liberals or Tories. They would do all they could for the Liberal party so long as the Liberal party supported them. He was delighted that the Liberals had stood shoulder to shoulder with the Irish people so long, and he hoped they would continue to do so, as he did not wish to be antagonistic to them. (Applause.) He had great pleasure in seconding the resolution, and it gave him additional pleasure inasmuch as it was on the anni- versary of the Manchester martyrs. (Applause.) The resolution was passed unanimously, and the fol, I lowing gentlemen were then nominated as membership of the committee : Messrs. Mullins, Kileannnon,Dalton, Lieutenant MeGarty, Malone, D. Walsh, Logan, Lane, 'Kenny, MeDermott, P. Kenny, J. O'Toole, and Noon. Mr. J. C. Dalton was appointed secretary, and Mr. Lane reaeurer. The president has not yet been chosen.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Emergency Committee was held in Manchesteron Wednesday night, when G. Harris, of Tyldesley, was reported for striking a player in the match against Manningham on Saturday last, but in the absence of the referee the case had to be postponed. Later iq the evening the Lancashire section of the Union 'net, and granted registration of player:, as follows : Widnes—G. Aspey,J. Rrostance and J. Donnely; Runcorn —F. Jones. F. Darlington and G. Woads ; St. Helens—J. Goodman •, Rochdale Hornets—F. Green. The team to represent Lancashire in the match avainst Yorkshire at Oldham, on Dec. 7. was elected as follows : Lancashire :—Winstanley (Wigan), back ; Barber (Warrington), S. Lees (Oldham), Chapman (Broughton Rangers), and Hurst (Oldham), three-quarter backs ; Varley and A. Les (Oldham). half backs ; Cnsworth (Wigan), Bonsor (Oldham), Briers (St. Helens), Nevins L (Warringten), Worthington (Tvldeslev). Yates (Wigan), Clemson (Broughton Rangers); and Hill (Rochdale Hor- nets), forwards. Reserves :—Coop (Leigh), back ; Midgley (Rochdale Hornets), and Burton (Warrington), centres ; Sudlow (St. Helens), and Taylor (Oldham), wings ; Molyneux (Leigh), half backs ; Furaess (Oldham), and Smith (Leigh), forwards.</p>
        <p>Try ordinary grated hwseradish for the removal of the cough which fellows the grip. Eat it at meals and be- twePn Isienls.</p>
        <p>Apply cagtor oil nneP a day t warts from two to six weeks and they will disappear</p>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>BUSINESS OF THE NEXT MEETING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council will be held at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday next,to consider the following business.</p>
        <p>TEN HIGHWAYS, PUBLIC WORKS AND SANITARY COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this Committee contain the following matters :</p>
        <p>Mr. Gregory, the deputy inspector, reports :—"I have to report to your Board that we are well forward with our work in the Sanitary Department, and that all is going on well. Since my last monthly repsrt I have paid my usual round of visits to all places likely to re- quire my presence ; I have paid special attention to all the slaughter houses, bake houses,and milk shops within your District ; I have also visited some of our farmers, where milk is supplied to the public, and I intend to visit the otheis early this week. My monthly tolls fof street hawking have been £3 12s. 5d., for market tolls, £23 134. : total. £27 54. sd. During the four weeks we save removed 137 loads of pails. 89 loads from Ashpits, and 187 loads of dry rubbish, total 413 loads. lam very pleased to be able to report that your district is free from all infectious cases."</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. Robert Wilkinson, relating to a shop on the market ground ,and it was proposed by Mr. Emmett, and seconded by Mr. Thompson, that the letter be acknowledged and to lie upon the table. . . _ .</p>
        <p>A plan for the ere"etion of a billiard room at Brookside for Mr. J. Smith was submitted and approved. . . .</p>
        <p>A letter from Mr. T. Smith, asking for the making of the footpath opposite Nos. 63 to 77, Tamwortti-st., was read, and it was resolved that the letter should receive the attention of the Council.</p>
        <p>- A communication was read from Mr. John Partington, as to the letting of the Town Hall, asking for its use upon a Sunday, and it was resolved that the Hall he not let for Suudaye. A petition was read from the inhabitants of Bradlegh. road, asking for the draining and paving of that road, and it was resolved that the communication be acknow- ledged, and the petitioners informed that the matter should receive the attention of the Council.</p>
        <p>- Dr. Watkin's report, calling attention to the state of the piggeries belonging to Messrs. Eachus, Elson, Owen, Sharp, F. Sharp, W. Owen, Connally, Fleming, and Mul- lins, was read, and it was resolved that the parties and else Lord Newton be written to, to abate the nuisance by 24th December, or legal proceedings would be taken against them. A letter from Mr. Emmett, enclosing a letter from Mr. Aveline, London and North-Western Railway Estate Office,relating to the closets and back passages connected with houses In Regent-st.„ Earlestown, was read. Mr. Gregory's special report on the stato of the closets was read, and the Chairman reported the steps taken bv the Council in the matter, and stated that in future the work would be better done. .</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The carbonizing account of the past month as sub- mitted to this committee gave fuel used 448 tons lOcwt., gas made 4,548,000 feet, being an average of /0,135 feet per ton of fuel used. Mr. Swann reported that the illuminating power of the gas had been kept baween 19i and 20 candles, and perfectly pure as regarded ofdinary impurities. He had had the penny-in-the-slot meters altered to the lower price, that is from 25 feet per penny to 28 feet. They had commenced laying the new 4-inch gas main along the Common-road. The cost of laying this,includ- nig pipes and lighting the road also, he estimated at £240 10/. Mr. Swann further reports that along this road there are upwards of 80 houses and 2 shops, and, from inquiries made, he believed we should get the greater number of the residents for gas consumers with the penny-in-the-slot meter system, if the gas was put in entirely free of cost. The reason he mentioned this is because there is a small garden in front of most of these houses, of about 15 feet in length, which is private pro- perty belonging to the owners of the houses, and pipes laid along these gardens, under our present rule, would have to be paid for. A great many people object to pay- ing for pipes laid outside their houses, and I may mention that a good number of Corporations and Gas Companies allow 15 feet of service pipe to all houses which may be situated so far from the footpath, and, in my opinion, it would be judicious to adopt this plan here."—ln view of this report the committee resolved that in future when application for the supply of gas to property standing not more than 15 feet from the para- pet is made, the service pipe be supplied at the cost of the District Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee adopted the recomnsendation of a sub- committee that they advertise for a clerk at the com- mencing salary of £l3O per annum, who must have a thorough knowledge of book-keeping under the Public Health Acts, and be well acquainted with the machinery of Urban District Councils generally, also the require- ments of the Local Government Board, so far as they refer to Councils similar to that. TECUNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTER. This committee reports that letters dated 25th October and sth November, were read from Mr. Bennion,and Mr. Ormand made an explanation relating thereto, and demonstrating that the letters were sent in error.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NOTES BY BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.)</p>
        <p>The first year of office of the District Council moves on apace, and the near approach of the elections, impels one to take a backward glance. I regret however, that although I have taken a good peep into the doings of the Council, I am unable to find that they have performed any notable action for the benefit of the township. As to such things as taking a penny off the gas, I take no account off because In this there is no credit attached to anyone seeing that the penny was put on to meet an emergency some two years ago.</p>
        <p>I have before me the address that one of the Councillors issued to the electors at the time he was seeking election ; and as all the others were drawn out in something like the same strain I take it that the promises therein made have long since been forgotten. At any rate, I don't know that any single one of the promises have been fulfilled. For the benefit of those who did not think it worth while to keep a copy of the literature they re- ceived, I will just briefly enumerate some of the promises.</p>
        <p>Here is one: "I will do all in my power to legally obtain possession of the waste land known as ' Newton Common.' and use the same to the best advantage." Now, if anything whatever has been done in the Council by any individual member of it towards carrying out this promise, then I must have indulged in a long sleep, because I certainly have never heard of it. And so far as Newton Common is concerned it is a " common " still, although it is gradually becoming a private pre- serve.</p>
        <p>-- The next is, "The appearance of most of our streets is disgraceful and I would urge the necessity of a speedy settlement of the question of street- making." Mark the "speedy." A snail is a fiery untamed steed in comparison with the Council on the question of speed. At the time this address was written, the streets were admittedly disgrace- ful, and that is the condition they are in at the present time, in fact Bunyan's "Slough of Des- pond," would be a paradise in comparison with some of our streets. In the main streets too things are not much better. Certainly they have been patched here and there; but the macadam stones have not felt the weight of the steam roller, and consequently a man makes a regular circuit of the patches in order to put the loose stones in position. So much for street-making.</p>
        <p>Now then comes the Dean Trust Charity,:—" I am in favour of an investigation into the Dean Trust Charity management, and, if possible, trans- ferring the same to the control of the District Council." I venture to think this question has not been so much as breathed on the Council, and so far as taking over this or any other charity is cancerned, the matter has been a dead letter. Of course there is no hurry, but there is a promise. There are other matters talked of, but I think the foregoing will be sufficient to show the fallacy of putting any faith in promises of candidates for District Council honours.</p>
        <p>Before closing I should like to call attention to a matter which has been before the Council,and that is the provision of a mortuary. Last week the body of a man was found in the canal,and for want of a proper receptacle, it had to be placed in an old shed behind the lock-keeper's house. Publicans have lately become wide awake and on the plea of having no accommodation refuse to make provision for the reception of the body. Of course so long as the police continue to find some sort of accommo- dation, I don't expect anything will be done by the Council, but if the police would only take the next body which comes into their hands to await the Coroner, and carry it to the house of the church- warden in whose parish it is found, I feel confident that there would be such a rumpus that a mortuary would be provided without any further delay.</p>
        <p>An engine-driver and fireman were on Wednesday killed 9n the London and North-Western Railway between Rugby and Leamington owing to a coal engine leaving the metals and rolling forty feet down an embankment. A third man was badly injured.</p>
        <p>Put a few drops of turpentine in the water when clothes are put to soak ; it whitens them.</p>
        <p>THE DROWNING OF RUNCORNIANS AT SEA.. ANOTHER BODY FOUND. On Sunday the remains of the youth John Inglesfield, who resided in Salisbury-st., Runcorn, and who was 'drowned off the Welsh coast in a gale, and whose body was found at Bisphatn, were interred in the Runcorn Cemetery, on Saturday afternoon. The cortege started from his mother's residence. In advance walked the Rev.J. Stafford, Mr. William Shaw,Captaiu Waterworth, Mr. J. Hoxworth, Mr. John Hoxworth, Mr. Joseph "lox- worth, Mr. A.Riley (representing Messrs. Speakman and Sons). These were followed by a number of the mem- bers of the Star of Freedom Lodge of the Grand United Order of Oddiellows, and the following brethren of the society acted as bearers :—Messrs. W. Jones, T. Millington, 0. Hughes,J. Caliwell,T. Mather, F. Corker, A. Marsh and D. Wheeler. After the coffin came the mourners. The funeral ceremony was conducted by the Rev. J. Stafford, who delivered a short address. On the coffin were several beautiful floral emblems, contributed by his mother, sisters and brothers, Mary, Joseph and William, his sisters, Jane, Margaret, Sarah and Emeline, Charles and John, cousins • Mr. and Mrs. Hoxworth, Messrs. John and Joseph lioxworth,Mrs. Hoxworth and children (Holloway), and Mr. William Basnett. FINDING OF CAPTAIN THOMAS HOX WORTH. _</p>
        <p>The body of Captain Thomas Hozworth was picked up near the Point of Ayr on Sunday. Mr. William lox- worth, Lowlands-road, received a communication on Monday and proceeded to Mostyn to view the body,and identified it as that of his brother,Captain Thomas lox- worth, of the Holloway, Runcorn. An inquest has been held and a verdict of " Found Drowned" returned. The whole of the crew of the flat "William Henry "has now been found. The funeral took place on Thursday after- noon in Runcorn Cemetery. Amongst those present in addition to the relatives were Mr. William Shaw (mis- sionary), Captain Thomas Burrows,and Mr. A. Riley (on behalf of Messrs. P. Speakman and Sons), Captains F. Williamson, Waterworth,Millinger, and G. Shaw,Messrs. Atherton (Widnes), Williams, William Cooper, James Shaw, R. Burrows, and Wilkinson. The Rev. A. J. Jameson was the officiating clergyman. Wreaths had been contributed by Mrs. Inglesfield, Mr. and Mrs.Orme, nephews and nieces, Mr. and Mrs. Bushell, Mrs. Snape, Mrs. James Shaw and family, Mr. and Mrs. Joynson, " Rohert and Sarah Alice," brother and sister W. and E. Hoaworth). Aothing has yet been heard of the body of Mr. Lloyd, the engineer of the Renfrew.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>A small two days' sale of work, the proceeds of which are in aid of the St. John's Presbyterian Church and school building fund, was opened on Wednesday after- noon in the schoolroom, Victoria-road, Runcorn. The room had been nicely arranged for the occasion. There were four stalls containing a selection of useful and orna- mental articles. They were in charge at the following ladies :—No. 1 stall, Miss Pritchard, Mrs, P. Booth and Mrs. Richardson ; stall 2. Mrs. James Branch and Miss J. Dutton ; stall 3, Mrs Jones and Mrs. Carroll ; stall 4, Mrs. Robinson, Mrs. Temperley, and Mrs. Warburton. In addition there were a number of assistants. A well stocked refreshment stall was in charge of Mrs. Thomas Kirkham, Miss D. Jones,Miss Dutton, Mrs. Thomas,Mrs. S. Hughes, Mr. and Mrs. Jesse Leach, and Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Booth. A museum of curiosities,looked after by Mr. Harrop and Mr. Thomas Kirkham, proved an at- traction. An entertainment committee consisting of Miss Edwards, Miss Hayes, Mr. Harrop,Mr. Warburton, and Mr. Matthew rhomas, provided several entertain- ments for the evenings. Mr. J. C. Humphreys, of Nottingham, formerly an officer of the church, performed the opening ceremony, and there were also present in addition to the stall- holders mentioned, Messrs. James Pritchard, William- son, Oliver, Burn, Harrop, Hughes, Warburton, and Richardson.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pritchard in introducing Mr. Humphreys said that gentleman was a very old friend of theirs and had been identified with the cause for many years. They were very pleased to see him amongst them once more. ' (Applause.) Mr. Humphreys, who met with a cordial reception, said that, in the first place, he wanted to thank the com- mittee for giving him an opportunity of taking an official part in connection with that good and glorious work is which they were engaged. He had watched the work in which they were engaged with very much pleasure. It had been a hard and laborious task, he knew, but had they followed his advice they would have had a chapel many years ago, and very likely it would have been paid for, and in all probability they would have been prepared te have started another work. Perhaps he ought not to have mentioned that but he only did it in order that they might realise how jealous he was at not being allowed to be more closely associated with them in connection with ' the effort they were putting forth. No doubt from time to time they would be downcast, but he was sure they would meet with every encouragement. They would meet, too, with disappointments, but he urged them to stand shoulder to shoulder, and not to be cast down, if they did not always meet with the success they anticipated. By and by they would be rewarded,and in time they would be able to put up a chapel in front of the schools. He concluded by wishing every success to St. John's. (Applause.) A vote of thanks to Mr. Humphreys woe proposed by Mr. J. E. Warburton.—Mr. J. Richardson seconded the vote of thanks, and Mr. Pritchard supported it.—The proposition was carried with enthusiasm.—Mr. Hum- ' phreys having acknowledged the vote, the sale then commenced. The amount realised during Wednesday was about £35. The sale was again opened on Thursday when there was a larger attendance than on the previous The total proceeds of the sale have exceeded £55.:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>HAL-TON.</head>
        <p>HAL-TON.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN BAND OF Horm.—On Thursday night a mis- cellaneous entertainment was given in the schoolroom to a large and appreciative audience. The chair was occupied by Mr. Walter Rowe. Miss Boys acted as ac- companist. The following programme was gone through: Song, "Stand like the brave," Band of Hope choir; re- citation, " Sing them to me," Majoris Faulkner ; solo, " Nobody's darling." "Mary Goulden ; recitation, " How a little one can tell," Edith Rowe; dialogue, "Be true," Fanny Looker and George Tomlinson ; duet, " Star of Twilight," Miss Boys and S. J. Molvneux ; recitation, " The little girl and her doll," Alice M.Ashley; dialogue," Miss Spriggins' work," A. HiEginson, M. Tomlinson, and M. A. Hughes; solo, "A light in the window," Clara Looker; recitation, " Come let us have a drink," H. Higginson ; so.g. "My happy home," choir ; recitation, "'Bessie's secret," Alice Faulkner ; recitation," es it nothing to you," Lizzie Ryalls ; song, " Our hopes are bright," choir; recitation, "my father's at the helm," T. Looker ; trio, " The social glass," C. Looker, A. Higginson, and M. A. Hughes; dialogue, "The trial of intoxicating liquors," Mr. W. Rowe and others. A good collection was taken. THE WESLEY GUILD. —On Monday evening, the fol- lowing persons took part in a discussion on the subject of " The prohibition of the sale of intoxicating liquors": Mrs. Boys, Messrs. J. Looker, S. :Barrow, W. Moors, J. Clarke, J. Saunders, and W. and A. Rowe. There were present 29 members, seven companions, and a number of other friends. The Rev. C, P. de Gliddon presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RUNCORis-T TRADE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORis-T TRADE.</p>
        <p>The commercial prospects of Runcorn for the coming winter are certainly far from being encouraging. The work at Runcorn docks during the past week or two,has been dull, the number of arrivals being comparatively small. With regard to the prospected new works of the Castner-Kellner Company at Weston, no definite intima- tion has yet been received. Another discouraging out- look. is the temporary closing of the Runcorn works of the Bridgewater Canal Undertaking. During this week werk has been at a standstill at the Sprinch works. The order which has been received from headquarters it goes specifies that the various departments shall cease opera- tions every alternate week during the present year. Even this limited period means a three weeks'stoppage,which, according to existing conditions, is sufficiently serious for the town. It is roughly calculated that the reduc- tion of hours at these works will affect fully 500 persons, so that the commercial depression will be considerably augumented. The general desire is that the works will resume full operations at the opening of the new year. Arrangements have been made for no interference with the shipping traffic of the Canal so that it will be con- ' ducted as usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>FRODILAM.</head>
        <p>FRODILAM.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A DOORMAT.-At Frodsham Police Court on Monday before Messrs. Reynolds and Thomas, a private in Her Majesty's Ist Border Regiment, named James Caldwell was charged with stealing a doormat value 2s. from the doorway of the Albert Inn, the property of John Rostock Forster, on Saturday nigbt.—A fine of 10s. including costs was imposed. BROUGHT BACK FROM IRELAND.-At Frodsham Police Court on Saturday, Michael Joyce was charged under a warrant for not appearing to a summPls for being on licensed premises during prohibited hours on Sunday, September 22. The evidence showed that he was with others, at the Volunteer Inn, Frodsham, kept by Mith- redath Vickers, on the date named. The prisoner failed to appear in answer to the summons, and absconded. He had been trought back from Ireland.—A fine of is. and £4 Bs. costs was imposed. FRODSHAM UNION CHURCH.-A sale of work in aid of the above church was held in the schoolroom OR Thurs- day last. It included stalls for fancy-work, refresh- ments, dairy produce, flowers, Szc., presided over by ladies of the church and congregation and other friends. After the usual preliminaries the sale was opened by Mr. Thomas Rigby, J.P., of The Cedar's, Sutton Weaver,and Mrs. Jackson, of South Bank, Frodsham, who addressed the assembled friends with remarks eminently suitable to the occasion. Notwithstanding the exterior gloom which prevailed, considerable activity was displayed within. Entertainments were provided throughout the afternoon and evening and were well patronized, The amount realized was upwards of £5O,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LIBERATOR TRIAL.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERATOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>In the Balfour trial on Monday Mr. Octavius Gregg from Devonshire, whose name had been made use of in connection with the Whitehall Court property transac- tions, gave evidence that certain bills of exchange put' porting to bear his signature were not signed by him Two of the signatures, he said, looked like his, but if he did sign the bills he never knew what they were. On Tuesday Mr. O'Connor, before proceeding to calf witnesses, asked that his client might be allowed te make a statement. Mr. Justice Bruce assented, and Balfour tnen read a long statement. He said he refused offers by Mr. Carr of a large commission if he would procure from the Liberator Society, a loan to complete the Whitehall Court property. He agreed, however, to mention the matter to Hobbs and Wright, who took an active part in the management of Hobbs and Co., and he told Hobbs that he had refused Cart's offer, and that if he arranged the matter he ought to receive a aornrnis- sien from Hobbs and Co. To this Hobbs agreed. An arrangement was come to for sale to Hobbs and Co. for £57,000, and Wright informed him that the Board of that Company were so elated at the bargain that thay agreed to put aside £20,000 for commission and ex- penses, and that his (Balfour's) commission would be paid out of that silm. He afterwards learned that it had been agreed to give him a commission of £7,500, and that the larger part of it would be payable by bills spread over a long time. He had never heard of Gregg• nor did he know of the bills having been drawn by' Gregg or in his name, and the books and letters showed that in carrying out the agreement for the compani.e,S interested he acted with scrupulous honesty and rig"' ance. Mr. Carr, contractor, was called as a witness. and he generally corroborated the defendant's state- ment. Mr. O'Connor in his address to the jury said that Balfour was fully entitled to the commission, as he had rendered most valuable services, and that the money be rcceived was not the money of the House and Lands In- vestment Trust, but the money of Hobbs and Co. The trial of Jabez Balfour on a charge of impreperly sharing with Henry Granville Wright, now in prisofl. in a commission of £20,000 in reference to the sale of en interest in the Whitehall Court estate, was concluded on Wednesday. The jury found the defendant guilty on all the counts of the indictment. Mr. Justice Bruce said be would consider what his sentence would be. On Thursday morning tho three convicts were brought before Mr. Justice Bruce in the Queen's Bench Court of the Royal Court of Jostle° to receive sentence. At the outset of the proceedings it was intimated by the Attor- ney General that no further action would be taken against Dibley , and that the remaining charges agaius the others would be abandoned. Counsel for Balfour, Brock, and Theobald having addressed the Judge in mitigation of punishment, his Lordship proceeded te pass sentence. Dealing first with Balfour, he told him that he had, by an extensive series of frauds in which he was the chief actor, obtained many thousands—na9 millions—of pounds from the public, and that in a: great commercial community like ours justice demand an exemplary sentence. It must be made plain that those who were guilty of ingenious criminality must bear the same penalty as the common criminal. He had n? doubt that the greater part of dalfour'e punishment during his imprisonment would be the feeling of remorsl springing from the remembrance that he had ruined thousands of humble homes. No prison walls and In) prison doors could shut out from his ears the wail Pf the widow and the orphan. The sentence he had to Pess,, was seven years' penal servitude in respect of the Lam,? Allotment Company, and seven years' penal servitude for the House and Lands Company frauds—in all feu" teen years' penal servitude. His Lordship sentences Brock and Theobald to nine and four months' imprison' ment respectively.</p>
